      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>-- LADIES' LETTER.</head>
        <p>-- LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>MT DEAR NEILLII2,, I need hardly rornind you that evening gowns will soon claim our attention. A lady has just shown me one. The bodice shows no seams, as they are under the strapping, and fastens by six little straps and buttons, and by hooks under the broad trimming which starts from the shoulder, and continues over the back. The basque is quite short, and smoothly fitting, the back breadth cut Into a little spade shape. A diversity of dainty little collars and cravates cs.n be worn with this style of bodice, which, it must) be remembered, is a good deal more economical ha Llutls, for this needs no blouse,</p>
        <p>Many of tne toques have fur brims and om• broideted cloth crowns. White or ivory cloth embroidered in turquoise and cold is favnurite, sable or chinchilla brim setting lb cir beautifully, with poppies, roses, or violets at the left gide. The most fashionable shape for hats now is kind of modified scoop. They are usually black felt; lined with colour. For instance, one I saw was in black felt, with a band of black velvet round the crown, and a black plume lying at the extreme edge, curling round the left side and back. The brim was lined with plf-ated velvet in palest green, and a pale green plume at the edge underneath, mingled with the softness of the black feather. These two feathers and the band round the crown are the only permissible trimming for these hats. Other hats, I am afraid, are of the old order, viz., ribbons, feathers cr fuetails.</p>
        <p>I should like you to remember stint in selecting a. coat the smartness of the black caracul is con- siderably enhanced by the addition of the white ermine on the collar and revere. Capes and caracul arc trimmed as the coat, with ermine on the revers and collar, which, with an ermine muff to match, would make an ideal winter costume. There are other capes galore, including some specially lovely ones in mink or sable. One very smartly made cape of the latter fur is a model of elegance and comfort with a high storm collar and large lapels which turned back at the neck and continued in flounce form all. round the rich-looking cape. In mink they are equally comfortable and look exceedingly well; besides, they are not quite so expensive. As to the popular sealskin, it is answerable for many lovely capes and coats. Of course I need not remind you that there are excellent imitations of the above furs, so that any of your friends who cannot go to such a long price may yet obtain a fur coat at a reasonable price. d</p>
        <p>Everybody seems agreed that the smartest tie in evidence now are of fringed crepe de chine. Th prettiest way to wc.2.r them is in a saikr•kno after passing them round the neck, crossing th ends at the back and bringing them to the front One of these ties in turquoise W3l introduced wit) a happy effect on a brown tailor•mado gown I saN last week. It was just the right touch of colour The vogue for scarf hat trimmings is on the wane It was always somewhat suegestive of the antiqu female, so its exit from the stage of fashion ma. take place without a sigh. Nothing is more diseouraginq•to the busy house wife than to have constant pain'and discomfort ii her feet. Often the trouble is aggravated by im proper foot-wear. No one should buy shoes the are uncomfortable when new. Of all the pain that women bear, the most useless and foolish i pain in the feet. For tired feet, a hot footbath with an ounce of salt in it, is restful. Put oi fresh stockings and a change of shoes after takini it. The quickes, relief from fatigue is to plung the foot in ice cold water, and ket,p it immerse. there for a moment, or until there is a sensatior of warmth.</p>
        <p>Your loving JENNIIL</p>
        <p>DAMP BEDS.—If there is any fear that a bed no usually slept in is damp, put a bright looking-glas between the sheets and cover it up. In a fey minutes examine it. If its surface is dimmed thee is cause for uneasiness. If a bright looking-glas is not at hand, a tumbler warmed and turned up side down will answer the same purpose.</p>
        <p>CARTER'S LITTLE LIVER PILLS. rill. Sr DO3O. 'Sm. a Price. in a.</p>
