        <p>Comedy "will recognise the beauty. Whea I shell be in presence of my Lord _ Full often will 1 praise thee unto Him." _</p>
        <p>People have begun to think seriously about Christmas shopping and the serious business of selecting some " really useful presents " for one's friends is agitating dcntestic circles. Many are already preparing and des- patching gifts for friends and relations in distant parts of the world, while in some instances the parcels have to 1, ave earlier so as to arrive in time for the festive seAson. One item invariably ccnsists of a substantial plum pudding, reminding those abroad of one of the national institutions of the old country, aLd it secrts that the Queen has taken care that all the troops in South Africa are to have their Christmas pudding. In addition, her Majesty is making a personal present of a box of chocolate to all her soldiers at the Cape. One hundred thousand packets are being prepared,put up in souvenir boxes, and the gift will certainly to highly apprecizted by "Tommy Atkins." No matter where he is, or what serious work the British soldier has to face, he always keeps Christmas to the best of his ability and surroundings, and we cannot wish him anything better than that circumstances may permit him to enjoy hi 3 Christmas rare.</p>
        <p>Several learned authorities took part in a public dis- cussion on women's dress the nth( r day at Berlin. Pro- lessor Rubner found a new reason for ccndemning long skirts, as, not only were they the frrquent cause of accidents, but they were also conducive to neuralgic pains broueht on by constantly holding up the dress. A leading artist also E pike sgainst the long skirt, and denied its gracefulness in the ballroom or in any quick movement. Madame Seler took the same view,and gave a still better reason for Avocating short skirts. They made the wearer, she said, look younger, and declared that it was about time vi omen ceased to be slaves to fashion, and their garments. In the end the meeting declared for greater freedom, and re,.olved that long walking dresses are irreconciliable v.ifh the modern re- quirements of hygiene, liberty of move ment,and beauty. Curled quills are no longer correct. Faehionablo milliners are utilising hand painted specimens, or those entirely covered with tiny feathers culled from the plumage of some gaily colouroc3 bird. The best bats and toques are now built on the the purchaser's head. The shape is selected and prepared, and the trimmings pinned in position. Then comes the "trying on" prccess alterations if necessary, and the final " finish off."</p>
        <p>" MADGE."</p>
