      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>CATARRH v. CONSUMPTION.</head>
        <p>CATARRH v. CONSUMPTION.</p>
        <p>This heading would imply that catarrh and consump- tion are opposed to each other ; we know they are not, in fact, the latter more often follows the former than not. We wish to impress on the people generally, and on the heads of families particularly,that nearly all cases of consumption, if not hereditary, can easily De traced to neglecting a slight, common, every-day cisld. It is the worse thing a person can possibly do, as the oftener you take cold the more you wear and strain the di floater membrane of the lungs by coughing. The medicine to take is Lavans Lung Unctus, as in removes at once tha` nasty tickling awl irritation which generally attends a cold, and eff-ctrially wards off those demons asthma and bronchitis, and as we mentioned bqore, those fearful destroyers, pneumonia and consumption. All chemis's and co-operative stores sell it, and the proprietors, Green and C Bolton. will send a bottle post free for is. 2s. 9d., or 4s. 6i.</p>
        <p>Warrington Reid Club.</p>
        <p>At the fourth meeting of the present session Mr. William Webster read a paper entitled " A fort- night's holiday in Hampshire." In it he happily combined pleasantly interesting reminiscences of of his observations of the hi-tory and scenery as well as of the zoology of Titchfield and its neigh- bourhood and some parts of the New Forest and the Isle of Wight. He had been very successful amongst the spiders,having secured representatives of nine out of the 15 British families, and of 40 of their species, belonging to 18 genera. His notes on the habits of some of the species were especially useful. His description of the dodginess of Xysticus Innio, one of the crabspiders, causing much amuse- ment, whilst his rufereaces to the shamming death of Araneus arbusterum, a spider which frequents loebushes and which when alarmed lays its legs close to the hDdy and looks like an unripe sloe- berry, elicited considerable di4cu,.sion, on account of the somewhat crude theory which appeared to have coloured the observation and record of a by no means unusual, though commonly misunderstood, occurrence. There was a good attendance, and Mr Webster was deservedly congratulated on his paper</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>"WOMEN'S CHAT,"</head>
        <p>"WOMEN'S CHAT,"</p>
        <p>The Emprese of Germany was, in her early married I days' credited with being one of the worst dressed Royal women in Europe, and for many years it ceuld not be disputed that there was much truth in this unflattering statement. But her recent vitiit to this country shows that a different state of affairs obtains now. Her Majesty's toilettes both fer day and evening were faultless, and in the very best of taste. At all Court fetes the Kaiserin is covered with jewels. These are, mostly. the property of the Crown, and worn only by the reigning Queen of Pruesia. The Empress however, possesies a very valuable collection of jewel% of her own, parZ, being the gift of the Emperor, the re- mainder being left her by the Empress Augusta, with whom she was a great favourite. The death of Lady Salisbury is not only a sad blow to the Prime Minister and his family, but it is also a national loss, seeing how closely that family is devoted to the interests of the country. The event was not altogether unexpected. Lady Salisbury Mid been ailing for two or three years, and when she was taken seriously ill at Walmer Castle, last summer, it was known to her- salt and family that she could crity look forward to a brief spell of life. The Marchioneas was in her 73th year, acme three years older than Lord Salisbury. She married the then Lord Robert Cecil in July 1857, and at that time her husband, as a younger son, bad little prospect of bec, ming. A Mat qui s. For the n( xt seven or eight years, Lerd Robert added to the family resources by writing for some of the principal London journals, a work in which he frequently had the assist- ance of his wife. On the death of his eldest brother, Lord Robert became Lord Cranborne, and in 1868 he succeeded his father—his wife then becoming the Marchioness cif Salisbury. The family has alweys been singularly united, simple, end happy in its home life. In fact Lord Sali-bury's resources, by way of recreetien have always been in his hemp, alld much public sym- pathy will be felt for him in the grievous loss he has sustained. • Among our leading Statesmen, past and present, there are many who have been bleFsed with devoted wives, and whose home life has closely resembled that of the Marquis of Salisbury. Take for example Mr. Gladstone. He himself failed to find words to exprsos the debt he owed his wife in relation to all the offices that she had discharged during their long and happy union. Then there is the beautiful though pathetic married life of the Premier Earl of Aberdeen,ancl the devotion of Disraeli and his wife to each other is well-known. Viscountess Beaconsfield was sot beautiful, she was about fifteen years older too than her husband, and the made gauche remarks about him. but she devoted herself to him in the spirit of a heroine, and became the con- fidante and adviser of toe Conservative leader. Be dedicated "Sybil " to her, and as Froude says, the mo- ment in his whole life which perhaps gave him the greatest delight, was that in which he was able te decor- ate his wife with a peen:tee. The Poet Laureate has ventured to make a small con- tribution to the reams which have been printed on the question " who was Beatrice ?" Mr. Austin is far from doubting that Beatrice had a real existence, and in sup- port of his view,he quotes the pareaee in the 30th canto of the Purgatorio, wherein the spirit of Beatrice is re- presented as thus addressing Dante. .‘ Observe me well. I am. in Booth. I am Beatrice, Whit ! and hest thou deigned at last, Approach the mountain ? Knowest not. 0 man, Thy h tppiness is here ? There is another passage in the 2nd •;lento of the In- ferno, which Mr. Austin ini:ht also have quoted. It is that in which Beatrice, having fears for the safety of Dante, who was wandering on ti;e outskirts of the dolorous shades, is stated to have had recourse to Yirgil, and begged him to succour " a friend of mine, and not a friend of fortune." " Beatrice, am I, who do bid thee go I come from there. where I would fain return, Love moved me. which compelled me to speak." _</p>
        <p>Then follows the pronaist which is not pertinent to this discussion, but of -a hich readecs of tho " Divine</p>
      </div>
