      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>NOBLER THAN REVFSGE.</head>
        <p>NOBLER THAN REVFSGE.</p>
        <p>l BY ESME STUART, Author of "In the Dark," "Inscrutable, "A Woman of Forty," "Sara's Mother," /cc.</p>
        <p>SYNOPSIS OF PRECEDING CHAPTERS: On his way to mt his cousin, Sir Harry Beauworth, Ales Beauworth, heir to the title, is set upon by two men and knocked senseless. Sir Harry Beauworth has a bad reputation. Bob Rabbitts, son of the evil- looking landlord of the Dog and Duck, revives Alec, and determines, as he has the chance, to enter Sir Harry's service for a time to see that Alec comes to no harm. Sir Harry has one daughter. Ruby, a beautiful and unsophisticated girl, who has never been allowed to see the world, and whom her father intends to marry to an Australian, James Felton, who with his father, Tom Felton, appears to have a powerful influence over him. These Feltons are the men who tried to kill Alec, though he never saw their faces, and it is obvious that they acted at the instigation of the baronet, who has some secret reason for wishing to be rid of the heir. Alec, when he sees Ruby, falls in love with her, and not knowing his danger determines, if possible, to stay and win her.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>CHAPTER VII.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER VII.</p>
        <p>Sir Harry came down the passage, followed by his two Australians. Alec looked again at the younger man, and again he felt a strange feeling of aversion for him. He must have a talk with Sir Harry, and ask if Ruby might at least wait and choose for herself. But, then, what right had he to ask this? Could he in some way make a will in her favour, but on this condition? The squire informed Alec that the boy was ready and at his service, and Alec at once propounded the idea that Ruby should come with Nancy to shew him the way home. Sir Harry made no objections, he even seemed in a hurry to grant the request. "Come, James, won't you go too ? " said Mr. Felton to his son.</p>
        <p>"No, thank you, I shall ride further afield. My pretty cousin must excuse me to-day. We shall have time after Mr. Beauworth has gone to sauntex through the woods." Alec felt as if he could knock the fellow down, and yet he was thankful to him for not being jealous, and for letting another man walk with the pretty girl who was to be his wife. "He does not care in the least for her," thought Alec. "This must be some family arrangement, and the girl will be sacrificed. But how can this be when certainly her father is devoted to her ? " As the men were going out, Ruby caught hold of her father's arm.</p>
        <p>"Papa, I have found some old letters; may I read them ? "</p>
        <p>"Pray do; they can perhaps tell you about the ancient Beanworths. I never cared to rummage for old information ; but it may interest you, Ruby." Shortly afterwards Alec, sunk in deep meditation, was following Bob Rabbitts through lonely fields and copses. What would be the upshot of all this? Sir Harry certainly did not look very happy this morn- ing, but he must have sonic good motive for letting him see so much of Ruby. If it was to get him tc make a will in her favour, it meant also making her a greater catch for James Felton. Now, this did not at all smile upon him, and he determined to be on his guard. Ruby should not be married against her will, but would she resist? Was she not quite devoted to her father, and would she not obey him without a murmur? Besides, what did she know of life or marriage? Evidently nothing. She had been purposely kept ignorant. This ignorance was partly her great charm. But why should he not try to win her? The idea was fascinating, and if she thus acquired the property, how could her father complain? Alec felt that he was very deep and cunning; but he must ge carefully to work so as not to arouse the suspicion of the two Feltons. He had hardly spoken a word to Bob till the boy suddenly stopped short, and touched his arm. "You said we was friends, sir, didn't you?" "Assuredly, Bob. You think lam not courteous to my friend; but, to say the truth, I was deep in thought." "I don't know what you means to do; but] should like to just tell yieu what you ought to do, Mr. Alec." "Do you know that you are a queer boy? lam stilr under promise of secrecy, I suppose ? " "Yes; it will spoil everything if you blab; but I've been turning it round and round, and I thin]] that you had best--" "Why, how do you kdow anything about my thoughts ? " asked Alec, laughing. "Don't know your thoughts, but it's the thoughts of t'others I guess." "You mean your kind friends who nearly did for me yesterday ? " "I didn't say as they was my friends, Mr. Alec. but I want you to-- "Bring them to justice? I shall be delighted." "T'ain't that, sir, I don't suppose you could but I want you to go away—right away. "Go away, Bob, my friend! Is that a very friendly act? Besides, I am only on a week's visit, and the time will soon fly." "No, I want you to go away to-day. In fact, t'ain't any good beating about the bush, as mothes says; I want to take you on the road that will be the shortest for you." Alec stopped short, and laughed heartily. "You want me to go now, and at once, and see me off the premises! What, leave Yewdale without a word to my host, and for no reason! I am sorry, Bob, to disoblige you, but I couldn't. Besides, as we are friends, I don't mind owning I am mud interested in what I see. I like Miss Ruby, as yott said I should, and I vant to see as much as I can of her during the week." Bob was silent, then he shook his head. "I wants you to go this morning, and to say that— Oh, gents can tell big lies quite easy." Alec paused. What could the boy mean? "You think, Bob, that my friends of yesterday, or yours, will want to keep my mouth quiet about their little mistake, and that I mat, have another encounter with them? " Bob nodded. "Yes, that's it. "But don't you see Tam forewarned now; I could not possibly be taken unawares—besides, I could not appear so rude to the squire. It could not be. Then what would Miss Ruby think of me? She is even coming to meet me presently, and I should indeed lose all her good opinion. A poor sort of cousin! Besides, Bob, you are a sensible chap, and I'll tell you I rather fancy that I can be of use to this motherless girl. If so, no idea of personal danger would move me." "Miss Ruby's safe enough, Mr. Alec. They don't want nothing of her." "Safe! I do not doubt that, but a girl—a beautiful girl—my find herself in a situation when the advice of a friend—not exactly her own father —may be of great use to her." "You can't help her," said Bob, scornfully. "He'd not smash her head." "Who is he? Do you mean her new cousin, Bob ? " Alec was much amused at Bob's schemes, but he did not forget what he owed to the lad who had done so much for him on the previous day, and who had taken such an unaccountable fancy to him. "You'd better go, Mr. Alec; I can't prevent things as I can't tell you about." "Look here, Bob, I have a great regard for you, but I really can't run away from such an imaginary danger as you suggest! You are a lad of spirit; you can see that because a curious misadventure happened to me on my way here, it is no reason why I should go away from a place which I have long wished to see, especially just as I have made the acquaintance of a charming cousin, who may want my help in a very difficult period of her life. It would, indeed, be ridiculous if I were to turn tail and run away without any good excuse." "You won't, you won't," said Bob, shaking his head. "Mother says as there are folks as won't see snakes under their feet 'cause they're looking at the stars, and it tain't the stars as'll do you any good here. But you'll not tell the squire, Mr. Alec, what I've said, or he'd pack me off pretty sharp, and what would you do then ? " Alec laughed heartily at Bob's opinion of him- self.</p>
        <p>"I should certainly lose a very entertaining friend, Bob, so make your mind easy. I shall be mum on all our conversations. Besides, I think the squire would think you demented. He might even include me in the same category." "Don't know what that is, Mr. Alec; but I'll __</p>
        <p>do the best I can for you. I'm naught but a boy, , and t'otl_3rs is men. The devil is awful cunning, and you has to learn his ways afore you're fit to , give he a beating." "Anyhow, we are friends, Bob, so trust me to COUIICOB. I don't want to hurt yeur.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, NOVEMBER 24, 1E99.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, NOVEMBER 24, 1E99.</p>
        <p>pr..sprrt.; aria it pH' stay with the squire, it will be the Erg. step in your ladder. sow, take mc to_; the birch woods. 'These are all pastures new fOi "</p>
        <p>Alec spent a pleasant morning, but what made it seem so bright was the ide that Ruby would come to him. It was ridiculous to take so much interest in a girl who was promised to another; but he could not help it. And then, when at last she appeared, accompanied by Nancy, it was like the appearance of spring and winter together. Ruby, on her side, did not hide her pleasure at being allowed, for the first time in her life, to walk with someone besides the old woman. How pretty she looked as she greeted Bob warmly, and how she smiled at Alec when she told him about the haunts of the birds, and where the flowers first came out, and a hundred little things she had noticed. Her talk was like a charming little brook on a hillside hurrying on to the river, humming the softest song as it went on. Nancy at first kept near them, but when they neared a gate opening on to the high road, she said:</p>
        <p>"If you are going to loiter here, Miss Ruby, I'll go off and see my girl and her little ones." "Do," exclaimed Ruby, delighted; so presently the cousins found themselves alone, for Bob had made himself scarce, and they could now talk to their heart's content. Alec wondered if the squire would approve; but it was not his duty to suggest this, so he gathered poetical roses in this winter wood, and felt angry that he had never befog() had the chance of knowing the perfection of girlhood. Ruby's range of subjects was perhaps not very ex- tensive, for her experience had been limited, but there was no want of thought in her conversation. She was one of those natures who only require opportunity to become really grand characters ; but at present she could not feel more than that she was in a cage, and she was longing to ask someone to open the door for her so that she might fly away. All too soon they met Nancy again and returned home, Bob reappearing on the way. "Bob, my friend, where have you been ? " asked Alec Beauworth.</p>
        <p>"Loafing round, Mr. Alec. I've got to keep my eyes open for the old gentleman. Mother says he's a roaring lion; but I says she's wrong—he don't roar at all, but goes sneaking about. You must be a sharp 'un to be even with he." At the midday meal all the party met, and Ruby gave a vivid account of her walk. The squire was somewhat silent, Mr. Felton was in high spirits, and James rather sulky. "If you will let me, I should like to drive you to Huckdale, our little country town," said the squire to his guest; "I want you to examine some of the title-deeds of the estate."</p>
        <p>Just as they were starting, Bob jumped up behind on the dog-cart. "Cook said, sir, I was to get some things for dinner," he explained. "One of the things she wants particular has disappeared." The squire said nothing, and Bob's face relaxed into a smile as he drove off. He had already made himself so useful to the two principal servants that they could not praise him enough. Bob kept saying to himself: "If only he would have gone away; but as he wouldn't, I must do the best I can. It's Miss Ruby as keeps him, I can see that, Ods Daggs!" And with this comfort he had to be satisfied. He was keeping his eye on the enemy.</p>
        <p>tone who seemed to nave no relations except a rond father, who would give her away against her will. All these thoughts ran through Alec's brain as he listened to her fresh young voice singing "Robin Adair." He longed to say: I wish I were in truth "Robin Adair" ; but no, the squire was there, and he had no business to hint that he was as foolish as any ordinary young man who falls in love with every fresh face he sees, till the next drives out the last. There was an old-fashioned grace about Ruby and a distincion of manner which can sometimes be found in the descendants of old families. Sir Harry had nothing of it, he was nrierely a handsome man, with no likeness to Ruby's finely-chiselled features. It was altogether a mis- take; and Alec found himself wishing that he had never come to the old place, which would, perhaps, some day be his. "It is time for you to retire, Miss Ruby," said the squire, when the clock struck nine and the song ended. "0 papa, not to-night. In honour of Cousin Alec, I might stay up."</p>
        <p>"And what about the roses on your cheeks? No, no, not to-night." Alec opened the door for her. "To-morrow you will sheNv me some more of your favourite haunts in the woods," he said, and Ruby assented, kissing her father as he remarked: "I will come presently, darling." As she ran out of the hall, Ruby dropped a flower which Alec had brought from the town for her, and he picked it up unseen and hid it in his pocket as he returned to the squire. "I wanted to talk to you without my child," said the squire, "as your visit will be short. You have made her acquaintance, and you will understand my difficulties. When I die she will be penniless. In my youth I was not of a saving disposition, and all my income is derived from this estate, which is, as you have seen to-day, very tightly tied up." "But the rent-roll is respectable," said Alec, with a smile.</p>
        <p>"Yes, certainly, but I have many claims and the property requires much to be spent on it. In fact, I have not been able to save."</p>
        <p>"That is why," said Alec, quickly, "you have promised your child to your cousin in order to make sure of her future. But, if I may say so, is it not rather a cruel thing? " "We are not masters of our actions. lam very fond of Ruby. Ever since she was seven years old we have not been separated, and I have thought it right that whatever happened she should not be left penniless." - "He might have saved for her," thought Alec, looking at the man before him, and wondering what kind of youth he had spent. "You have brought me here to say this to me ? " he said, coldly. "Not exactly. I wanted you to understand your chances of inheritance."</p>
        <p>"But suppose that you married again, and that your wife had a son, I should count for nothing. I do not peckon on this property; I never have done so.. It can only be--'.. .</p>
        <p>"In the meanwhile you are the heir, and you can do a great deal for Ruby, who, as I said, when you inherit, will be penniless." "She will have her husband," said Alec, in no very cheerful voice. "You will come into a large estate, and she will have no one to look to."</p>
        <p>"Even if I had never seen her, I am not the man to forsake my relations." "But there is another case; suppose you die, say soon after inheriting, then all this estate passes fto a still more distant relation, who is only con- tnected with the Beauworths in remote times." I "He would most likely not be less generous to a • girl like Ruby than I should be." . "Excuse me, I have found out that he is a man of the world, and that if I were to leave Ruby to his tender mercies, I should be--" He paused as if overcome with the vision he was calling up in his own mind. "Let me assure you that. if you had trusted me sooner, there would have been a very natural way out of your difficulties, and one which I only wish it could be in--"</p>
        <p>"Yes, I see, but no, this is not possible. James Felton is most anxious to make Ruby his wife. There is no drawing back from that." "Who would not be anxious? " exclaimed Alec, impetuously. "Ruby is a dear girl, but she is penniless. James proposed for her hand at a time when I was in distress, and his father has done me a great service."</p>
        <p>"Oh! in that case you are using your daughter to pay a bill," said Alec, scornfully. "If she were my daughter I should tell her to consult her heart, and I should let the man she loved most win the</p>
        <p>day." "You are a young man and ignorant of many things," said the squire, slowly. "Perhaps, but I would give much if I might try to teach Ruby that life is not the happier for riches. I have been poor all my life, and yet I have not been unhappy." "You may have lived on expectations," said Sir Harry, with slight scorn in his voice. "That is always exciting.' "I have not been so foolish; even now I do not quite understand why you have wished me to come into your life. You might marry, and I might never see you or your daughter again." "Destiny is a strange thing, said the squire, slowly. "You are a young man, you know nothing of the complications of life, and how one thing of necessity drags another after it. As you say, I might marry, I might have children, I might do many things which would seem easy and probable to you, but in fact they are not probable from causes which you do not see and which you cannot understand." _ _ _</p>
        <p>"lam young in years, perhaps," said Alec, "but because I have had to struggle with fortune, and because of my natural temperament, I have thought a good deal, and I know that it is simpler in the end to go straight on." "Yes, one has to go straight on. The way is marked for one, and one must needs walk in it." "You mean about your daughter? " said Alec. "I think that no considerations, no past mistakes would justify the sacrifice of a child—and such a child." "You speak like that because you admire Ruby. If your admiration is sincere you will try and do what is best for her. It is best for her to marry James Felton. It was arranged long before you were invited. I have been open with you." "Yes, you have," said Alec. "I do feel a great interest in Ruby. If anything I could do would make things easier for her, I would beseech you to throw over James Felton—"</p>
        <p>"Oh you are speaking from self-interest, as I said just now, because you admire Ruby. Every- body would admire her if they saw her. Alec felt that the elder man saw through him. Of course, it was because he had fallen in love with Ruby at first sight that he was so anxious that she should not be coerced. Had she been nothing to him,would he have been so anxious on the subject? Alec did not answer at once, then he said: "It is true that everybody would admire her, but her father should protect her." "That is what I have done. I have settled a marriage for her. which I know will be for her advantage." "In what way? " "If I had not done that Ruby would have had certainty of having a home." Alec could not understand, but referred again to his former idea. "It would be better to leave her free to choose her life. She is such a child, so ignorant of the world, surely you will let her choose ? " "The happiest marriages are settled by outsiders. Ruby is a child, quite incapable of knowing what is best for her and for me. She is very fond of me; she would consult my wishes." "Naturally." "She will do what is best for her father," repeated Sir Harry. "Her future husband does not seem very atten- tive to her," said Alec, feeling ill-tempered. "At all events, he has not ,tried to win her by many attentions." "There will be time for that when you sre gone," said Sir Harry, coldly. "When lam gone! Then he does not care, ho does not realise what it means to win the love of a girl like Ruby." "You are an idealist, Beauworth. You have not seen much of the world and you do not understand James Felton." - _ _</p>
        <p>"No, I do not understand him—or you." "Well, if you are so anxious abolit Ruby, you may prove it by carrying out a plan I have sketched." "What is I would do anything for Ruby, if I might put some conditions." The squire thought a minute. "Well, what are they? But first hear my plan. Ruby will be poor if I should die or if anything happens to me. One can never be sure of anything in this life." The squire shaded his eyes and Alec put in: "Of course, one can be sure of nothing.' For bulance—r-" Aleq. pulled himca up Lbe bad</p>
        <p>been on the point or forgetting his promise and telling the squire of his adventure. "If you will sign a paper promising to become guardian to Ruby in case of my death, then-" "If she were married she would not want me " __. . _</p>
        <p>"But cannot you see that it would Le a comb= to me? As you know, 'you will b:ire—yon have now—the power of leaving this property to anyone. If you have children you will have no difficulty in leaving it, but if you do not marry make Ruby your heir. After all she is a Beauworth, and should by rights--" "Yes, that cutting out of daughters ads very unfair, but then-"</p>
        <p>"You feel that you would benefit Ruby's husband ? "</p>
        <p>"Well, rather ! "</p>
        <p>"But suppose you died before Ruby was married, then the girl would be in a better position to. choose her own life."</p>
        <p>Alec laughed. "I am a mere puppet in your estimation." "Oh, it's only a bare chance. All wills depend on chances."</p>
        <p>"But I don't want to chance my death! Not that I am superstitious. There are some men—but. more women, I am glad to say—who hate making their wills, thinking it means sudden death! Sir Harry laughed uneasily as he looked at Alec. "If you had been such a one I should never have broached the subject to you." "What you wish me to do means very little," said Alec, thoughtfully. "Of course, if I married I should immediately make another will." "Of course. It is only for the present that lam asking this." Alec thought a minute, then he said: "But I must make one condition."</p>
        <p>"Certainly ; what is it? " "That you should not tell Ruby herself, and that she should not be forced to accept the hand of any suitor before the end of this year. On these condi- tions I will make the will yoa suggest." Sir Harry paused a moment. "I shall not force her—but suppose that it is her own earnest wish then. . . but after all you are only giving something which will be of no use to you unless you marry." "True, but men think a good deal even of giving what is no use to themselves," said Alec, laughing. "I have had so little to give in my lifq, and now—"</p>
        <p>"Then you feel for Ruby ? " "Miss Grey, who brought me up, loved me as you love Ruby, but I was not her son." "Ah " said the squire, "I heard that you were devoted to her."</p>
        <p>"She taught me to value what my mother had valued. Without her, I should indeed have been left to a cold world of strangers." There was a pause, then Sir Harry remarked: "To save time, I have had my wishes about the property written out in legal form. Will you think about my wishes to-night, and to-marrow I will ask you for your decision." When Alec found himself in his room he sat down and meditated deeply upon the conversation he had had. The squire had been very plain with him, and certainly he had not disguised his wishes. Alec felt that now was the time to shew his dis- interestedness. It is hard to do something which will bring us no visible benefit. Ruby would not know now what he had done for her, and the man who was going to make her his wife would reap the benefit in case he, Alec, died unmarried.</p>
        <p>Alec forgot all this reasoning when his thoughts reverted to Ruby herself. He wondered whether she liked him, and he wasted some time in wonder- ing how it might have been had he known her sooner, and had he been in a position at this moment to say to her father: "I am a rich man, give her to me." Tired out with thought he retired to bed and to sleep. The great poster bed oppressed him, however. He wished that the red faded curtains did not keep out all the air, and when at last he fell asleep he had bad dreams. Someone was trying to take Ruby away, and he was running after her, but though he strained every nerve he never could get near to her. Suddenly, in his dream, he felt that he was nearing a precipice, and that if he could not reach her she must be killed. He struggled to go faster, and then, in an agony of fear, he called out, and started up in bed. His fire had died down to the merest flicker, but almost immediately Alec had a strange feeling that he was not alone in the room, and that something had been moving near him. For one instant he listened. Someone was breathing, or was it his own breath? He jumped out of bed and struck a match. Then he remembered Bob. Of course, it was the boy who was in the big cupboard. He walked across the room and opened the door of the great roomy cupboard; but as he did so, he thought that again he heard the faintest sound from the other side of the heavy curtains. He opened the door, but no Bob was there. Then he remembered that he had quite forgotten to unlock the window. He walked' round to where he had heard the sound, but nothing was visible. He must be dreaming, or it must be, as Bob had said, that the house was haunted. He went back to the window and opened it. The night air came in cold and crisp. He drank it in and felt refreshed, then after a short time, he went back to his bed and fell asleep. (To be continued.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>CHAPTER VIII.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER VIII.</p>
        <p>That evening Alec fell in love in real earnest, and it came about in a very natural manner. In the first place, the two Feltons did not come in fos the evening meal, and Ruby did the honours oi the old Hall with such evident delight, and with BO much fun, that even her father hardly recog• nised her. The girl felt as if she had known Alec all her life; and he on his side was fascinated by her beauty. He knew now that he had fallen in love at first sight; but he also knew that the squire had nc intention of allowing hinilto become his daughter's lover. The visit to the lawyer had not been very interesting to Alec. He had patiently looked over deeds, and had recognised that he was certainly the heir-at-law, and that this inheritance could be left by will by the last Beauworth. In default, it would revert lo a distant blanch of the family. If Sir Harry married and had a son, of course this would put Alec out of the question—and this was certainly possible. In his heart he wondered that Sir Harry had not married again, for there must be many a woman who would be only too delighted to be mistress of this old property. The fact remained, however, that he, Alec Beauworth, was the heir of Yewdale Hall, and that Sir Harry now fully recognised it. "But why did he not ask me to stay with him before, when I could have won the heart of this charming child ? " thought Alec. "She is a child, after all, and what businecs have I to fall in love with her when her father means her to marry that young bully. lam sure that James Felton does not care two straws for a girl like Ruby. What does it all mean? He is certainly not jealous, and his love must be for the estate; whilst if I might love her, I would marry:. her if she had not a penny." Ruby came back to the hall just as Alec was thus meditating. She had gone to fetch some music. "Papa loves to hear me sing," she said, "but I play so badly, Cousin Alec, that you will laugh at me."</p>
        <p>"Perhaps your cousin can play," said Sir Harry. "Ask him, Ruby. The Beauworths are said to be a musical family." "Yes, I am fond of music, and can play enough to accompany a song," answered Alec. "Papa, I know now you did once play very well, for I have read it in the old letters."</p>
        <p>The squire did not look at Ruby as he answered. "But, my dear child, you must know that a young man leaves such frivolities behind him when he goes to Australia as young as I did." "Oh, that is no excuse! Why, you mentioned in your letter to my grannie that you sang Robin Adair' to some friend you made on your landing, and that it made you think of the old Hall. That is why you are so fond of it, isn't it? Will you play it, Cousin Alec Ff Then Ruby's sweet young voice, like a wild, free bird, rang out in the old hail: 'What made the assembly shine? Robin Adair. What made the ball so fine? Robin Adair. • What, when the:play was o'er, What made my heart so sore? Oh, it was parting with Robin Adair."</p>
        <p>Alec knew that Ruby was not thinking of him in the least as she sang these words, or she could not have laughed so happily as she ended. "Papa, when shall Igoto a ball ? Oh, I should love to dance at a real ball."</p>
        <p>"My child has been kept from all:such things, and yet you see how a woman will be worldly even if kept away from the world," said the squire, patting Ruby's cheek. Alec thought that he had never seen anything so sweet and perfect as this child, so eager to see beyond her own narrow life. "Your father is only waiting for you to be a little older before taking you to London to shew you what the world is like," he said. The squire shook his head. "Ruby is safer here with me. When she marries, she will do as her husband thinks best; till then--" _ _ -</p>
        <p>"Husbands would have to be like fairybrinces," said Ruby, laughing, "for no one ever comes here I " Then suddenly she stopped short, and looked at Alec. "Oh, I quite forgot that you had come. It seems so natural now to have a cousin..."</p>
        <p>"When you have no brothers, as is the case with Ruby, a cousin soon becomes like a brother," said the squire, and Alec felt that this was meant to remind hint that Ruby was already promised. Suddenly Alec felt rebellious. Why should this child be sacrificed to such a snob as James Felton —and snob was a mild word for him. Why should he, Alec, not try to win her for himself? But would this be honourable? Had not the squire been quite open with him? No, he must be her cousin, only her cousin—nothing more. Alec was somewhat of a philosopher. He had no intention of falling in love when he had started on his journey; and yet he had tumbled into that water, the depth of which, when a man is in it, is described as over head and ears! Ruby was pretty —very pretty—but Alec had seen even prettier girls. She was not highly educated; on the con- trary, she was evidently kept in ignorance of many things that girls of seventeen seem to know well. Perhaps it was the chivalry which is in every man worthy of the name, unless stifled by himself, which made him feel that he must have the right to protect her. The very precaution which the squire had taken to prevent him from setting his affections on his daughter had wrought the mis- chief. His better nature had revolted against the sacrifice of this young girl. But then was it a sacrifice? She knew nothing of lovers or marriage. Would not the man to whom she was given have as good a chance of pleasing her as any other, and was it fair to the child-girl to shew her marked attentions when nothing but sober friendship could come of it? Bnt even friendship must be of use.to</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>LADYSMITH "JUSTICE." --- Grave Story from Bester's Farm.</head>
        <p>LADYSMITH "JUSTICE." --- Grave Story from Bester's Farm.</p>
        <p>RECENT events in South Africa, the glamour of War, and the distance tempt us to forget that our country. men in the colonies, who, banally, are not always fight- ing, have much the same troubles as we at home. Eng- lishmen are Englishmen the workl over; tellers of the truth and haters of injustice beyond some • ther nations that mieht be named, and these national characteristics lend weight to a story that came from Natal just before the war. Mr. W.A.C. Bester (of the oft-mentioned Beaters Farm, noir Ladysmith) is a Justice of the Peace for Ladysmith, and be told of same grave experiences: " For a long time," said he, when interviewed," I suffered from very poor health. I always felt tired,was afflicted with swollen and very gainful legs, which made it difficult for me to walk, and also with severe bilious headacbes. Often I felt so bad that I had to leave my work and go to the house to lie down ; and I think that others, not so active as myself, would have given in al- together and taken to bed. Very often when I walked I became so giddy that I felt like falling; in fact, I did fall twice. On one occasion, I remember, in particular.. I had gone out early in the morning to kill an ox, and I felt so bad afterwards that I fell down, and my _ .</p>
        <p>_ HAFFIRS HAD To ASSIST me to the house. Indigestion also caused me a great deal of suffering. "Needless to say, I consulted the doctor and took the medicine he prescribed for me, but with no good effect. Afterwards, haying read in the newspapers of the cures effected in England and abroad by Dr. Williams' pink pills, I tried them, and although the first box did me only a little good, after using three boxes I felt that I was getting better. For some time longer I went on with Dr. Williams' pink pills, and they completely and permanently cured arm of the indigestion and torpid Liver which occasioned the trouble. For the last two years I have not telt a touch of my old complaints. My son-in-law, I may also state,was afflicted with neuralgia, and, acting on my advice. he used Dr. Williams' pink pills for pale people,whict: were recommended as a nerve tonic. He used to suffer terrible pain from neuralgia in. the head ; _</p>
        <p>BR WAS TORTURED</p>
        <p>so that he could neither eat' nor sleep, and felt thoroughly miserable. The pills did him an immense amount of good, and when I saw him last he could eat, work, and sleep admirably. Be was a man again, and a strong one. What is my opinion of Dr. Williams' pink pills? Well. I have no hesitation in saying that I am convinced, providing tt.ey are properly used, they are- certain to prove most beneficial in cases like mine." These experiences of a Colonial magistrate confirm the published statements of persons, equally trustworthy and respectable at home, who have been cured of ar semia, general weakees, loss of appetite, palpitation, early decay, after-effects of ieffuenzi, hysteria, paralysis, rickets, locomotor ataxy, rheumatism; sciatica, vcrofula, chronic erysipelas, consumption of the bowels and lungs. These pills are not a purgative! and contain nothing that coulJ injure the most delicate. They are genuine only with the full name, Dr. Williams' pink pills for pale people, and are sold be Dr. Williams' Medicine Company, Holborn Viaduct, London, at two shillings and nineeence a box, or six boxes for thirteen and nine. They invigorate the system after overwork, worry, and incliFerptinn of living. -"Av</p>
        <p>Married Ladies. rz.E.l 1E . FREE. Information of priceless value FREE on reciApt of stamped enve!ope, and address. TESTIMONIALS DAILY. MRS, M. FARLETGH,</p>
        <p>(Box 63,) 160: HACKNEY ROAD, LONDON, N.B</p>
