      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>TIW ,4:XAMINER' FRIDAY NOVENIBER 24, 1899.</head>
        <p>TIW ,4:XAMINER' FRIDAY NOVENIBER 24, 1899.</p>
        <p>THE FREE CITURCECES.</p>
        <p>AN "UNUSUAL HOLIDAY" FOR MR. PRICE HUGHES.</p>
        <p>Perhaps there is no Free Churchman whose face is so familiar all over the country as the Rev. H. Price Hughes, M.A. Mr. Hughes has appeared on a hundred Free Church. Council platforms, and he has gone on many begging expeditions for the West London Mission. Mr. Hughes feels that after the special duties and responsibilities of his year of office as President of the Wesleyan Conference, he may take an "unusual holiday." Accordingly he has made arrangements to leave London on January 12 for a tour to Greece, Palestine, and Egypt. He will get back about the end of February, much the better, we hope, for his holiday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>LIBERAL-MINDED ARCHDEACON.</head>
        <p>A LIBERAL-MINDED ARCHDEACON.</p>
        <p>Free Churchmen have for long felt that it is very unjust to keep their sons and daughters out of the teaching profession in so many villages in England. The Voluntary Schools of the Church of England derive the greater portion of their support from public funds, yet they insist that the teachers shall be communicants of the Church. In 8000 villages of England the only school is a Volun- tary one. Archdeacon Wilson, of Manchester, raised his voice at a recent diocesan conference against the imposition of a religious test .on candidates for the profession. "If all the clerics of the Establishment were as he," says the Free Churchman, "we should see more justice in England."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>DR. PARKER AND GEORGE WHITFIELD.</head>
        <p>DR. PARKER AND GEORGE WHITFIELD.</p>
        <p>Dr. Parker was the principal speaker at the opening of the new Whitfield Memorial Church, London. "I always feel when here in an atmo- sphere of simple piety, of unquestioning child- like faith in the living God." The Rev. J. P. Gledstone, the writer of the best biography of Whitfield that has been published—such is the opinion of Dr. Robertson Nicoll—took part in the ceremony. Dr. Parker publicly recognised his indebtedness to him for "a really useful contribution to Christian literature." In the course of his address he spoke of "a subtle something." The pastor of the church is the Rev. G. A. Suttle.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>INTERESTING PATIENTS,</head>
        <p>INTERESTING PATIENTS,</p>
        <p>We are glad to hear that the Rev. W. J. Woods, 8.A., the Secretary of the Congrega- tional Union, is slightly better. Mr. Woods staying at Broadstairs. He has been very severely pulled down, but it is confidently hoped that a long rest—say, of three months— will give him back his usual health. The Rev. Thomas Law, the Organising Secretary of the National Free Church Council, has been pass- ing the period of convalescence at Folkestone and Brighton. He expects to be at his office again before the end of this month.</p>
        <p>WHY " FREE " CHURCH?</p>
        <p>Anglicans are inclined to question the use of the word "free" by the Nonconformist churches. They say that they are no more " free " than the Establishment. But what Noncon- formists mean is very simple and undoubted. They are free to elect their own religious teachers and leaders, free to change the order of service and introduce what they think best; free from the possible interference of Parlia- ment; tree trom the obligation to take tithes; and free from being placed in the invidious position of being supported by the taxes of those who do not agree with their worship. They do not for one moment suppose that they are independent of the law. If freedom means "independence of the law," there are no free men or free women in England.</p>
        <p>WHEN THE WAR IS OVER.</p>
        <p>The Free Church Chronicle for December is, we think, an especially interesting number. The attitude of Free Churchmen towards the war is canvassed at some length, and there are important letters from Dr. Monro Gibson, Mr. Silas K. Hocking, the Rev. F. B. Meyer, 8.A., and Mr. J. H. Yoxall, M.P. The editor rather inclines to the opinion that when the war is over the Transvaal mining district should be detached from the rest of the country and in- dependently administered, and that the Boers inhabiting the agricultural districts should receive a generous measure of self-government under the British flag. There is a most interesting account of the extra- ordinary work for social purity which has been done by a Free Church Council in a poverty- stricken and degraded part of the metropolis. Lord Rosebery's recent speech on Cromwell is highly praised. There is a good supply of fresh and interesting news.</p>
        <p>THE REV. J. H. JOWETT, M.A.</p>
        <p>Dr. Dale's successor at Carr's-lane Chapel, Birmingham, has been slightly indisposed on account of too great a burden of work. Mr. Jowett is in great demand as a, preacher on special occasions, but in a letter which we saw the other day he expresses an intention to limit his outside engagements. A busy and success- ful minister has to meet great demands on his nervous energy and intellectual force. His reputation tempts him to overwork himself, and almost inevitably produces a breakdown. Mr. Jowett evidently means to take himself in hand in time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>LORD ROSEBERY ON CROMWELL.</head>
        <p>LORD ROSEBERY ON CROMWELL.</p>
        <p>The ex-Premier's eulogy of the Great Pro- tector at the Queen's Hall, London, was beard with delight by an audience which crowded the great building from floor to ceiling. It was in some parts rather an exclusive and fashionable assembly, and the enthusiasm was not quite so unrestrained and tumultuous as at the City Temple demonstrations in April. All Lord Rosebery's acts and words have not won the approval of Noncomformists, but there is a remarkable unanimity as to the genuine religious fervour and intensity which marked his Cromwell oration. The British Weekly, the Christian World, and the Methodist Times were all loud in its praises. The sentences in which Lord Rosebery described the secret of the Great Protector's extraordinary power have been specially noted: "He was a practical mystic, the most formidable and terrible of all combinations. A man who combines inspira- tion apparently derived—in my judgment really derived—from close communion with the supernatural and the celestial, a man who has that inspiration and adds to it the energy of a mighty man of action, such a man as that lives in communion on a Sinai of his own, and when he pleases to come down to this world below seems armed with no less than the terrors and decrees of the Almighty himself."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>Ladies' , Ailments.</head>
        <p>Ladies' , Ailments.</p>
        <p>Speedy relief will be derived by taking at once Mrs. St Clair's fam.ns 'Specific." Known for 20 years as the Safes.- and Most Effectual Me lieine of its kind. Cures all Irregn larities and Promotes and Maintains Regular Health. Testi, monials Daily. Avoid Imitations. Post free (under cover) price 2s. 9d., 4s 9c1., and lls, per case. Miss. N. ST. CLUB. 46, Southampton Buildings, London, W.C. Particulars ant Advice for Stamped Address. N.B.—Not to be taken for Illegal Purposes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>LIBERAL WOMEN.</head>
        <p>LIBERAL WOMEN.</p>
        <p>MEETING IN THE CO-OPERATIVE HALL. Address by Miss Balp,-,arnie.</p>
        <p>A meeting under the auspiess of the Warrington Women's Liberal Association was held in the Co- (Terence Mall, Sankey-street on Tuesday evening. Mr A. H. Crosueld pride ci and he was supported on the platform by the Mayoress (Mrs. Henry Roberts), Mrs. John Crosfifild, Mrs. Clay, Sirs. Walker and Mrs. Grimshaw (secretary). Letters of apology were read by the CHAIRMA.N. Miss B. Dutton,of Moore, wrote that she very much regretted her inability to be present. Mrs. Lba Armitage wrote that the was unable to be present but expressed the hope that there would be a large attendance.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN in his opening remarks said he thought it was his duty to express on their behalf the feeling of deep sympathy that they must all feel for the Premier who had suffered a terrible blow in the loss of his wife. Sinking absolutely at this moment all differences of Opinion, they recognised the way in which the Premier had urdertaken the terrible burden of both the Foreign Office and the Premiership, either of which was suffi- cient for any man to carry on his shoulders, without this additional trouble, this irreparable loss, which had befallen him. He only expressed the feelings of everyone there in giving voice to their sympatby and condolence on that untoward event. Se many of them who were present that night came to hear Miss Balgarnie the last time she paid them a visit that he ahnost felt inclined to sympathise with her, because on that occasion she established so high a reputation amonget them that it would be difficult for any oae •f ordinary powers to live up to it. He knew very well they were on the fUt vive, and waiting with appreciative mind for the instructive eloquence with which last year they were all edified and charmed. The subject then was something of an antithesis to the " Restrictions on Women's Labour," which was the topic that night, and a topic of the very first importance. (Hear, hear.) They would remember that on that occasion it was rather upon the emancipation during the last thirty yeare or more of women that Miss Balgarnie for the most part dwelt, and in all that had happened during the close of this wenderful century he thought it would be difficult to name anything that had added more to the sure of human happinese and well-being, in this island home of ours at any rate, than the increased opportunities for, and the wide field now open to women who wished to lead use- ful active and beneficent lives. Whatever 'pinions there might be respecting the boundaries to this unexhaust- No field of labour, there were two points upon which there could not be any doubt or queatiou at all. The influence of women, both that which was exerted priv- ately and indirectly, and that which was the result of active practical work in the Arena of public life, bad on the whole done an incalculable amount of good, and to the betterment of human affairs. In the next place such admirable. they might say such truly noble work as Miss Balgarnie had undertaken was beyond all praise, because it possessed in a pre-eminent degree what was the most important, though often less apparent feature in all politics—it touched the daily life the mental and physical well-being of not a small section of people, nor the lucky moneyed few, but the whole community. (Applause.) Emerson said that the hest pelitical economy was the care and culture of man. Yes, and women too. The lords of creation were apt to be just the least little bit self-centred se that it was just as well now and again to give them a gentle reminder, at least he thought some of the ladies there would endorse that. They told there that the wheels of humanity moved slowly on their stages towards the goal of a better civilization. Well, whether the pace be fast or slow they owed their thanks to those who had the ability and the energy to put their shoulder to the wheel and help the old coach en its up-bill journey. He had the pleasure and the honour to call upon Miss Balgarnie to address them. (Applause.) Miss BALOARNIE then delivered an address on "Special Restrictions on Women's Labour." She said that prob- ably their minds were feller of the great crisis with which they were face to face than of any ether question and, perhaps, at such a juncture almost every other fell flat. They bad had no doubt ha Warriogton their great sensations in the departure of troops for the seat of war, and up and down the country they had seen brave men torn from the arms of those they loved, and carried away to distant lands to fight in the war—a war which she, for one, wished had not been begun, and which might have been prevented bad wiser counsels prevailed. But they wore in for a war now, and naturally the minds, hearts, and sympethiel of all of them were cen- tred in that direction. She wished them te concentrate their thoughts upon another question which equally touched the women who were left behind. Women ceuld not go to the war. They were left at home in sad- ness but this question of wonten's ind ustries was one which more or less directly or indirectly concerned them all. As a Women's Liberal Association they existed pri- marily for the helping forward of great Liberal reforms; for the furtherance of Liberal principles ; hut they also existed with the object of trying to propagate the inter- ests of women and children, and further, they were striving to get the Parliamentary vote. It was of the greatest import that they should consider the labour of women. There wore persons connected with their Asso- ciation who bad thought about the subject from divers points of view. There were a few people whom she was afraid they must look upon as antiquated fossils. (Laughter,) They were people who were wrapped up in the ideas of 50 years ago, and who thought that any legislation restricting the hours of labour was a mis- take. There was another section that was most enthu- siastically in favour of legislation for women who worked in factories and workshops. They were in favour of restricting the hours of work and driving them nut of certain trades which they considered unhealthy. There was another section which she had the honour to represent which was in favour of factory legislation for women, but they contended that it was a mistake to stop at women. They contended that further factory legislation would be good for men as well as for women; but that it would be distinctly dangerous to the interest of women to legislate on purely sex lines. She was not present to oppose legislation, but legislation of a sex character. A wiser I, gielation had stepped in,and child labour was now restricted, because when the new year of 1900 dawned it would be a golden one for little chil- dren. No bey or girl under twelve would be allowed to work as a half-timer in any mill at all. We were still very much behind some countries in this matter. Proceeding, she said she objected to women being invariably classed with children in legislative enactments. She.. cave in- stances of how special restriction/ upon women'a labour had werked injuriously against women when they had been applied to women only. She contended further that women should receive equal pay with men when their work was of the same value to the employer. (Hear, hear.) Alluding to the theatrical profession she said it was one of the few professions in which women had the same advantages as the men. Except the leading "stars" they got as good a salary as the men, and met with as great a success. If therm was any class of the community with which she had the rrto;t sympathy it was for the young women—many of them being cultured and re- fined, and had known be•ter days—who had to work Vag hours in shops, and still longer in their bars and refreshment rooms. (Hear,hear.) But it would be better to wait until public opinion war so far advanced,as well as legislation, that there could be a shortening of the hours for both tears. There would have to be further legislation with regard to dangerous trades, net legisla- tion tending to drive One sex cut of employment but to make the employment healthy for both sexes. They wanted legislation for men and boys as well as for women and girls, and to her it seemed to be an outrage to suppose that the life of a woman was more valuable than that of a man. (Laughter and applause.) If they continued their legislation on sex lines alone they would drive women out of employment, as had been done in Neweastleoind the ph ysieal condition of the woman would deteriorate and starvation would be the lot of many. (Applause.) Women, she pointed out, had been drift- ing out of the laundries because they had had legielar ion of a one-sided character for them only. The result was • that many of the workers drifted int • small rooms, into miserable cottagee, With which the Factory Acts could , not interfere and where there was a danger to the worker at well as to the reie who sent the lanndry work from their houses in the shape of fever. In large fac- tories women wore apt to be at a dig-mint because of one-sided legislation. They should have legislation of ruch a character that there should be the same hour for opening the laundries both for men and women. (Hear, hear.) There was a danger threatening women because of the determination of the Government to undertake legislation of a purely sex kind when they were striving to enter the more skilled trades They wanted a levell- ing up to a hither Standard wh ch would act upon both sexes alike. (Applause.) The MAYORESS then submitted the Following resolution —" That this meeting is heartily in favour nf factory legislation, provided it is carried on in such a manner as not to prevent the employment of women. and is ex- tended as far as possible to men working in the same trades." She said she had derived much pleasure from listening to Miss Balgarnie who had shown them that when factory legislation affected women only it operated against them. She thought they might safely pass the resolution.</p>
        <p>Mrs, Grumsakw seconded. the motion with equal pleasure,and said it was necessary women should be pro- tected, and that legislation should be equal so far as men and wemen were concerned. (Applause.) The CHAIRMAN said they would b 9 pleased to hear any remarks on the suljsct. Mr. NIGHTINGALE referred to the necessity of</p>
        <p>employers providiO4 their workmen with the best con- ditions for carrying on their sv,rk. Hy providing healthy work,hops and improving the condition of the men, the employers w.Aild get more work out of their man and better work. (Hear, hear.) Flo had known men ren- dered unfit tor their work by thing dirty water instead of clean. The eame argoment applied to women. A great many of the dangerous trades to-day were dan- gerous because little precautions were not taken which employ ers at a small expense could provide. Mr. O'finiax spoke of the conditions unAer which women worked in the weavinr, trade in Clinton, in the Uni'ed States, and which were saperior to those in England. The workshops were kept in a cleaner and healthier condition. The Americans did not act on continent altogethe:. The almighty dollar still existed—(laugkv.er)—odt the conditions under which the people worked were favorable. The pay of the women in soma cases exceeded teat of the men. The CHAIRMAN observed that a certain class of labour at the vr irks with wiac!) he was connected was now better done by females that it had been by males. They had added a new branch to their industry—high class perfumery and toilet preparations. There was no com- parison in this resphi:t : male labour would not be in it with the female labour. (Hear hear, and applause.)</p>
        <p>. , . . In answer to a question by Mi 44 13.sizarni.e as to whether it wae ever found neceiiary to work over- time.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN said youth and men connected with the works had worked till nine and ten and, he was sorry to say, until eleven o'clock at night wnea a Ire• mendous push had ben on ; tit difficulty had net yet arisen with regard to female labour. Nor did he think the firm would be justificd in putting them to that push. rtie motion was carried. •</p>
        <p>Mr. MOUNFIRLD prop-ised a vote of thanks to the lecturer,remarking that he never could see why different wages should be given to women when ttere was the same proluction, and the sarn profit was gained to the employer. (Hear, hear.) Mr. JACKSIN HOLVEi seconded, and this )(lotion also was heartily agreed t, the CHAIRMAN romarking tiat he knew of no lady who came to the town to lecture t) them who gave them m,re original matter than Miss Balgarnie did. (Applause.) Miss BiLGARNIE returned thanks, and propesed a vote of thanks to Mr. Crasfield for pr.siding. This was seconded he Mrs. CLAY, who remarked that she knew no one in kVarrington for whom they had a greater respect than Mr. Arthur Crosfield. (Applause.) The motion was carried unanimously.</p>
        <p>Mr. Cnosrnmp, in reply, remarked that last Christmas Day, at the annual c'eanine up of the works, the decorations were carried out almost entirely by the f males employed there, and the work was well done. The works were thrown open to the inspection of the inhabitants, and t ey had no fewer than 8,000 visitors. It was highly prob.)ble that the experiment would be tried again. (Applause.) The meeting then terminated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
