      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>CHARGE AGAINST A LADY-</head>
        <p>CHARGE AGAINST A LADY-</p>
        <p>Second Class Carriage and Third Class Ticket. Case Dismissed.</p>
        <p>On Monday at the 8)r ougti Police Court Warrington, before Mr. Minks (in the chair), and other magistrates Miss rate Clegg 52, Rose-lane, M issley Hill, was charged with having on the 25:h Ootober travelled front Moseley Hill to Warrington " with-mt having paid the fare." She was also charged with travelliag from Warrington to Mossley Hill under similar circumstances. Mr. Browne appeared for the defendant, Mr. Fenna provecu- ted on behalf, of the London and North-Western Rail- way Company. He stated that the defendant had a third class season ticket between Mossley Hill and Run- corn, but on the occasions in qus“ tion she travelled in a second class compartment and did not pay the excess fare. Although a fare had been paid in this case, it was not the proper fare. The onus upon him was to prove the intention to avoid payment, and he thouOtt that in this case the evidence would show a deliberate intention to do so. He should show that it was a rersistent act. These were not isolated cases, and the company were represented that morn- ing to stop the system carried on by the lady. She travelled on the date in question fr )m Mossier Hill. At Ditton Junction she was seen in a second class com- partment. She arr'ved at Arpley Statioa by the train due there at 114 p.m. She went to the collector and said " Ditton," and handed in a third class ticket, al- though she knew perfectly well that she had travelled secend class. She offered the third class fare, although she ought to have paid second class fare between Dittoa and Warrington. On the second occasion she travelled from Bank Quay to Mossley Hill. She went to the book- ing office under observation and took a third class ticket to Ditton, but she deliberately went into a s-cond class eempartment. Detective Howell travelled by the same train and noticed that RP she passed out of the station she did not pay the excess fare. He followed her into the street, and he went home with her at her request, and she admitted the offence, but added that she had done so because the third cla,s carriages were filled with dirty workmen, but it was found that the assertion was not correct. With regard to the second charge, she said she got into the second clam compartment in mistake. _ .</p>
        <p>The Chairman : Has there not been some laxity in the past in this respect on behalf of the company ? _</p>
        <p>Kr. Fenna : 1 don't admit that.</p>
        <p>The Chairman : Of course, you won't. (Laughter.) I have travelled more than once in a first•class carriage, and have had disreputable chArscters put in. Mr. Fenna : That occurs in North Wales during the summer seasen when the traffic is very heavy, but not in these parts except on Bank Holidays. The Chairmen : On a Bank Holiday I have had to go inte the guard's van. Mr. Birchall : A more serious thing railway com- panies are guilty of is in allowing people in a st ite of intoxication to tray I in the earriagee. I came from Ellesmere Port on Saturday night, and I had the same complaint to make. _</p>
        <p>Mr. Fenna : Bot that is beside the question, I am regularly prosecuting people under the bylaws for being drunk on the company's premises. It is sometimes diffi- cult to tell when a man is drunk. Mr. Birchall : We have that difficulty here.</p>
        <p>Several witnesses gave evidence in support of Mr. Fenna's statement. Mr. Browne contended that before the Bench could convict they must find that Miss Clegg intended to avoid payment. His contention was that she had no such intention. She was simply avoiding dirty and objectionable passengers as she was rendered sick owing to overcrowding a short time ago. The stationmaster at Mossley Ef ill had put her into a second class carriage and she had changed carriages at Widnes and had besn put into a second class owing to the train being overcrowded. In these circumstances she thought she would be doing no harm in going int, a second class carriage. The Bench then retired from the Court to consider their decision, and on their return the Chairman said the magistrates recognised Mr. Fenna's position,and also Mr. Brovvne's. There was no queelon as to the actual facts, but it was within the knowledge of the Bench that there had been a good deal of laxity in connection with travelling in first and second-cla. s carriages, and the defendant might have come to the conclusion that what she did was not a matter of much censequenee. She might have prac icallv given herself the benefit of the custom. In the circumstances both cases would be dismissed. Not only so, but she had been put into a second-class carriage several times by the officials. Mr. Fenna : But it is lawful and right for a servant of the company to do so. The Chairman : We recognise your legal positions. Mr. Farina : We have a right to do so. lam not arguing against the decision. The Chairman : We recognize that whatever yon d you have a perfect right to do. Mr. Fenna ; Lela!l take it that we have been too kind in the past. Mr. Birchall : You should find more accommodation for third-class passengers. Sometimes you have not sufficient.</p>
        <p>Mr. Smethurst : There should be a ladies' compart- meat in every train. Mr. Fenna : There is no law to compel us to do so. We are here in a criminal court, but I hope that what has been said will do good. Mr. Birchall : I hope it will have served a purpose. Mr. Fenna : I should like the Bench to say that they think this is a case .he company ought to have brought into court to inquire into. The Chairman : They are proper cases for inquiry. Mr. Birchall : We are all convinced of that.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <p>* --A-- AltgL * 3E.44r * _*,.3teg--lic-**.- 44,* 41( * 41,0 Alt</p>
