      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH PAROCHIAL</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH PAROCHIAL</p>
        <p>COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>A meeting of this committee was held in the National Schools, Stockton Heato, 14s`, Tnarsday evening. Mr. Davenport presided and there were also present : Messrs Pearson, Whitley, Wintry', Pemberton, Clarke, G Pilling, ith G Ashton (eliak),J Swinton (surveyor),and J Farrington (nuisanoe insp.ctor.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>TELE ADOPTION OF TUE PAIL SYSTEM.</head>
        <p>TELE ADOPTION OF TUE PAIL SYSTEM.</p>
        <p>The Clerk read a le_t .r from 11r. Aibiusoa in which he stated that he !WAS prepared to supply covers for the pails he had been instructed to provide at lls each. The Inspector was instructei to communicate with the Corporation as to the cot of covers for the pails used in the borough.</p>
        <p>THE LIGHTING ~F THE DISTRICT.</p>
        <p>The Surveyor produced a plan so wing the position of the lamps lighted in the district. The Clerk stated that there were 35 lamps lighted by the C)rporation. The committee paid for 32. It was clear that the cominitte had not paid for the lamp at the corner of Old London-road as contended by the Cor- poration. Mr. Whitley reported that th.,re ha,l been some com- plaints as to the long distances between lamps in the district. He made several valuable suggestions, and moved a resolution that the Lighting Committee go round the district and insp:3ct the lamps on Tuesday evening next. The Chairman seconded, and the resolution was serried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>AN UNSIGHTLY HOARDING.</head>
        <p>AN UNSIGHTLY HOARDING.</p>
        <p>Mr. Whitley asked why a hoarding had been allowed to remain up so long near the Mulierry Tree, seeing that the new house behind it was furnished and occupied P The Chairman said the hoarding was not only an obstruction but an eye-sore. He should think the property had ben finished for months. Mr. Pemberton said the hoarding might be an eye-sore but he could not see that it was an obstruction. It was sm private property. Mr. Clarke : It is not a private road round there.</p>
        <p>• Mr. Pemberton : I beg you pardon, it is. Mr. Whitley. It is a road leading to the property. Mr. Clarke said there was a joint ownership of the road. The plans were submitted to the Parochial Com- mittee by Mr. Pernberon's predecessor. Mr. Pemberton : lam not responsible for what my predecessor may have done. Mr. Clarke ; There is an agreement existing between your predecessor and Messrs. Greenall, Whitley, and Company. It is impossible for Mr. Pemberton to go back,son his word and say it is a private road. Mr. Pemberton : I say I have never submitted any plans for th it road. Mr. Clark. : I can tell the comrni'tee of my own kr:owledge that this is a joint road opened out by Messrs. Greenall's and Mr. Pemberton's predecessor under a mutual agreement, and the plans have been pissed by this Committee.</p>
        <p>Mr. Pernb?rton : I say the Colvnittee ought not to take notice of anything of the sort. If Mr. Clarke knows what my rights are he has no right to come here and state them.</p>
        <p>The Chairman : I must call you to order Mr. Pemberton : Mr. Ch re has no right to bring in anything relating to a private agreement. The Chairman : We are dealing with the hoarding. I should like to ask the Clerk whether we are able to deal with the matter.</p>
        <p>The Clerk : Is this house behind the hoarding oceu- pied ? Mr. Whitley Yes. _ _ _</p>
        <p>The Clerk: Then it may be a question of insufficent air space. It would be better if tne Surveyor reported to me on the matter and I should then be in a position to advise the committee. The Chairman: There must be some reason why this hoarding is kept up. There must b 3 some reuse we don't know of.</p>
        <p>It was decided that the Surveyor should prepare a report on the matter.</p>
        <p>2011 PAVINIII OF GASKELL-13TRMIT.</p>
        <p>MR. PEMBERTON'S OPPOSITION.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said he would like to know what was being done towards paving Gaskell-street. He had re- ceived many complaints as to the condition of the un- paved streets in the district and medical men told him that they were responsible for fevers and diseases in the township. The Surveyor said the plans had been prepared for the work but the Rural District Council had recently passed a resolution specifying what materials had to be used throughout the district for new streets. Mr. Clarke: A resolution has been passed by the committee authorising the work to be done. The Chairman said it was time something was done in the matter. The streets were a disgrace to the town- ship. Mr. Pemberton said the plans prepared had never been sent back to the committee from Dutton. The Chairman said they had come back. Mr. Pemberton said the Rural Council had agreed that concrete flags were to be put down. Mr. Clarke said Gaskell-street would have to be made from top to bottom. The Chairman asked whether the Rural Council had submitted th•ir new specification. The Surveyor said they had The Clerk informed the committee that the general specification for new streets had been definitely settled and the committee would have to adhere to it. Mr. Pemberton thought it would be better for the committee to go into the matter before sending out notices. Personally he was not affected to any great extent but he should certainly oppose concrete flags. Ho was quite willing that thl street should be done, but he should appeal against such an extravagant specifica- tion as had been approved by the Rural Council. Mr. Whitley : There is no one to appeal to. Mr. Pemberton : There is an appeal to the Local , Government Beard.</p>
        <p>Mr. Pearson said there was only one person stopping the work of street making and that was Mr. Pembertin. The policy was to serve notices on the property owners at once and those who had any objections must object. Mr. Pensberton said the plans had been before the committee and approved of. The Council had since passed the new specifications. The committee would be getting into trouble with the matter. • Mr. Pearson said he would like to knew how 'many years they were going to be in getting these streets made. He did not need to be told who had obstructed them ; Mr Pemberton, one of their own members, had been the great obstacle in their way. Formerly it was the width of the curbs that he obj•eted to, then to the depth. and now his latest opposition was to the " corn- crete " flagging. (Laughter.) Re :bought it was incon- sistent of a man who was supposed te be representing the ratepayers to be representing himself. He did not think they ought to take any notice of Mr. Pemberton's ot jection. Mr. Clarke moved that notices be served for the work to be done. Mr. Pemberton did not think it was fair for Mr. Pear- son to make personal allusions like that.</p>
        <p>The Chairman: I want a seconder ta the resolution. Mr. Mclntyre seconded. The resoluEion was carried. Mr. Pemberton said he could not allow Mr. Pearson's remarks to pass without comment. Mr. Pearson (interrupting), said he would leave it to the members present to judge whether the chief oppo- nent to the making of the streets had not been Mr. Pemberton.</p>
        <p>Mr. Pemberton (warmly) : Nothing of the kind. My part of Gaskell-street is the best portion. I have put footpaths there, and no one can say a word about the street opposite my propPrt. The Chairman: Mr Pearson has had a long experi- ence on the committee. ' Mr. Pemberton : I will leave the public to judge of the condition of the strcet in front of my property. Mr Pearson : Why do you oppose the work? Mr. Pemberton : It is unnecessary. The meeting then broke up.</p>
      </div>
