        <p>WIDOW WELCH'S PILLS FOR FEMALE COMPLAINTS /I RS. SMITHERS. Grand-daughter to the Widow 1_ Welch. continues to prepare this celebrated Medicine from the only original and genuine Family Recipe. Its just celebrity for all Female Complaints has obtained for it a most extensive and ißcreasing demand Purchasers should therefore attend to the following CAUTION,—Te be careful that what they purchase is prepared by Mrs. Smithers, who is the only person in possession of the ORIGINAL RECIPE. The genuine are wrapped in Blue Paper, and signed on the Outfitle label by Mrs. Smithers, and the names of the sole Wholesale Agents," W. EDWARDS &amp; Sox 157, Queen Victoria-street," formerly of 67, St. Pau Is' London, are engraved on the Government Stamp, Sold in boxes at 28. 9d. each by _most Chemist.</p>
        <p>IT is probably true that two The Dutch members of the Cape Legis- Colonists and lative Assembly have joined the War. _ _ _</p>
        <p>the Boers at Colesborg. We Bee no reason for disbelieving that the young men at the training college at Burghersdo:p have taken the same course. South of the Orange Free State a good many British subjects have joined the in- vaders, and the same thing has happened in parts of Natal. The fact that so many of the prisoners taken about Ladysmith only spoke the English language must be convincing evidence that some of them had been living in British possessions. We were threatened with the defections, and some of them have taken place without making any stir. On the other hand, a good many British colonists of Dutch descant and proclivities have remained faithful to their allegiance, and have kept, perfectly quiet. Others have suffered for their allegiance, and they have had their farms raided of cattle and have been unpleasantly re- minded in other ways of the existing conflict. They have had to seek refuge in flight, or they have been commandeered. General GATACRE has made it known that loyal persons will receive compensa- tion for the injuries they have sustained, and he advises that the names of disloyal persons should be recorded, so that future action may be taken in regard to them. There will probably be an in- creasing number of desertions to the enemy, and of cases of individual hardship, until the advance of our columns actually begins and British territory can be cleared of the invaders. The tendency of the Boers to ramble about an 4 plunder is not an agreeable sign. Some of the shops in Dundee were looted by Boers who were natives of N atal, and the same kind of thing is going on at the farms about Estcourt. General JOUBERT has dis- couraged these raids, as he was in duty bound to do, but he has to deal with irregular troops, and he has no real authority over many of the depreda- tors, who have never come under his direct juris- diction. The robberies are part of the civil war which may be said to be raging in districts not actually under the full command of either the Boer leaders or the British generals. The Dutch who sympathise with the Boers, even when they have not openly joined them, take the oppor- tunity of being revenged upon their neighbours, and it is a terrible testimony to the chaos which prevails that these proceedings can go on to any extent just outside the districts covered by British troops as well as within the lines dominated by Boer commandos, whose leaders invariably exer- cise an &amp;nit )st independent command. The pause in the British advance has favoured this disorder, and our only hope now is in its early and steady commencement. Some congratulations have been uttered as to the advantage of having a Dutch Ministry at the Cape just now. We must offset this advantage with the state of laxity which has, in some respects, followed it, and is now becoming more manifest every day. It is true that Mr. SCHREINER has sent a message to President STRYN protesting against the commandeering of Dutch loyalists in Cape Colony, but we hardly suppose ,</p>
        <p>that it will receive much attention. The Orange Boers rather like the plundering which chance has given them an opportunity of practising, and some of them seem to be such poor specimens of soldiers that they may have been animated more by a desire to loot than to fight. At any rate, they are deter- mined to make the Dutch loyalists as miserable and as poor as they can. Such proceedings may be inseparable from war with an irregular army, but they are not calculated to increase our respect for the noble aims with which the Boers are said to I, be animated, and we shall be glad when they come to an end.</p>
        <p>A PLEASANT side to English Party Spirit politics is shewn in the and Human Feeling, tender sympathy manifested for Lord SALISBURY in his hour of bereavement. Politicians may fight in party struggles, and hurl epithets at each other, and ascribe motives to each other, but when we touch domestic life we are changed creatures. The PREMIER of England must feel, in the warm sympathy expressed for him yesterday in all parts of the country, that the people are just in their general judgments, and that they are chivalrous whenever death enters a statesman's home and strikes down the wife who has shared his vicissi- tudes and triumphs. Nothing is more English than this personal good feeling. We have seen it before on many occasions, and it always seems to show how little the wrangles of party strife disturb the generous hearts of the people. It is said that the fiercest storms on the sea leave the water twenty or thirty feet from the surface in a state of comparative repose. It is so in our politics. We may be bitter, argumen- tative, and irreconcilable. But when misfortune comes, when " the shadow feared of man" breaks up life's sweet companionships, strikes the lifelong companion, aid immures whole families in gloom, we cease to be politicians and we be- come human beings, Englishmen, and patriots. It is not necessary for us to say anything more than has been already said about Lady SALISBURY and her devotion to her husband and her family, and her fondness for the joys of domestic rather than the glare of public life. Our object is to point the moral of the whole matter, and to let it be seen with what natural human feeling we are all alive when our public men are smitten by the griefs they cannot talk about,hut must bear as best they can,whilst conducting the business of the positions they occupy and turning a brave face to the world. In Airs. GLADSTONE and in Lady SALISBURY the world has seen bright examples of that home life and tenderness which politics,we hope,will never be able to dull and destroy. The former has outlived her husband, and for her there is a world of memory and musing, in which sadness anl chas- tened joy are combined. The latter has done her I part, and the former is sure to feel more than any other person can what it is to be alone and to be bereft of the companion of busy years and political struggles. It will be impossible for us to make up to Lord SALISBURY what he has lost, and it is equally impossible for us to say or do anything that can deprive Mrs. GLADSTONE of the rare con- solations which embower her present solitude, and enable her to look forward with a zest born of per- fect love and trust. The pure domestic life of an English home stands revealed to us in either case and it lessens the bitterness of party strife and the fears that come with the conflicts of our public life. Some observers think that the influence of family life is declining. It is a superficial view, based upon a few facts that do not constitute a new departure. In the lives of our statesmen, at any rate, home has had its subtle and penetrating influence, and the significance of this haven is greater than the triumphs which may be achieved outside. It is a good thing for us all that we can cease to be slaves to a party and bow to the com- mon human feelings which make "the whole world kin."</p>
        <p>Warrington has had some unusual experiences of late, but none so unique and impressive as those connected with the departure of reservists for " Afric's foreign strand." During the past week end several hundred of our citizen soldiers have had to report themselves at Orford Barracks, and have subsequently departed for their headquarters from the London and North-Western Railway Company's Station at Bank Quay. The largest detachments were sent off on Monday evening,and the occasion was one never to be forgotten. Several of the big works closed after noon,some from choice and the remainder because they had no choice left them by their workmen, who refused to be tied down to humdrum employment when there was so much stirring. The martial spirit completely took hold of the people and when the gallant soldiers marched through the town they passed down lanes of wildly excited men, women, and children all cheering themselves hoarse in their efforts to show "Tommy" hew they loved him. The siege of Ladysmith was as nothing compared with the siege of Bank Quay Station, and notwithstanding the determined resistance of the railway staff the stronghold soon fell and the besiegers poured in. Every platform and vantage ground was occupied, while the soldiers were almost carried in by the excited multitude. Hands were clasped, wives and babies were hugged to the scarlet coats, and the men finally entrained. Martial music sounded above the din, and after " Soldiers of the Queen" had ceased, the train steamed out of the station to the strains of " Auld Lang Syne," terrific cheers, and the frantic waving of hats, sticks, garments. and well-nigh everything detachable at the moment.</p>
        <p>Most of the men were in the prime of life, and many appeared to have had a lot of the military spruceness rubbed off in the time they had been absent from the colours. They nevertheless looked a body of soldiers fit to do honour to their Queen and country when occasion demanded. In the midst of all the uproar one caught oneself wonder- ing seriously and sadly how many of the brave fellows would return. War has its glories,but it has more horrors than anything else, and one of the smallest of them is the picture of thousands of happy-go-lucky soldiers forgetting home and kindred, sinking all their affections,and answering a call to duty which may be the last they will ever hear. Many of the unhappy wives on Monday were plainly bowed down under such thoughts as these. The glamour of war was nothing to them; they stood dry-eyed and distraught as husband son, or brother, was borne from then: to the accom- paniment of deafening cheers. Their case is in- deed one for sympathy, and if we cannot restore the absent men we can at least make the lot of the wives and little ones the easier to bear. And it is a pleasing sidelight on the war that the people of the country are nobly recognising their obliga- tions in this respect.</p>
        <p>What is to be the future relationship of the cycling and motor-car trades? Sxne cycling, firms would avoid all reference to the new indus- try, and it is probably to these that motorists owe the small space placed at the disposal of motor-car , makers at the great cycle shows in London this week. For the present, it is true that cycle makers do not need to fear competition with the rival in- dustry. Cycles are cheap, while motors are absurdly dear, and can only be purchased for pleasurable purposes by the moneyed classes. Two or three years hence, however, a great reduction in price is bound to come, and the cycle trade will probably feel the effect of it in two ways. Share- holders will be anxious to put their money into</p>
        <p>motor-car companies, and large numbers of people, having had time to be tired of cycling, will take up the new amusement with avidity. At first there will be not a little amusement and personal dan- ger to be derived from motor-car driving. To master the art is as difficult and dangerous for the novice and his beholders as was the art of cycling. The public will have to be protected by stringent police regulations. In France the authorities have devised a sharp set of rules, and these are to be strengthened presently, much to the joy of foreign cycle makers, who view with favour every step that is calculated to retard the growth of an industry which is destined to inflict grave injury upon them.</p>
        <p>Much commotion was occasioned last week by the announcement that meteors, leonids, or some other more ®r less heavenly bodies were to fall, shoot,and otherwise disport themselves in the public view. On Monday, Tuesday, and Wednes- day nights thousands of eves were turned aloft in the hope of witnessing the " shower of stars." In this part of the world, at any rate, the expected display of celestial pyrotechnics was a delusion and a snare. Not a star was seen to fall, not a solitary leonid flashed across the translucent blue, nothing whatever extraordinary was to be observed in con- nection with the starry firmament. We have been shamefully imposed upon either b 3, the astronomers or the heavenly bodies, and personally we pro- test most vehemently against having our well- earned slumber and prospective sweet repose disturbed on such ephemeral grounds. It is all very well for scientists to blame the full moon or the vapoury clouds; but where is the compensation for people who have spent the small hours wandering about chilly back-gardens, or gazing through :windows till their necks were "cricked' and the edge of their tempers sawlike? We are not going to waste any more time' n watching for bashful meteors, leonids,and the like. If the solar systems cannot keep their engage- ments in a business-like manner they will get little patronage from this terrestrial sphere, and the sooner they realise it the better.</p>
        <p>There is little to complain of at present with re- gard to the condition of the water in the Ship Canal but another nuisance prevails which, if not quite so serious, is sufficiently discornforar.4 and annoy- ing to be worth taking notice of. 'T his arises from the frequent ernittance of foul, black smoke from steamers which ply up and down the waterway. Complaints have been made to the Ship Canal Company but they have had little, or no effect. It may not be generally known that the steamers are prohibited by law from emitting black smoke when passing along the canal, and inhabitants in the neighbourhood of the waterway are by no means bound to put up with the nuisance. At present they have to grin and bear it pretty frequently, but we are in hopes that some definite determined action by the proper authorities may eventually put a stop to the practice. At Liverpool such an offence would be promptly taken cognisance of,and the offender mulcted in the sum of £5. It is bad stoking, and nothing else, which causes steamers to emit black smoke, and if a few of the owners were prosecuted the evil would probably be con- siderably lessened in a very short time. Were the offence only committed in districts where there is a very scant population the nuisance would not be so great, but as a matter of fact the steamers seem to select residential neighbourhoodslikeLatchford and Stockton Heath for advertising their presence in such disagreeable fashion. Our complaint may, perhaps, result in some deterrent steps being taken. We sincerely hope so.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</p>
        <p>There was a moderate attendance at the " open "ser- vice on Sunday afternoon, and it was evident tnat other meetings and demonstrations in the town had tempted many away, who otherwise would have been present. rhe meeting however, was a very good and inspiring one Mr. S. B. Stringer who presided, briefly referred to the Divine aide of life, in its various stages and aspects. Mr Thomas Johnson, of Manches:-er, who has had many years experience of mission work in the slums of tha:', city, delivered an inspiring address on the duty of living a good and useful life. The speaker in the course of his address, related an incident in his own experience, in connection with a vis:t to South Africa, showing how far reaching is the influence for good on others ; the doing of a kind act to one in need. Mrs. R. H. Pearson added considerably to the BUCCES3 of the service by her excellent rendering of the solo" 0 Divine Redeemer." Mr. Pearson accompanied on the violin. The ttev. J. Yonge, Mr. B. Farrar, and Mr. T. Whetnall also took part in the service.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>St. Peter's Church.</head>
        <p>St. Peter's Church.</p>
        <p>Mr E C London, of Padgate, was the special speaker at the Men's Service, held in S. Peter's Church os Sunday afternoon, the subjeet of his address being " DPbt." Mr Thomas Reddish was the soloist, and he sang" Alone, yet not alone," and " Knocking," with good effect.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON. Warrington Y.M.C.A. Debating Society.</p>
        <p>The usual meeting was held at Friars' Green on Monday at 8.15 when Mr. W. J. Steward read a very interesting paper on " Force of Habit." The paper opened up a wide field for debate and was sup- ported bs, dr. Windle and Mr. Green and opposed by Mr. Fay and Mr. Garside. The usual votes of thanks to the c3ntributor of the paper and the Chairman, Mr. Bilsbrough, brought a very pleasant and profitable evening to a close. The subject for next Monday's debatA will ime introdue 3d by Mr. A. Jolley, viz.," Cremation." It should prove rather warm.</p>
        <p>USEFUL PRESENTS of every description, See Win- dow.--J. S. (31-..trewn Ironmonger. Bridge-st.—Advt•</p>
        <p>Loss of the Manchester Enterprise.</p>
        <p>Many of our readers who saw the large steamer "Manchester Enterprise" whilst in the Ship Canal, will be surprised to learn that a Baltimore telegram received on Tuesday, states that a cable received from Cape Henry reports the passing of the steamer "Lakonia" which vessel signalled "Manchester Enterprise foundered all bands saved." The "Man- chester Enterprise" which left Manchester early in November for Montreal was a steel steamer of 3,878 tons gross register. She was built at Dundee in 1890, and was owned by the Manchester Liners, Limited. The boat was one of the largest that ever negotiated the Manchester Ship Canal.</p>
        <p>KITCHHIC RANGNS,TiIe Grates,and Chimney Pieces. Large Stock.—J. S. GanLaNnironmonger,Bridge-st. Advt. Volunteer Ball.</p>
        <p>The annual ball arranged by the Non-Com- missioned officers of the local Volunteer Corps was held in the Drill Hall on Monday evening, under the patronage of Colonel Ridgway and other officers. The hall had been very tastefully decorated for the occasion. Union Jacks being hung from the roof. About 150 couples put in an appearance, dancing being kept up with spirit till an early hour next morning to music supplied by the Regimental Band under the conductorshin of Bandmaster Armstrong. Colonel S. Willcocks officiated as MC., and Colonel S. Wood was the assistant M.C. The catering, which gave every satisfaction, was in the hands of Mr. J. Standish of the" Big Horseshoe "Inn.</p>
        <p>"LAMPS, 011 Stoves, Coke Stoves, J. S. GARLAND, 51, 53, and 55, Bridge-street, Warrington. Advt .</p>
        <p>Latchford Improvement Society.</p>
        <p>At the last weekly meeting of this society, held in the Temperance Hall. Latchford, the topic for discussion was "The Tobacco Plant; its Uses and and Abuses." The opener of the debate spoke about the cultivation of tobacco, glided into ancient history, brought forward Sir Walter Raleigh, and by an easy jump had Charles Lamb, Thomas Carlyle, and Tennyson as authorities and votaries of the weed, and then impartially gave contrary samples. During the evening strong protests ware uttered by the anti-tobacconists,and quotations were read from James 1. and his " Counterblast to Tobacco "and other kindred. publications. Medical men were hopelessly divided on the question, and" if &amp;atom differ,haw shall we decide?" As a practical gardener one speaker gave some interesting facts connected with flowers and plants,and show' l how the poison of strong tobacco will soon when applied destroy them. In the course of the evening it was asserted positively that tobacco smoking was injurious to adults, and juveniles ought never to use it, and it was not a necessity. The discussion was voted in- structive, interesting, and entertaining.</p>
        <p>TO THE DEAF.—A gentleman who cured himself o Deafne.se and Noises in the Head rafter fourteen years safe,. ng will Ygiadlyt send full particulars of the remedy, on receip i stem ped addressed envelope.—F. CLlFrort, Amberle House, strand, London, W.C.—Advt.</p>
