      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>SOCILL AVTHE CIIIIRCII SCIIDOLS. The members of the Parish Church Young Men's Class held a social at the National Sch:)o,s, on Wedne3dav night, which was well attended. A programme of con- siderable diversity was provided, and included songs, dances, selections on the gramaphone, and numerous games. A capital supper was also provided.</p>
        <p>WESLEYAN FOREIGN MISSIONARY SOCIETY. In connection with the above mission two sermons will be preached at the St. Paul's Wesleyan Church,Run- . corn, on Sunday December 3rd, by the Rev. Marshall Hartley, of London. As Mr. Hartley is one of the general secretaries of the mission, and has acted as secretary of the Conference, he will be eminently fitted to deal with many interesting features of the missions. Major General Campbell, who was so well received when last at Runcorn, is again announced to speak, and doubtless will be accorded a hearty reception. PICKWICK LITERARY AND DEBATING SOCIETY. At a meeting of the above, held in the Guild Hall, on Tuesday evening last,a paper was read on"A.rbitration," by Mr. R. Bunion. The essayist was strongly in f 4vour ef an Arbitration Board being formed. He believed that with a mutual understanding of nations we might re- duce armaments and settle disputes by arbitration. He instanced the Venesuslan question as one settled satis- factorily by this means. A discussion took place the following members taking part :—Messrs. Booth, Doug- lass, Saunders, Hughes,Neild, Bridges and Faulkner. ' Mr W. Millington presided over a good attendance.</p>
        <p>BIRD SHOW AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>On Friday and Saturday the sixth annual exhibition of canaries, mules, British and foreign birds was held in connection with the Runcorn and Widnes British and Foreign Bird Association at the Co-operative Hall, Runcorn. The show was adjudged to be the best the association had yet got together, the exhibits of 17 competitors numbering no less than 103. The entries ex- ceeded those of the last show by about 100. A cup given by Messrs Lever Bros. for the highest points ob- tained throughout the show produced a strong competi- tion. Messrs. Ball (St. Eic_lens), Guy (Runcorn), and Cawley (Runcorn), all obtained 63 points, but as the first named had obtained the most prize money, the cup went to him. A number of special prizes were given by the tradesmen of Runcorn and Widnes. The judge was Mr. W P Barnes, of Chesterfield.</p>
        <p>MILTON P.S.A. WIDNES.</p>
        <p>There was beta lair attendance at the usual weekly meeting on Sunday, when Mr. Lacy (one of the vice- presidents) occupied the chair. Mr G Roberts, sang " The Bettn. Land," which was highly appreciated. and Messrs Roberts, Baggend;.7,e, Thomas, and Wilkinson, sang a quartette, " Rock of Ages," in effective style. The speaker,Rev M Hooper. of Frodsharn, gave a capital address on "This house to let," and compared the human body to a house, urging the men to take Christ as their tenant, and allow Him to take the house for a life-long lease, and thus secure the lease through eternity. The address was appropriately illustrated, and the points 'elucidated were highly appreciated. CAMDEN WESLEYAN CHAPEL.—SCHOOL SOCIAL. At the above annual social gathering there was a fair attendance gathered in the Camden Schoolroom, on Thursday night last week. Mr. A. Harrison presided, and during the evening the following programme was gone through :—Hymn, 500; duet, " Meadow where we played," Misses Weave and Littler ; reading, " Some- body's mother," Mrs.Dutton: mandoline duet, "Darkie's lament ; Miss L Temperley and Mr A Temperley ; song, " Blue eyed Minnie," Mis,s Gleave ; readieg, " Adven- tures of a young man," Mr W Littler ; song" Cathedral Voices," Mr J Hinds; mandoline quartette, Misses L Temperley, Heath, Burton, and Evans; recitation, Mr Gearge Blythe; song, "Flight ot age," Mr J Hinds. The evening proved highly enjoyable and was very success- ful.</p>
        <p>DETACHMENT OF WIDNES RESERVISTS.</p>
        <p>Considerable excitement prevailed in Widnes during Monday morning, caused by the departure of about 33 reserves, who left the town to join their regiments, the Lancashire Fusiliers at Bury, the Bth and 40th at War- rington. and the Royal Irish Fusiliers at Southampton. From 8.30 to 10.30 Victoria-road, between the two stations, was crowded with people assembled to bid the men "God cpeed." By far the larger number proceeded by the 12,55 London and North-Western train, and for a time the precincts of the station were well nigh impass- able. Only a limited few were allowed on the platform, but even here the enthusiasm was great. As the con- ductor impatiently waved his flag to be off last hand shakes and the parting ki5535 were given,and as the train slowly steamed out of the station a ringing cheer was raised, which was taken up by the crowd outside. The men bore the parting with considerable fortitude, and re-echoed the cheers heartily.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>RELIEF FUND.</head>
        <p>THE RELIEF FUND.</p>
        <p>Last Friday evening a successful entertainment was given in the Victoria-road Board Schools, Runcorn, in aid of the local fund for the relief of the wives and families of the soldiers who are engaged in the Transvaal War. The proceedings took the form of an exhibition of limelight views, illustrating a lecture on " Men who face death, or Britain's soldier heroes." Mr. J. F. Moul- ton, the head master, presided, and the lecture was given by Mr. J. C.Cragg, assistant master at the school. The lecturer dealt with the principal events in the Crimean War, the Indian Mutiny, the Maori War, Zulu War, Afghan War, and Soudan War, the most striking and thrilling incidents being graphically depic- ted by a series of excellent pictures on the screen. At intervals loyal and patriotic songs were effectively sung by the scholars, the musical programme including "Soldiers of the Queen,"" Rule Britannia," and the National Anthem. Mr. J. Brown recited" The absent- minded beggar," and also sang" The powder monkey," which was also illustrated by a series of views. After the lecture an amusing sketch entitled "How Bill Adams won the battle of Waterloo" was given. The entertain- ment was received with great favour, and numerous re- quests made for its repetition. As a result of the proceedings some £5 or a will be handed over to the fund.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN HARRIERS DEFEATED.</p>
        <p>The Runcorn Harriers entertained Halton View Harriers at Runcorn on Saturday. This being the first inter-run of the season at Runcorn great interest was taken in the affair. There was a good muster of runners on both sides, although Runcorn were without three of their prominent men. Trail was laid ever about 8 miles of good country by Messrs. E. Faulkner. S Shallcross, and J W Ha milton, in the direction of Rocksavage, Sut- ton Weaver, and back to Halton, where a mile break was formed. This was the chief feature of the run. Halton View have not yet been defeated, having gained a great reputation for mile breaks. The men lined up at the top of Halton-brow to race home through a dense fog. The finish was an exciting one, being closely con- tested between Saudlands and Lightfoot of the visitors, and Hopkins and Ray of the home team, which resulted in favour of the home men. The following were the positions :—Runcorn-1, Hopkins; 2, Ray; 7, Shatlcrosa; 10, Clarke; 12. Davies ; 23, Cadman; total, 45. Halton View-3, Sandlands ;4, Lightfoot ;5, Linaker ;6, Tern perley ; 8, Ince ; 9, Knight; total, 35 ; being a win for the visitors by 10 points.</p>
        <p>NATIONAL UNION OF TEACHERS</p>
        <p>The quarterly general meeting of the Runcorn, Widnes. and District Association was held in the Victoria-road Schools, Runcorn, on Saturday. Mr. Joseph Brown (vice-president) occupied the chs.ir.—The "Scheme for Rsconstruction of Conference Representa- tion" was discussed, and it was unanimously decided not to adopt the scheme recornmonied by the N.U.T. Executive. The Manch ,ster scheme was then placed before the meeting, and was unanimously adopted.—ln connection with the question of the" size of school departments" the following resolution was submitted to the meeting :—" That, in the opinion of this meeting, on purely educational grounds no department of a school should in future be erected with, or enlarged to, a capacity exceeding 400 children." Those taking part in the discussion were divided as to whether 300 or 400 should be the number. Finally, however, the resolution as submitted was carried by the narrow majority of one vote.—Mr. Allen Croft was nominated as vice-president of the N.U.T„ and Dr. Beach and Mr. Croft as members of the Executiv 3.—T wo 'new members were proposed and accepted.</p>
        <p>UNLAWFUL WOUNDING AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>At the Widnes Police Court on Monday, before the Mayor (Alderman G. I. Neil), in the chair, and Alderman J. W. Waring. .Annie Jones, a well-known character, was charged on remand with unlawfully wounding Michael Burke.—Complainant who lived at 22, Water- street, had known the prisoner for several years. About a fortnight ago prisoner was allowed to sleep at his house because she had no where else to go to. In con- sequence of her subsequent bad conduct Burke had to Put her out of the house on the 4th November, On that oceasion she smashed several panes of glass in the win- dow. On the 11th November about 830 p.m., when witness got home prisoner was in the kitchen, and he ordered her out. She refused to go and said that he was not the " bons." Witness said he would go for a policeman, and then went to look for one. After- wards complainant was standing at the door smoking. Prisoner came through the kitchen with two bottles, and with the one in her right hand, she struck witness over the right eye causing him to bleed. Prisoner then struck at him again, but he raised his arm and caught the blow on it. Witness went to Dr. Hutchinson's sur- gery and got his wound dressed. Complainant had given no provocation to the prsoner.—Margaret Ann Burke, wife to complamant, gave evidence of a corroborative character.—Dr. Hutchinson stated that he attended ta complainant on the 11th inst. There was a cut about two inches long over the right eye. It might have been caused by a bottle.— Theresa Jane Burke, aged 12,stated that prisoner had told her grandmother, on the date named, that she would give her father. a slight blow when be was not aware.—P.S. Ratchffa deposed to apprehending prisoner.—ln reply to the charge prisoner said she did hit him with the bottle. She made no corn- plaints about Burke. —The Bench said there was a prima facie case and committed prisoner to the Liverpool Quarter Sessions.</p>
        <p>THE RUNCORN BRITISH WOMEN'S TEMPERANCE ASSOCIATION. This society gave evidence of its activity by the hold- ing of a most successful gathering at the Brunswick Schools, on Thursday afternoon. There was a good attendance of members,and considerable work was done of the nature covered by the society. A vigorous campaign against the drink curse in the neighbourhood was strongly advocated and decided upon. BRUNSWICK MUTUAL IMPROVEMENT CLASS. Mr. T. Sirhister presided at the weekly meeting of the above held on Wednesday night. The subject was "Should Christians smoke ?" Mr. El Dodd took the affirmative and thought it quite consistent for Christians if so inclined to indulge in the soothing weed. Mr. J J Pickthall who took the negative said the habit of amok- ing was filthy, dirty and objectionable and a great number of diseases could be traced to the intemperate use of tobacco. It was not only a moral but a physical evil. An animated and well sustained discussion fol- owed supported by the members of the class. CONCERT AT THE SEAMEN'S INSTITUTE, STATION-ROAD. The weekly concert at the above place was given last Tuesday by the Oakland-street concert party, from Widnes. A capital programme of songs, recitations ,and humorous dialog .es was submitted, and duly appreci- ated by a large audience. The chair was occupied by Mr. J. H. Spiby, who at the cernmeecernent expressed pleasure at attending and spoke highly of the efforts to amuse and ele sate the seamen within the port on win- ter nights. A hearty vote of thanks was accorded the Chairman and the artistes on the proposition of Mr W. Shaw (missioner), seconded by Mr. W. By field. The Chairman responded on behalf of himself and party and expressed the pleasure they would all feel at coming over the water again if asked. The singing of the "National Anthem" concluded a pleasant entertainment. DEATH AND FUNERAL OF MR. HENRY WHITE. Last Monday night, Mr. Henry White, was happily released by death from a life of long and terrible suffer- ing, and on Thursday afternoon his mortal remains were conveyed to their last resting place at Runcorn Ceme- tery amid manifestations of grief from his sorrowing relations, and friends. But a short twelve montbe ago Mr. White followed the remains of his beloved wife, to that same sacred spot where now he himself lies. Mr. 'White was the son of the late Mr. Thomas White,builder and contractor, of Runcorn, and succeeded him in his business, during which time he carried out many exten- sive building operations that remain behind him as samples of his skill in his chosen employment. He it was who built the Trinity Wesleyan Church, Frodsharn, and the Trinity Wesleyan Chapel at Halton, and amongst one of his greatest local pieces of architecture there ranks the extensive alterations to Parr'e Bank,Runcarn. As a public man,Mr. H White was highly esteemed and occupied a seat on the Board of Commissioners for many years. In 1892, however, owing to failing health he retired from that body and never again sought to serve his townspeople in that capacity. He suffered greatly from cancer in the neck and though operated upon time after time seldom received anything but temporary relief. He died as stared, and was buried on Thursday be' the Rev. Canon Wood. Amongst his other public offices Mr- White acted as a capable and efficient church officer. He leaves a daughter and son with whom general sympathy is felt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>This Council met at the Workhouse, Dutton, f3r its usual fortnightly meeting, on Tuesday, when there were present :—Messrs. H Woodward (chairman), J Mclntyre, Admiral Jones-Parry, H Talbott, J B Chad- wick, T Wright, I' Lewis, J Moseley Williams, E Clarke, J Edwards, E Gleave, E Fletcher, J G Sydd, P Leather, J Baguley, :with G F Ashton (clerk), W E Hough (deputy clerk), W Diggle surveyor), J Farrington (inspector). FRODSHAII AND THE TOWN'S POLICE CLAUSES ACT. Mr. Dansie said first of all he wished to congratulate his friend, Mr. George F. Ashton, upon his appointment as clerk of that Couucil, and only hoped he was follow- ing in the footsteps of his late father from whom he (Mr. Dansie) had always received the greatest possible assist- ance. (Hear, hear.) With regard to the application of the Frodsham township asking powers under the Town's Police Clauses Act, 1847, Section 28, he pointed out that the whole of the provisions of the Section were quite inapplicable at Frodsham, on account of its semi-rural nature. It was quite unusual for the Local Government Board to grant the application to such places without the bolding of an inquiry. As the Local Government Board would, however, most likely grant the adoption of portions of the clauses within the section, he sugges- ted that the matter be referred back to the township, so that they could reconsider what would be best to apply for. Further, he thought, the Clerk agreed with him, that the local committee of the Frodsham Township did not exactly understand what they required. Mr. J. Edwards moved that the -matter be referred back to the local committee for reconsideration. Mr. Mclntyre seconded Mr. J. Edwards, and the matter was referred back.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>DR. ADAUS BUSY.</head>
        <p>DR. ADAUS BUSY.</p>
        <p>Dr. Adams, medical officer of health, telegraphed from Warrington as follows :—" Over 400 reservists for ex- amination to-day. Sorry cannot be present at Dutton." Several members expressed disapproval of Dr. Adams' peremptory way of dismissing the Council.and a strong expression was made that the Medical Officer should either attend by deputy or in a written report when so:busylae to be unable t• attend personally. On the motion of Mr. J. Edwardsaseconded by Mr. E. Clarke, a resolution "That the absence of a report from the Medical Officer was most unsatisfactory " was un- animously passed. A WARNING FROM THE LOCAL GOVERNMENT BOARD. Mr. H. C. Munro. assistant secretary Local Govern- ment Board wrote forwarding the formal sanction of the Board to the borrowing by the Rural District Council of the sum of £2lO for works of sewerage for the township of Weston. Be was also directed to point out that, if, as in the present case, the District Council commenced works which they intended to pay for out of borrowed money, without first obtaining the Local Government Board's sanction to the loan, they did so at the risk of having to defray the expenditure incurred out of cur- rent revenue, if for any reason the Board should be un- able to give the required sanction.</p>
        <p>ANALYSES OE WATER.</p>
        <p>The Local Government Bpard,in reply to a letter from the Council on the 4th bast wrote: "To state that it is not their practice to obtain analyses of samples of water for local authorities." Mr. Thomas Wright proposed. that Mr. Alfred Smetham, analyst to the Royal North Lancashire Agri- cultural Society, (Liverpool), be employed to make the In .IyEl Mr. Moseley Williams seconded and the motion was agreed to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>COMPLAINTS FROM HATTON..</head>
        <p>COMPLAINTS FROM HATTON..</p>
        <p>Mr. R. Hewii-t7ClJii. to the Elatton Parish Council wrote forwarding the following resolution passed on the 31st October : "That the Clerk be instructed to write to the District Council, with regard to the trees which overhang the hiehway between Hatton and Walton, and also with regard to a deep and dangerous ditch which is under these trees." In a second letter Mr. Hewitt stated that since the Parish Council meeting a traction engine got fast in the ditch through the side of the road giving way and another traction engine had to be got to ex- tricate it. The Surveyor (Mr. Swinton) wrote asking the Council to defer the consideration of the question of trees until he was able to be present.</p>
        <p>This course was adopted. SEWERAGE WORKS AT WESTON. Mr. W. Joynson wrote stating that Mr. Bennie had almost completed his contract for the extension of the sewer at Weston Point. Mr. Warsley was constructing the requisite drains, St 3. in connection with the 25 houses he is building at Weston Point. If agreeable to the Council he would undertake to supervise the same, and also to see that the drains from Mr. Collins's pro- perty were properly connected, for £6. Mr. Joynson was duly appointed.</p>
        <p>LIGHTING OF THE SHIP CANAL BRIDGEg.</p>
        <p>Mr. F. Taylor, secretary to the Warrington Corpora- tion gas department wrote with re7erence to the lamp on the Ship Canal Bridge, stating that the three lamps were re-lighted last night (9th inst). As regards the other new ones they met Mr. Pilling by appointment. If they found any difficulty concerning the removal of the lamps at Walton Inferior they would write further in a few days.</p>
        <p>THE RIVERS POLLITTION.</p>
        <p>Mr. Reginald Potts, c'erk to the County Council.wrote as follows under date 10th November :—" The Rivers Pollution Committee of this Council at its last meeting further considered what action, if any, should be taken with reference to the pollution of streams, etc., within your rural district. The committee decided to further Postpone the !natter until its next quarterly meeting and instructed the County Medical Officer of Health to report to such meeting upon the proceedings which are being taken by your District Council to prevent such pollu- tion. Mr. J. Farrington submitted further analvses of effluents all of a satisfactory nature,the results of which, when the whole are complete, will, in due course be forwarded to the County Council.</p>
        <p>NEW POSTAL FACILITIES FOR FRODSIIAM.</p>
        <p>Mr. Moseley Williams -intimated that he had been in comtrunication with the postal authorities respecting the erection of a pillar or wall box at Church-steps, Frodsham, and that the Post-master had finally decided to fix such a box for the public convenience if the sanction of the District Council could be obtained. He proposed that such sanction be given. Mr. Edwards seconded. . _ -</p>
        <p>Mr. Diggle (surveyor) said the b)x would be out of the way, and would be a great boon to the neighbourhood. The necessary permission was granted. THE MOORE FEVER HOSPITAL. .</p>
        <p>Mr. Gleave reported the roof of the Fever Heapital at Moore, to be in a very delapidated condition, and it was decided that the Hospital Committee be empowered to carry out the necessary repairs, the cost of which were not to exceed £lO.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>EXAMINER. FRIDAY, NOTEMBER 24, 1899.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER. FRIDAY, NOTEMBER 24, 1899.</p>
        <p>do any balancing. (Renewed laughter.) The man and woman were having words, but steps were taken to put them out of the place as soon as possible. He was the man called " uncle." _ . .</p>
        <p>After a brief retirement, the Chairman said the Bench had no doubt, the man was drunk, and would impose a fine of 20s. and costs £5 us. Th,, license would not be endorsed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>LICENSING CASE AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>LICENSING CASE AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Publican Fined for Per- mitting Drunkenness.</p>
        <p>At the Runcorn Police Court, on Monday, before Messrs, Henry Reynolds (chairman),and A. Tbomas, Mary Ellen Ind,licensea of the Royal Oak, Runcorn, was summoned for permitting drunkenness, on</p>
        <p>_ licensed premises, on the 7th of November last.— Mr. Fletcher (Northwich),prosecuted for the police; and Mr. F. A. Lake,solicitor (Runcorn), defended. Mr. Fletcher, in his opening statement, said the case arose out of one in which.a strong man (Oscar Wilhelm Hildgenfelt) was fined by the Runcorn Bench for drunkenness on the of Bth November. It would appear that Hildgenfelt got drunk on the premises of the Royal Hotel, and whilst upon the premises conducted himself in such a manner as to be doing his best not only to make himself drunk but also by treating other people to drinks,—for which—by the way, he refused to pay—he was do- ing his level best to make other people drunk also. A woman, supposed to be his wife, but on that head there was a diversity of opinion, was also assaulted by him. Finally the police were called in and the met was locked up. During the time he was confined in the cells he conducted himself like a "mad-drunk" person and caused the police con- siderable annoyance. The following morning when brought up before Mr. G. L. Davies he pleaded guilty to a charge of drunkenness and was fined. Those were briefly the facts, which if proved he took it would establish his case. He contended—as he had often done before in that court—that hotels were established as places where people could obtain reasonable refreshment to satisfy their needs, and, if desirable, their pleasure to a certain extent; but it was clearly not contemplated by the law that a man should be allowed to sit in a public-house making himself drunk and assisting to make other people drunk, whilst all the time the people re- sponsible for the conduct of the house must have known what was going on.—A number of witnesses were then called in support of the prosecn- , tion</p>
        <p>Etifiabeth Saunders, residing with her parents in Nel- son-street, statel os3ar Hildgenfelt came to lodge at their house from the 3rd to the 91h of November. On Tuesday (7ch November) the man left the house at 12 men, and did not return that day. She subsequently law him in the bar parlour of the Royal Hotel where he was drunk. He was supplied with a glass of beer. Hildgenfelt'a wife went to try and get him home to have something to eat, but he would not go home and struck the woman. There was a great disturbance in the house, and the police came and Hildgenfelt was finally locked up.—By Mr. Lake: Witness had had ne experi- ence with drunken people ; that was her first case. When the row was going on Mrs. Ind tried to prevent it, and efforts were made to get the people out of the house. _ _ _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>Thomas Shingler, labourer, 48, Bridge-street, Runcorn said he was in the Billiard-room of the Royal Hotel when the strong man came in. He was under the in- fluence of drink. Hildgenfelt a,ked them to have drinks, end immediately left. He saw the mai and woman assaulting each other about 9 eelock. About 29 minutes to ten there was a ;neat disturbance in the pas- sage, Hildgenfelt and his wifee were quarrelling and row- ing. The man was drunk, and.seemed to have no sense about him or he would not have acted OA he did.—By Mr. Lake: Mrs. Ind did her best to separate the man and woman. He judged the man to be drunk from his behaviour. _ _</p>
        <p>Frank Shingler. Bridge-street, deposed to hearing a row and calling Mr. Id's attention to it. Later about 10 o'clock there was another row, and that time he noticed the Supposed strong man" was in his (wit- ness's) opinion " mad-drunk." Constable Gregory, testified that about quarter to 10 he went to the Royal Hotel and found a number of people were trying to put Hildgenfelt out of the house. The man was "mad drunk," and very violent. The man smelt very strongly of spirits. After Hildgenfelt was Locked up he kept up a tremendous noise in the cells. The next morning ha was brought up before Mr. G. L. Davies, and fined 103 and costs, after pleading guilty to the charge of drunkenness.—Acting-Sergeant White visited the Royal Hotel in clnsequence of what he heard and assisted Constable Gregory to lock him up He went twice subsequently to the Royal Hotel and told the licensee on the last visit- that she would be proceeded against. She asked was he (Hildegnfelt) drunk and he replied " yes."—William Robert,Davies,manager for Bradley's Bridge-street, said about 9 30 he went to the Royal Hotel. There was a man commonly called "uncle "present who paid for a small rum and soda for Hildgenfelt. When he got the rum he wanted all present to drink with him, and the cost of the drinks came to is. 10d. The barmaid asked him to pay for them and 'he said he had paid. Another person present said" we have had enough of that,' and offered to pay for the , drinks. The-man " uncle," however, said he would pay half. The wife of Hildgenfelt had warned the people present that her husband weuld not pay for drinks after ordering them.—By Mr Lake : It•would be Impossible for a drunken MRS te perform the tricks Hildgenfelt was doing,as he was balancing upon an ordinary office stool. The man was net drunk •, but had had enough. Later the man was thrashing his wife in a dark room, and they all went to the rescue. Witness sent for the police. When Constable Gregory came the man was ia a passage near the doorway. Witness and others assis'ed at lock- ing the strong man up. Thomas Dillon, Aleock-str Pet, and Thomas McDonald gave further eVidence. Sergeant Brewster said he found Acting-Sergeant Whits and Constable Gregory in the cells trying to pacify a prisoner they had lust brought in. He was "mad drunk " and his breath smelt of rum. He (witness) was obliged to go into the cell with a constable and threaten to strap him down to the bad before Hildgenfelt became quiet.</p>
        <p>For the defence Mr. F. A. Lake submitte4 it was not sufficient for the prosecution to make out that the man wls drunk, but they should also prove—and he sub- mitted they had not done so—that he was so palpably drunk that defendant was cognizant of the fact, and knowingly permitted it. Mary Ellen Ind, defendant, said she was licensee of the Royal Hotel. She first saw Hildgenfelt about 8 o'clock at night. He went looking into all the rooms of the Hotel on Tuesday, and told her he was looking for his wife. The man was then sober. Later Mrs Hildgen- felt entered the house, with the girl Saunders. Witness then went out, and upon returning heard a scream which she thought was in the street, but found after- wards it was from the woman Hildgenfelt. The only drink that Hildgenfelt get was one glass of beer, but he might have bad a rum. The man appeared _ . _</p>
        <p>to be" mad" and wanted to go and open th'e-show. When the row commenced witness took Mrs Hildgenfelt out of the dark room and put her in a small room. She heard the woman aggravating the man saying "he was no husband of hers," and that she had taken him out of the gutter and kept and worked for him for eleven months." James Ind, husband of the licensee, said he was as- sisting in the house. The man Hildgenfelt came in about one o'clock on the first occasion and against little later. He was then sober. After 8 o'clock he saw the man a third time. He went into the billiard room and said he wanted to see" Uncle" to ask him if he had seen his" missus." He then went out to look for her. About nine o'clock witness's attention was called to a scuffle in the lobby but by the time he went there it was all over. The man had had his face scratched by his wife. He ordered the barmaid to turn the woman out, and not let her come in again, Hildgenfelt went out. He did not want any asking. Later the two women came back, and he told them to go out as they knew what a temper Hildgenfelt was in, and that he would " give it them." The strong man and his wife then commenced rowing. He (witness) went to them at once. and tried to induce the man to leave the house. Wit- ness was desirous Hildgenfelt should leave the house be- cause the woman was sheutinF,P murder and creating a disturbance.—By Mr. Fletcher: He took part in the management of the house. It was not true that the man was drinking rum for he was not nerved with rum at all—By Mr. Lake, He attributed the man's condi- tion to excitement consequent upon the aggravation the woman had given him. Elizabeth Smith, niece of Mrs. Ind, and assistant in the house and bar, said Hildgenfelt came and had a mild beer. Later she served him with another. After 8 o'clock she saw the man again ;he was all right. She supplied him with drink when Mr. Davies was present. It was a soda-water. The man was not drunk,—By Mr. Fletcher : She did not supply him with rum. She did not know what drink the man might have had be- sides but he had four drinks from her two mild-beers, and two codas. She was positive that the man was sober. She did not know that the woman had come to ask her husband to go home. Elizabeth Saunders deresed to the people living with her, and, to their beieg quiet. The day before (Mon- day) she knew the man had been drinking, and had no meat.—By Mr. Lake: He fed out of the house on Mon- day.—Mr. Lake: Just se ; a strong man cannot live on nothing. William Cumbleholme, Union-street, fitter, stated he went into the Royal Hotel about 8 o'clock. The man was not drunk, but was a bit excited about a weman who kept tantalising him.—John Florsefield, moulder, in the employ of the Shin Canal Company, stated he lived at the Royal Hotel. With regard to sobriety the man seemed ell right, and calmed down considerable. They had drinks together, and Hildgenfelt had a soda water. The man began showing off his "feats," and sat crass-legged on a stool, which caused some laughter.—By Mr. Fletcher : Witness prescribed soda water because he felt it was better for the man than other drinks. Witness considered the acts</p>
        <p>of the man were consistent with thcre of a sober man. He was ordering drinks but did not pay. (Laughter.) His own drink would be soda water if he was going to</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF</p>
        <p>GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The usual fortnightly meeting of the above Board was held at the Workhouse, Ditton, on Tuesday. There were present :—Messrs J Moseley Williams (chairman),Samuel Taylor, J.P., A R Norman, J.P., T Lea, Thomas Rey- nolds, R Wright, Dr Gastaldi, the Rural District Coun- cillors, with the clerk, Mr G F Ashton ; deputy clerk, Mr W E Hough ;, master, Mr James Cutmah ; and Mr R T Dansie (poor law inspector).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>DEITEI OF MR. tOHN WEIR.--.A RESPECTFUL TRIBUTE!</head>
        <p>DEITEI OF MR. tOHN WEIR.--.A RESPECTFUL TRIBUTE!</p>
        <p>The Chairman (Mr. J. Moseley Williams) referred with regret to the notice they had all received that morning from the Clerk announcing the death of their esteemed friend and fellow guardian, Mr. John Weir, which took place the previous Saturday. He thought, he was right In saying, that Mr. Weir was the oldest member of the Board of Guardians. Mr. Weir had been amongst them as a character they admired in many respects. Though he (the Chairman) did not at all times see with Mr. Weir, he had always held the greatest possible respect for him. He was sorry that it had been of late his duty to so often have to refer to some sad bereavement, but they were things that came as a natural consequence; and if they only went away from their occupation carrying with them the respect and esteem that Mr. Weir carried from. that Board of Guardians, it would be a great consolation to their friends and relatives. He moved that the Clerk be in- structed to forward a letter of condolence to the members of Mr. Weir's family expressive of the kindly sentiments they entertained for him. Mr. S. Woodward seconded. He perfectly agreed with all that had been said of the character, usefulness, and general popularity of their old friend Mr. John Weir. He was not one of the quickest moving men,but he was steady, safe and thorough—(hear, hear)—and he was one of those members of the Board whom they very highly respected. He seconded the proposition as Chair- man of the District Council.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Mclntyre said be should like to support the resolution, as he had worked in conjunction with Mr. Weir for so Yong. He was not very sure whether he (himself) or Mr. Weir was " father of the Board," but rather thought they had been associated with it for a like period. Mr. Weir had devoted the greater portion of his life to the duty of Or, Union, and had worked assiduously and moderately. He was a man on whose opinion they could always rely, and one of amiable temper, devotion to duty and kindlioes.. (Hear, hear.) Mr. Thomas Wright wished to support the remarks of the Chairman, Mr. Woodward and Mr. Mclntyre. He had known Mr. Weir for a period of about 40 years, and his acquaintance had givan him very great pleasure. Mr Weir was a man very well thought of by his land- lords, his neighbours, and the members of that Board of Guardians.</p>
        <p>The proposition was carried unanimously. TEE COUNTY AND POLICE RATES.</p>
        <p>Mr, Reginald Potts, clerk of the County Council, wrote informing the Board thit the County rate at 2/1 in the £ ; general police rate 3-161 in thee ; and tun local police rates for the several divisions in the county had been confirmed and adopted on a new basis. The rate for the County and General Police was, however, the same a 4 last year. The local !police rate for Runcorn was id in tht, £ as compared with /I last year.</p>
        <p>TILE VACCINATION QUESTION.</p>
        <p>Mr. Joseph Pym, clerk to the Belper Union,forwarded a report and printed statement re vaccination. He had sent to upwards ot 268 Unions asking for particulars and received replies from 164 of that number, 49 paid the minimum fee and less than Belper's 84 ; 4 paid the same as Belper's (8s); 91 paid more, and 20 paid less. In re- ply to the query "Has there been any Lnaterial increase in vaccinations performed since the alteration of fees ?" 100 Unions replied "material increase;33 "no increase," and 11 "slight increase." In 133 replies the word "Yes" had been returned that the recent vaccinations had worked satisfactory. 11. added "costs greatly increased." Altogether there were 51 methods of payment in 164 Unions.</p>
        <p>The Chairman considered it was a matter that would have to come before them again before the end of the year. All over the country the Beards of Guardians were greatly reducing their rates of vaccination, and he sug- gested, therefor?, that the subject be referred to the Parliamentary Committee. This was agreed to. AN INDIGNANT PARENT AND THE DOCTOR.</p>
        <p>Mr. Charles W. Couch, Grove Bank, Helsby, wrote as follows :—" Dr. Burton, of Frodshatn, called at my address on the 10th hist, in my absence and vaccinated my boy, having persuaded my wife thereto, notwith- standing he was informed Dr. Bryant of Helsby, had the matter in hand. The District Registrar, Mr. Riley, had called previously and was referred to Dr. Bryant. I feel much annoyed with this procedure and trust you will bring the matter before your Board at its next meet- ing."</p>
        <p>The Clerk stated that he had forwarded a copy of</p>
        <p>the letter to Dr. Burton, who in reply wrote as folle-ws : —" In reply to Mr. Couche' s letter I called at Grove Bank, Helsby, to vaccinate the child, Charles Marcus Couche, and Mrs. Couche referred me to Dr. Bryant. I replied that I had nothing to do with Dr. Bryant and did she want me to vaccinate the child or not. Mrs. Ceuche said as you are now here you may as well do it! No persuasion was ased, and I was not in the house fiv4o minutes." The Chairman asked whether the Board would like any action taken in the matter. Several Guardians : No. , The Chairman, thought Mr. Couche should be replied to.</p>
        <p>ILLNESS OF MR. T. DARLINGTON.</p>
        <p>Mr. T. Darlington wrote acknowledging the sympathy of the Beard and Council as expressed at their last meeting. Their kind letter had touched him very much, and be was pleased to convey to the members his heart- felt thanks.</p>
        <p>FINANCES.</p>
        <p>Mr. Woodward presented the financial statement. Outrelief was paid as follows ;—Runcorn. £B5; Bud- worth, £23 ; and Frodsham, £2l. The Treasurer's re- ceipts since last meeting amounted to £1,120.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>CHRISTMAS AT THE WORKHOUSE.</head>
        <p>CHRISTMAS AT THE WORKHOUSE.</p>
        <p>It was decided to afford the inmates the usual trea' on Christmas Day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>ADMIRAL JONES-PARRY AND THE LOCAL GOTER/•TMENT</head>
        <p>ADMIRAL JONES-PARRY AND THE LOCAL GOTER/•TMENT</p>
        <p>B 111. RD INSPECTOR .</p>
        <p>Mr. R. I. Dansie (poor law inspector) intimated that he bad been through the hospital, and found it on the whole in very good order. Yet there were one or two things he would suggest with a view to improvement. He noticed that the beds were of straw, and as there were a good many bed-ridden pemple unable to get up, it might be that straw was not the most comfortable thing to lie upon. Sometimes it slipped down the sides and from under the body, and occasionally it was likely enough the people laid upon the iron supports. He suggested that the House Committee would consider the matter with a view of substituting some of the straw beds with flocks. He would also ask that the nurses gave a little more supervision over the " lockers "in the sick wards, and prevent the introduction of forbidden articles. He further suggested that the House Com- mittee consider the advisability of having the children of the House medically inspected about every 2 months, and also suggested that the children's teeth should be periodically inspected by a compstent dentist. Admiral Jones-Parry said he happened to be Chairman of the House Committee which met that morning- He had asked the Inspector if he would like to make any remarks, but he declined. He (Admiral Parry) wished ta say that he thought two-thirds of the remarks which had just been made would have been better made to the House Committee.</p>
        <p>The Inspector: lam much ebligel to you, sir.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>WESTON.</head>
        <p>WESTON.</p>
        <p>THE RELIEF OF THE SOLDIERS' DEPENDENTS.-A mos successful meeting was held at the Church Schools, Weston. on Tuesday night, for the purpose of raising a fund for the relief of the soldiers' relatives affected through their "bread winners "being called out to figl,t in South Africa. The proceedings were presided over by the Rev. Canon M. Wood, M.A., and there were also present:—Revs D S Bowen, W H Stables,Mesers R Cook- son, A G Guest, Samuel Basnett, James Collier, G H Garner, the band of the Runcorn Detachment of the 2nd Earl. of Chester Volunteers, under bandmaster T Outh- waite, and a large number of the general public.—The proceedings opened with "Soldiers of the Queen." played by the band.—A capable address followed from the Chairman, explaining the objects of the meeting.—Mr. AG. Guest proposed "That considering the dangers incurred, and the hardships endured by our brave soldiers, in fighting our battles in South Africa, it is our plain duty to assist in providing for the wounded ; helping to 'relieve the anxieties of the wives and children and to care for the widows and orphans."—Mr Garner seconded, and the resolution was carried with enthusiasre.—The Rev. D. S. Bowen moved " that a committee consisting of the Parish Ceuncil in the township of Weston (including Weston Point), with rower to add to their number, be formed to raise a fund to co-operate with the Runcorn' C,ommittee, by contributing to the Soldiers' and Sailors Families Association ; the:Patriotic Fund Committee ; the British Red Cross Society; and like South African relief funds which should seem best ; and to see that no local case was neglected."—Thie was seconded by the Rev. W. H. Stables, and agreed to with acclamation.—lt was announced by the Chairman that Mr. L. Rosenburgh, of Weston Point, had written regretting hie inability to attend, and intimated that the employes of the Weaver Trust, at Weston Point Docks had made their first collection on Saturday last, and deposited the amount in Parr's Bank. Mr. Rosenburgh also enclosed a cheque for 103. 6L</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>RUNCORN NOTES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN NOTES.</p>
        <p>This week there has been a slight disagreement between employer and employed at the Castner Kellner Works, Weston Point, but it is pleasing to ' see the attitude of the men in the matter. Though asked to accept what at first to them appeared unequitable,they with a little demurrence have accepted the masters' terms for a month's trial, at the end of which time a conference will take place for the purpose of settling the dispute amicably. In the event of the grievance of the " employed " being a substantial one, and—if what the men say concerning the loss in health they sometimes suffer, is true—it is to be hoped the " masters " will treat the men considerately and even generously.</p>
        <p>Meetings in support of the fund for the relief of the distressed and afflicted relatives of our brave red-coats have been held in Weston, Frodsham, Runcorn, and several other adjacent places all with very satisfactory results. The speeches at each have always been identical and in all " Kipling and his poem "have figured prominently. That war news is scarce, is a general complaint at Runcorn, and here, as in many other places, there reside in- dividuals w'ao consider themselves far more capa- ble to occupy the war correspondent's post than he himself ever ha i any pretentious to,</p>
        <p>Notwithstanding all the fuss and boasted patriot- ism of Runcorn in the matter of " doing its duty by its soldiers "it is a lamentable fact, that what few reserve men have left the town for their several depots, have done so as it were by the back door; and not a single instance of a " send-off " is to band. This is truly characteristic of Runcorn's esprit de corps. To cheer anything but a football victory is evidently a crime in the eyes of the Run- corn public.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>FOG.</head>
        <p>THE FOG.</p>
        <p>Railway Collision at Widnes.</p>
        <p>Brakesman Seriously Injured.</p>
        <p>-On Saturday afternoon during the fog a passenger train collided with a goods train just outside Widnes London and North-Western Station. The force of the impart caused considerable damage to the goods waggons and van, the truck next to the van being smashed to splinters. The brakesraan was found lying insensible, suffering from serious injuries which necessi- tated his removal to the Widnes Hospital. The people in the passenger train miraculously escaped, a fact which is to be attributed to the train having slackened down speed preparatory to pulling up in the station, and also to the goods wagons being empty. STATEMENT BY A PASSENGER.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. H. Richardson. chief clerk at Runcorn Goods Station, in the course of an interview, stated that he left Runcorn by the 3.2 train. It was rather foggy. When he got to Ditton Station a long train of empty coal waggons from Garston passed, and preceded the passenger train in the direction of Widnes. When the latter was within 200 yards of Widnes Station there was a very loud report, as if it might have come from the mouth of a cannon, and suddenly Mr. Richardson, who was sitting in a compartment by himself, was thrown violently forward, his head coming into sharp contact with Coe end of the carriage. For the moment he was rendered insensible, and when be recovered he heard screaming from the women and children in the train. On laoking out of the carriage window towards the engine he saw what appeared in the distance to be tlie signal box, but alighting from the train he found it was the brake van of the coat train. The waggon nearest the van bad been smashed to splinters, and the Wheela were lying on the permanent way. The brake van had been forced on the top of the second waggon. Mr. Richardson hastened to the assistance of the passengers, and was very much relieved b find that beyond the shock consequent upon the collision they were, with one exception, none the worse for the accident. The person referred to was a middle-aged lady who had to be assisted to the railway station where Mr. McConnell, the station- master' promptly obtained the necessary medical assist- ance. The brakesman of the goods train was found lying unconscious in his van, with an ugly gash on his forehead, his cheek badly cut, and his nose apparently broken and bleeding profusely. The ambulance was telephoned for, and the unfortunate man (whose name is cyPollard, and lives at Garston), was taken to 'he Widnes Hospital. His collar bone and three ribs were fc,und to be broken. Mr. Lowndes, assistant superha- tendent at Lime-street, was apprised by telephone of the Idisaster, and he was very soon on the spot, and break- down gangs from St. Helens and Warrington were re- quisitioned to clear the line. Owing. however, to the obstruction caused by the damaged waggons this work could only proceed slowly, and the line was blocked for several hours. Among the passengers were Miss Abel, daughter of Mr. R. Abel, of Higher Runcorn, and a lady companion. These were happily, like Mr. Richardson, none the worse for the accident, and they continued their journey by the Cheshire Lines. So far as Mr. Richardson could judge, it appeared that a portion of the goods train had become detached, and had been left behind, but of course, this is merely a supposition. It would be difficult to ascertain the real cause of the accident owing to the dense fog which prevailed. The driver and fireman of the passenger train both stated that the signals were down, and from this it may be understood that the signalman thought the goods train had gone. It was fortunate that the passenger train had slackened speed, and that the goods waggons were empty. Had it been otherwise, the consequence might have been terrible to relate. •STREET BETTING AT RUNCORN.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>‘1 Tout " Smartly Punished.</head>
        <p>A ‘1 Tout " Smartly Punished.</p>
        <p>Some considerable interest was centred in a case, in which William Brierley, of Duke's Fields, Runcorn,was charged with frequenting Waterloo-road, Runcorn, and using it for the purpose of "bookmaking "on the Bth of November 1899, and which was heard before the Run- corn Bench at Monday's Petty Sessions.—William Yar- wood, John Dowd, Frederick Bate, George Ramsden. and Lawrence Rafferty, all of Runcorn, were also im- plicated,and arraigned on the charges of freqaenting and using the same place for the purposes of agreeing to bet on the same date.—The cases were. taken separately, but the evidence did not materially differ in any case. Constable Fry said that in consequence of instructions received, he concealed himself in the Duke's stables. off Waterloo-road, and remained there from 11.30 to 1.20 on the 26th of October. From his hiding plact he commanded a complete view of the road and bridge. About 12.30, he saw the defendant (Brierley) come into the read, and take up a position about seven or eight yards from the place he was concealed at. Then a man (Dowd) came up, took out a newspaper, and after looking at it he spoke to Brierley, who made an entry in a book on the wall. Several other men also came up, looked at the paper in turn, and then after a conversation with Brierley he entered something in the book. He also saw Brierley give a man named Broadey three pieces of paper. Shortly after a number of men closed around Brierley, and money might have passed, but he could not be sure of that. Another man Eiward Broadey (he believed his name was) came along, and had a con- versation with Brierley and got the book off hina.—On the Bth November he was again in his hiding place this time accompanied by Constable Carrington. Several men came up with newspapers, and after consulting them, went to talk with Brierley who had taken up his old position. One of the men named Yarwood, then took out a brown leather purse, and abstracting a coin passed it to Brierley. He was, however, still writing in the book,but when he finished ha gave Yarwood change. After that witness saw several people who had to go off the footpath which defendants were obstructing. Both he and Carrington, then rushed out and he went after the bulk of the men whilst Constahle Carrington had a conversation with Bi ierley. He told the men they would 'be reported for betting on the footpath.—By Brierley Did you see any money pass ? Witness : Yes. saw Yarwood give you a coin and you gave him change.—Constable George Carrington, corrobora- ted the previous witness. The book (produced) the de- fendant handed to him on being challenged. There were W 6 bets recorded in the book, totalling £8 17s. There were 69 bets out of them which appeared to be made that day, the total amounted (for the 69) to £2 16'. Of the 69 bets recorded they concerned the names of 29 loxspe, which he found on looking at the " Liverpool 'Courier "were the names of the horses in connection with the Liverpool races. Also, he found in the book several initials, and took "W.Y." for William Yarwood " J.D." for John Dowd, "F. E." for Frederiek Bate. respectively. On going tan to the defendant he asked him, who it was that had just gone over the bridge and had spoken to him about is 6d. Brierley said it was Yarwood.—Defendant (who would not be sworn) said at the time the constable came to him, he was simply get- ting the horses for these men, and" getting it on" for them. He denied that any money kad been pass.d, and as for Constable Fry's statement that he had passed three slips to Mr. Broadey, he contradicted that.—The Bench considered the case was proved and decided to fine Brieeley 203 and 10s 6 1 costs or 14 days —The other men were each fined 51 and lOs 6cl costs ; which the chairman slid was a very lenient fine, end only imposed because of the defendants being first offenders.</p>
        <p>A Mare of obstructing the footpath was withdrawn against all the defendant.</p>
        <p>SPECIAL VISIT TO CREWE.—Mr. Thorns. Ison, Oculist and Aurist (of Tson's Eye and Ear Dispensary, Leeds), established 1871, will visit the Royal Hotel, Crewe, Wednesday December 6th. hours 11 to 1, and the Royal Hotel, Wigan. Thursday December 21sP. Hours 2to 8 and may be consulted on all cases of failing sight and hearing. New treatment without operation. Sufferers should avail themselves of the present opportunity of consultation. Specta•lles adapted for weak or defective eight.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>LAUREATE'S LATEST,</head>
        <p>THE LAUREATE'S LATEST,</p>
        <p>What is it makes the whole world kin ? What is it each of us ought to take What is it heals the broken skin? When starting out on bike or brake? Is it not Homocea? Surely 'tie Homocea. (CHORUS.) CHORUS Then surely we'll stick to what we know, I What is it "always touches the spot," And take Homocea wherever we go: I And heals whatever sores we've got? Hurrah I Homocea! Hurrah! I Nothing but Homocea. (CHORUS.) *N.B.—Not the Poet Laureate, he couldn't have done it; but the Spot-touching Laureate, your friend, HOMOCEA. For BURNS, BOILS, BRUISES, BITES, and BROKEN] SKIN; For CHAPPED HANDS, CHILBLAINS, CUTS and COLDS; HOMOCEA For STINGS, SCALDS, SCRATCHES, SWELLINGS SORES; FREELY. For PILES and ALL PECULIAR PAINS WITHIN:)</p>
        <p>USE</p>
        <p>"MY FRIENDS ON THE SHELVES."</p>
        <p>Half-Hour Lecture at Runcorn.</p>
        <p>On Monday Mr. William Handley (chairman of the Library Committee), presided at the half-hour lecture delivered at the Technical Institute, Runcorn, in connec- tion with the Runcorn Free Library. The lecturer was the Rev. C. W. L. Christiea, and there was a good attendance. The Lecturer chose for nis subject "My friends on the shelves," and in the course of an able address said ;—The greatest invention of the last thou- sand years is not the steam engine, the electric tele- graph or telephone, but the printing press. The press began a war against icreorance and all the miseries that flow from it, al war that has been advancing ever since. The first printed book was a prophesy of the downfall of all despotism, political and ecclesiastiall. For the last 450 years the press has been enriching the world, when we were born one of the most procioua treasures in our heritage was the gift of books. In trying to estimate the unbeunded resources of the book world we are as utterly lost as we should be in an effort to catalogu, all the vegetation of the Mississippi Valley. The books in existence are well nigh infinite in number, and they are increasing daily. There are pro- ductions on every subject, in every style, with every variety of human nature represented in the authors, of all values, from the utterly worthless to those worth a Kingdom to possess. Leaving out all reference to mere text books, and magazine literature, the lecturer spoke of his biblical and theological friends, showing the effect of the Bible upon ths literature of the world, giving quotations from many authors to show that it had taken hold of the world as no other Nook had ever done, and given to the Anglo-Saxon tongue the richest litera- ture the world has ever known. Then came the Historians, in which after urging the study of history, without a knowledge of which no man could do his duty to his generation, he recmamended the study of special periods such as the time of the puritans or the French Revolution. Following the historians came the Biographical friends, a needtul suppl,ment to history inasmuch as history is very largely a history of the doings of great men. Under this heading came autobiographies and books of travel. In the poetical group mention was made of a large number of men and women of genius from Homer to Tennyson and the reading of the best poets was urged as a set-off to the increased pressure of our age. The essayists were held up as models of English writing,and a brilliant tribute was paid to Ruskin. The last section was that of the novelists, under which young people were warned not to make fiction the main portion of their reading,but to use it only as a mere recreation and to choose only the best and purest authors. In the closing portion of the lecture books were spoken of as a help against the materialism of the age!, as a means of constant education, wider mental growth, and thus an ever present three to elevate us head and heart and fit us for the fulfilment of our life work.</p>
        <p>The proceedings terminated with the usual votes of ;hanks.</p>
        <p>THE DISAPPEARANCE OF A RUNCORN WOMAN. The Canal Gives up its Dead. Last Saturday, about 12 noon, the body or Clara Harrop, aged 35, of 32, Bridgewater-street, Ruoct:rn. was found in the Manchester Ship Canal at Runcorn The deceased had been missing from her home since 6 a.m. on the let November.—On Monday irornieg an inquest was held on the body by Mr. R. Debson,coroner, t the Police Court. Mr. Byfield was the foreman of the ury.—William Harrop, aged 14, son of the deceased, ( aid his father was blind. He identified the body as that of his mother, and saw her last on the evening of the 51th ult, when she said to him, " This is the last time you will see me." They went to bed between ten and eleven o'clock. Witness awoke about six next morning and hie mother was gone. He saw the body last Saturday, and knew it was his mother by her ehees, her dress, and the warts on her hands.— George Cowley, Weston Point, said he was the berth 'master at the Ship Canal Docks- He saw a body in the canal near the Runcorn lay-by, about noon, on Satur- day. He got a rope and tied it round the body, and then sent for the Weston Police Constable, with whose assistance they got it into a boat. They rowed to the ferry at Runcorn, and the police took charge of the body. Detective Bowyer stated that he examined the body, which was in a very decomposed condition. He searched the pockets, but there was nothing in them.— The jury brought in a verdict of "Found drowned" The Jury and the Viewing of Bodies. The foreman said he wished to ask, on behalf of the jury, whether it was necessary for them to view the body.—The Coroner said strictly speaking it was necessary. He wished the law otherwise many times himself, but they had to deal with it as they found it.—Mr. Lea said during the late coroner's time the jury were allowed to view the body at the cemetery, and then attend the inquest.—The Coroner said if the police made arrangements he would have no objection to swearing in the jury at the cemetery, but he wished the jury to see the body after they had been sworn.—The foreman said some of the jurymen lived at the other end of the town and they wished to be spared the inconvenience of coming down to the police station, and then going to view the body and coming back again.—The Coroner said he should h pleased to swear them in at the cemetery if it could be arranged.—Mr. Shaw, Mersey Mission to Seamen, said that the foreman of the jury had been kind enough to spend some time grappling for the woman since she had been reported missing ; and he was also organising a concert in aid of the husband of the deceased, who was blind.—Mr. Byfield said the husband was totally blind and almost penniless. The concert would lee held in three weeks time, and he should be pleased to have any assistance. He thanked the jury for their recognition of his services.—The jury gave their fees for the man and sight of them gave is. beside, making a total of £l.</p>
        <p>STRIKE AT CAStNER KELLNER CHEMICAL WORKS.</p>
        <p>Almost simultaneously with the issue of new shares to the public and a glowing description in the prospectus of the financial success of the Cast- net Kellner Electrolytical Chemical Co., Weston Point, comes a disturbance amongst the workmen in the: form of a strike. Of the 400 employed at the works about a hundred ceased work on Monday night in a summary manner, and fresh " hands " had to be procured in order to keep the manufac- tory going throughout the night. The origin of the dispute was amongst the shift men who work alternately day and night in the cell room, a department of the works where a very important process is carried out. Hitherto the shift men have each looked after, or superintended the working of one row of cells. At the beginning of the week the employers required each man to overlook one row and a half, an increase in work of fifty per cent. and at the same time a change was made in"the rate of wages, which the men contended was equivalent to 2s 3d per week. There was therefore, an increase of work with a reduction in wages, and the men rather than submit, quitted in a body. In addition to the 42 shift men, other hands employed in the same department struck work, making about a hundred in all. The manager contended that under the new conditions the men had no more work than could be comfortably man- aged and that the wages earned were ample as the work was far from laborious. Against this we may state they have to work in an atmosphere. the reverse of pleasant, and their wegas averaged about 295. per week. The masters refused to listen to any representations from the men after they had gone on strike, but promised to consider their position if they would resume their occupation. Accordingly on Tuesday morning the day-men took their places as usual and at five p.m. , the night-men joined them and jointly they had an interview with the managers,, he outcome of which was that the men consented to work under the new conditions for a month. At the end of this time a further conference will be held, and the ex- perience gained may lead to a permanent solution of the dispute.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>PRIMITIVE METHODIST BAZAAR.</head>
        <p>THE PRIMITIVE METHODIST BAZAAR.</p>
        <p>The Executive of the Primitive Methodist Church have issued an interesting statement showing the pre- gress of their efforts to wipe off a portion of the debt upon their Church and schoolroom, and wish to take the opportunity of thanking the kind friends who have con- tributed to the success of the RAzasr. The statement is as follows :—Donations, £53 6s. ; entrance Money, £l9 4s ; enterprise stall, £l4 is 4d ; congregational stall. £32 is 01--d ; bee hive stall, £27 12s 7i; bon marche stall, £35 17s 1-30 ; advertising stall, £24 2s 71; flower stall, £1 13s IC-1d ; refreshment stall. £l4 2s 2d ; knick-knack stall, £5 121 6cl ; ice cream, £1 4s 1111-d ; bran tub £2 18s 23 ; entestainmeotc, £4 61 9 ; teas for baa.aar fund. £2l 19s ; profit on hand book, £3 6s 6d; total, £261 8s 80.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>OUR FOOTBALL COMPETITION</head>
        <p>OUR FOOTBALL COMPETITION</p>
        <p>(COUPON ON PAGE 7.) £5O Offered Weekly—ZOO Aggregate Prize.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>RESULT OF LAST WEEK'S COMPETITION,</head>
        <p>RESULT OF LAST WEEK'S COMPETITION,</p>
        <p>CONTEST UPSET BY THE FOC.</p>
        <p>Owing to the foggy weather on Saturday last, most of the important football matches had to be abandoned, and orily four matches selected for our competition were played. Hundreds of competitors gave the correct results of those four matches, and any attempt to divide the prize would only result in the successful competitors receiving a ridicul- ously small sum. We have therefore decided to arrange a new competition in which competitors are requested to Bend in exaely the same number of coupons as in last week's. No money must be sent, the number of attempts made must not exceed the number made in last week's and only those competitors who sent in coupons in this competition will be entitled to compete. The following are the ten matches to be played on Saturday. December 2, by which the com- petition will be decided : 1 Notts. Forest v 2 Neweistle United</p>
        <p>1 Stoke T 2 Manchester City</p>
        <p>1 West Bromwich Albion v 2 Bury</p>
        <p>1 Everton v 2 Derby County</p>
        <p>1 Wolverhampton W. v 2 Blackburn Hovers</p>
        <p>1 Lincoln City 2 Leicester Fosse</p>
        <p>1 Gainsborou 412 Trinity v 2 Chesterfield</p>
        <p>1 Loughborough v 2 Walsall</p>
        <p>1 Batley v 2 Bradford</p>
        <p>1 Northampton v 2 Leicester</p>
        <p>All attempts must be made ou Wain piper, enclosed in an envelope marked Pootball Aro. 17. and posted on or before Tuesday Nov. 28.</p>
        <p>THE COUPON PRIZE.</p>
        <p>The £3 coupon prize has been won by Mr. H. Proctor, 5, Spring Vale, Haslingden, tomes.. with 6439 coupons, and will be forwarded in due course.</p>
        <p>DEATH OF MR. JOHN WEIR.</p>
        <p>We regret to record the death of Mr. John Weir, of the Baronet Farm, Lower Walton, which took place about 11 on Saturday evening,at bis residence Mr. Weir was a well-known agriculturalist and had held almost every civic office possible in a country district. Ever since the building of Walton Church, Mr. Weir has been a churchwarden, and for a long time previously he held the office of superintendent of the Sunday schools. For a period of nearly 40 years he has been a mem- ber of the Runcorn Board of Guardians, was a member of the Warrington Shire Horse Society and of the Warrington Farmers' Auction Company, and for very many years was a member of the Dares- bury Highway Board until its abandonment. He was the oldest tenant on Sir Gilbert Greenall's estate, the Barone,', Farm having been in the Weir and Foster family for over 200 years. Up to a few months ago he was remarkably strong and vigorous for his years, but in the early part of July he was seized with paralysis, from which he never re- covered. His medicll attendant, Dr. Sherratt, of Warrington, attended him regularly up to the time of his death. The deceased was nearly 75 years of age. His remains were interred at Walton Church at two p.m. on Wednesday,</p>
        <p>OBTAINING LODGINGS BY FALSE PRETENCES.—A WOMAN'S INGENIOUS Tines.</p>
        <p>On Thursday at the Warrington Police Court, before Messrs. John Fairclough and J. Hephsrd, Annie Brown,a young woman, was charged on a warrant with having between the 31st October and the Ist November ob- tained food and lodgings to the amount of 43 from Alice Banner, 35,Golborne-street, by means of false pretences. —She pleaded guilty--The Chief Constable said that on the 31st October prisoner presented-herself at the resi- dence of Dr. Smith and told Mrs. Smith that she had been sent by a lady at a registry office to apply for the position of cook at her house which was vacant. Mrs. Smith wanted a more elderly person than the prisoner so she told her she was not suitable. Prisoner went away and half as hour later, presented herself at the house of Mrs. Banner, 35, Golborne-st. and said she had obtained a situation as cook at Mrs. Smith's, but at the situation was not quite ready Mrs. Smith had sent her there to lodge and had given her to understand that she would be responsible for any debt she might incur. Prisoner must have got to know that Mrs. Banner had served in the family of Dr. Smith as nurse in years gone by. On the strength of the representations and the de- pendence that she pia eed on Mrs. Smith's promises Mrs. Banner allowed her to have food and lodgings that day and night. On the following morning at 11 o'clock prisoner went out saying that she would return latra on to accompany Mrs. Banner to the Market. She, how- ever, decamped and was not seen again till the previous night (Wednesiay) when Mrs. Banner " spotted " her in Lowe-street and at once gave her into the custody of P.C.Monks who was in the neigh- beurhood in plain clothes. It appeared that prisoner had visited various Lancashire towns and had played similar tricks. He had receivel a large number of communications from which it appeared that the woman was very much in request at St. Helens, Wigan, and other places. She had endeavoured to play the same trick and had succeeded in one or two instances in the district of the Lancashire County Constabulary. She had been living upon her wits for a number of years. The Chief Constable produced a record of prisoner's Previous convictions from which it appeared that she had undergone several long terms of imprisonment for stealing and false pretences, including twelve months at Salford.—She was now sent to gaol for three months with hard labour.</p>
      </div>
