      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</head>
        <p>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</p>
        <p>THEFT OF TIMBER AT WARRINGTON. WAt the Warrington Police Court on Friday, before the Mayor and other magistrates, Walter Daniela, labourer, Factory-lane, was charged with stealing from a flat at Cresfield's Wharf, Bank Quay, a quantity of timber value 15s.—He pleaded guilty.—Mr Browne prosecuted on behalf of Messrs Crosfield. He said the theft was only discovered by accident. The foreman missed a plank from the wareheuse and on looking across the river saw a boat and noticed that the plank that was missing was on it. He informed Messrs Crosfield's police- man and went across the river and instead of finding on the boat the plank missing he discovered five bundles of ' timber. Prisoner, when apprehended, said " I took it on my own responsibility. Walker had nothing to do with it." Walker was the man that worked with him. He asked the magistrates to make an example of the man.—A fine of 20i and costs with the alternative of a months' imprisonment was imposed. SAVAGE AMITJLT ON A WITS AT WARRINGTON. At the Warrington Police Court on Wednesday, before Alderman Sutton and ether magistrates, the alourned ease in which James Lewrenson, 7, Stamford-street, was charged with ass suiting and beating his wife Amelia Lawrenson, on the 14th of October, again came en for hearing, The Clerk informed the magistrates that since the adjournment a summons had been taken out for desertion.—Mr T S Steel (Jenkins and Co.), appeared for the complainant, and the defendant was represented by Mr. A. Browne.—Mr. Steel said that the case first same before the court on October llth, when cemplain- ant was cot legally represented, and it was adjourned for a month. Me. Browne had endeaveured to bring about a pacific eettlement but without 8UG0833 —Mr. Browne : I say with success. We have got an agreement signed—Mr. Steel said that the promises made in the agreement were evidently satisfactory to both parties but had not been carried into effect. The ease again came before the Bench last Wednesday and was adjourned for a week. Since then he had issued a summons for deserion and with the permiesion of the maeistratas he should like to proceed with the case first and allow the assault case to stand over for a time.— The magistrates had no objection to Mr. Steel going en with the case.—Mr. Steel said that the parties were married at the Parish Church on the 4th April, 1880. They had eine chiLtren living. She had been subjected to illtreatment by her husband since about three months aftPr the birth of the first She bad t) take out a summons against him and, he was bound ever to keep the peace. They then lived separately for a time but at his request and on his prom ising to behave differen'ly they agreed to live togethe again. On the 14th of Octolver defendant came heme a 4.30 in a drunken condition. Complaina.nt was sittin in a chair near the fire, and there were three children i the house. Defendant took the kettle from the fire and washed himself. When he came into the kitchen com- plainant was pAing the fire with a poker. Ile made use of a foul expression, took the peker from her and attempted to hit her with it. Re then struck her on the head twice with his fists. She was trying to get out of his way, when he seized her by the throat and held her with both hands. He held her in that position two or three minutes and said he would choke her. The son Thomas then came to his mother's assistance, and after a severe struggle managed to pull his father away. Defendant then changed his clothes and prepared to go oat, and his wife asked him for money to buy food for the children. He said he would not give her anything till he was forced to do so. Ile thee went out and she followed him fel' some distance, and he said he had left all the money there was for her on the mantelpiece. She went back home, but found there was no money. She had not received anything from him since.—Mr. Browne said it was through complainant not giving defendant his clothes that the terms of the agreement had not been carries out. —The magistrates declded to make a separatists order, defendent to contribute 13s. per week towavis the maintenance of his wife and family. He was also oreered to pay the court costs and 103. 61 advocate's iee.—Mr. Steel withdrew the charge of assault.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>MINOR OASES. --- Warrington Borough Police Court.</head>
        <p>MINOR OASES. --- Warrington Borough Police Court.</p>
        <p>FRlDLY.—Before the Mayor (in the chair), Alderman Wright, Councillor Smethurst and Mr. Hepherd. AN APPRENTICE SUMMONED.- Mary Jane Murat 9, Scott- street, was summoned, being an apprentice in the employ of Messrs, Platt, Wilkinson and Company, Limited, for leaving hen work on October 2nd. thereby putting the firm to a loss of 12s. —Mr. Browne appeared on behalf of the firm. —Defendant said she had served her time.—Mr. Browne said that the defendant was evidently under a misapprehension. A case was brought, in the Counts Court in March, 3.898. whereby the plaintiffs claimed damages and an injunction against certain persons in town from taking away their apprentices, and in consequence of the decision be (Mr. Browne) advised Platt, Wilkinson and Company, to have their apprentices rebound Defendant consented to be rebound for two years and she was given a sovereign in addition to her ordinary wages. The fresh indenture was for two Tears from the 6th July. 1898. Defendant said she was only rebound for twelve months.— Mr. Baines, one of the managers for the firm stated that the defendant did not make any objection to being re- bound.—Defendant's mother said that her daughter had been bound five times. the first time for three years, and four times for twelve mouths and reeeived a sovereign each time. Two years was never mentioned, when she was bound this time. Mr. Blear i did not read the indenture to them. He slid they had heard them read many a time so he need not read it then. —Defendant now produced a doctor's note saying she was unfit to work, and the charg-‘ was withdrawn. • lit SBARD ABSCONDS.--Willtarn Whittaker. 18. Marsh House-lane, was summoned to show cause why an order should not be made for him to contribute to the mainten- ance of his grand child who had become chargeable to the union—Mr. Shearer prosecuted on behalf of the Guardians and Mr. T. S Steel (Jenkins and Co,) dcfended.—Mr. Shearer said defendsmts' strand child and its mother were admitted to the workhouse on the Bth of the present month. The husband had gone to America and he thought defendant bad had something to do with his going away. Defendant earned £2 2s. a week and he asked for an order fer ss. a week.-7-Mr. Steel said defendant had nothing to do with his son going away. It was against his wish and he had offered to help him if he would remain at home. He, however, re- fused, and had gone away on his own responsibility There</p>
        <p>were three persons liable to pay. Two grandfathers and the mother, and he thought it was unfair • hat the Ettiardiins shotud summons this man. lie couid mly afford to pay 23.6,1 a week, as h- hAd t: children at intro.—An order for 3s. 6d. a week was made. . .</p>
        <p>CLJELTY TO aiiPirisr.—Sarah Yates. 20, Mersar-street, was summoned for illtreating a pony on the 6th inst. by work- ing it whilst it was in an unfit state —lnspirtor Brown, R.S P.O A.. said that oa the date named he was on duty in Bridge-street, when he saw the defendant driving a pony attached to a cart Loden with glass bottles. He noticed th tt it was lame and stopped it. On exarninination be found it was suffering from strained tendons in the near fore leg It appeared ta be in great pain. On. the back of the knee there were traces of recent blistering, and when he pressed it the animal flinched. He told th defendant of the condition of the pony and she sail she knew it was so. She thought of se!ling it to Mr. Shepherd the horse slaughterer.—Defen- dant was fined in the casts (Ss)</p>
        <p>ItoNner.-11aforl Mr. F Monks (in the chair), Clouricill•rs Smethurst and Shaw Green, and Messrs. S. Wright and</p>
        <p>Birchall.</p>
        <p>TEMPOR ARY AUTHORITY. —Mr. Robert Whitfield was granted temporary authority to sell at an off licensed house in Leh-street In lieu of Mattl,la Payton. " A SEA.FORTH HIGHLANDER" IN TYE DOCE. MAID Kenny was charged with boing drunk and disorderly in Bridge-strest at 7.15 on Saturday night.—lt was stated that prisoner pulled his coat off and challenged people to fight. He said he belonged to the Seaforth Highlanders, and the police could net lock him up.—Prisoner said some friands of his were going to the Transvaal and he ha been drinking with thent.—A fiae of fis and costs was imposed. DRUNICENNESS.—The following persons were fined for the above offence 3—Michael Lee. lUs and costs, Margaret Andsr- son, be, Sarah Maloney Sc and costs, and Michael Collins 101 and costs</p>
        <p>DRUNK IN CHIROL—TIER RETURN FROM A FUNERAL.— Daniel Flanaghaa, cab driver, 13, ICigevrorth-street, was summoned for h iving been drunk in Bridge-street on the 9th November last whilst in ohaege of two horses and a coseh.— P.O Bradley said the defendant was returnieg from a funeral, and he wts so drunk that he elnaost fell off the box when at Bridge foot. lie was float se. and costs —Thomas Walker. fa-mer, Pump House farm, Antrobus, was on the evidence of P.C. Stewart, fined 5s for ')eing drunk in charge of a horge and cart at Bridge-foot on the 15th init. CRUELTY TO A HOR3E.—Thonvls Percival, Hall-street, was charged with working a horse whilst In an unfit c )ndition on the 7th November, and Arthur Lunt, Bridge-foot, was charged with causing the animal te be worked whilst in that nottill- tion.—Mr. Unsworth was for the defence. After hearing the evidence which was to the effeet that the animal was v-ry lanae and was practically walking on three legs, the Bench decided to fine Yeraival in the costs. aw 4 the second defen- dant lOs and costs and the fee of the veterinary surgeon. TUESDAY.-13fore Mr. Birchen (in the chair), and Dean-</p>
        <p>cillor Shaw drama.</p>
        <p>Two MONTHS FOR ASSAULT—James HighAßl was charged on a warrant with assaulting P.C. Wright on the 18th inst.—He pleaded not guilty. He was further charged with assaulting his mother, Mart ha Higham,on he 11th inst., to which he pie tded geilty.—P.C. Wright said that at 8.30 oil Saturday night he was called to 11, Roger-street by the prisoner's mother, who complained that she had been ejected from the house by her son. When he got near the bouse, prisoner came up and said" Well, what do you want" and made use of bad language. He then struck at him and hit him in the face. Witness closed with him and tried to arrest him. Prisoner struck him hi the chest and kicked him on the I gs. After a very severe struggle the man got away, He was told that he had gone for a poker. but he never returned.—Martha Higham, prisoner's mother, said that she :called the last witless to eject her son from the house. He had put her out. On the 11th inst. he struck her oa the arm causing her to stumble against tha dresser and hurt her side. Sh, was stunned for a oalnute.—Sarsh Mos- ley, a neighbour, having given evidence, prisoner, who was described by the Chief Constable as one of the worst eharaeters in Warrington, was committed to gaol for two months. MARGARET OAKES AGAM—Margaret Oakes, Dial-street, was charged with indecent behaviour In Cobden-street, at 12.5 that morning.—The Chief Uo•istible sail prisoner had been before the court 18 times previously, seven times this year.—She was committ ci to gaol for 14 days. WmanEsDaY.—Before Alderman Sutton (in the chair) and</p>
        <p>Messrs. Birchall and Geddes.</p>
        <p>OCCOIGNAL LICENCE—r. Shillcock, of the Bay Horse Inn. was grantel an occAslonal license to sell at the Parr Hall on Friday. the occssion'being the Warrington Cricket Club annual ball.</p>
        <p>Scnooi. CASE 3. —On the evidence of Mr, W. Jones, school attendanc3 officer, the following were fined for neglecting to send their children regularly to school :—John Ehniels, forgeman. 14, Plilway-squlre, 2s 63 ; Wiliam Hayes, forge- man, 17. Dale-3treet. 2s 64. ; Hiward Smith, 72, Cartwright- street, 23 6cf; James Oliver. labourer, 67, Howard-street, 5s ; ladvrard Smith, forgeman, 144. Liverpool-roal, 53 ; Thomas Pott r, lab mrer, 3. Melville-street, 53 ; Patriok Glynn, labourer. yard off Stamford--treet. 2s 6,1 ; Thomas Kenyon, labourar. 45, Tillerr-streA, is ; Joseph Hathbone, shunter, 45, Lythgoes-lane, 23.</p>
