      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>Runcorn Petty Sessions. MONDAY.—Before MessrsF. J. ,N orman, (chairman) and</head>
        <p>Runcorn Petty Sessions. MONDAY.—Before MessrsF. J. ,N orman, (chairman) and</p>
        <p>F. Wiewall.</p>
        <p>MINIR ClSF.B.—Andrew Gardner (18),Artlour Fernival (9), James Hanapsen (29), youths of Runcorn, pleaded guilty to a charge of obstructing the footpath at Heath-road on the 14th of November. On the intercession of the Superintendent ef Police the boys who had apologised for their conduct, were dismissed with a caution.—John Dobson, and John Fearnett, for a similar offence which they admitted committing in Victoria-road were etch fined 58 including ccsts.—John Cundy and Charles Booth, of Weston, were tined las for tid- ing upon their lurries en the 9th of November with- out having control of the same whilst these vehicles were procooding along Cl,fton-rbad.—Alfred Alustree, for partaking in the evil practice of setting fireworks off in the ~treets on the 4th of November was fined ss.—Rmma Field and John Rafferty, allowed their several dogs to be at large in contravention to the byelaws, for which neglect they were mulcted in 5s fine each.—Jchn NicKirk, being annoyed by several bees who knnniced off his hat, used "swear words," to the urchins. Constable T. S. Bowyer, chancing to hear the vulgar expressions, reported the affair, wi: h the result that Ma Ki,k was discharged with caution.—An allegation against Charles ilrnest Bowvers (a young lad) for cutting, and otherwise damaging property belonging to the exors of I'. J. Pagett, amounting to 16s was withdrawn.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Local Successes.</p>
        <p>On Saturday, Nliss M. Silcock and Miss Mabel rien,.hali (pupils of the Misses Rimmer, Wilson Patten-Atreet) were each awarded priztis for position gained by them in the examination of the London College of Music, held last December. Ak or CUNNINGHAM' HIGH-CLASS MINIMAL WATEHJS.—Advt</p>
        <p>Spiritualism.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Porter was the speaker at the weekly meet- ings on Sunday in the Bewsey-strest liall,and gave two addresses, the one in the evening being upon "The spiritual world a reality." Clairvoyance fol- lowed at each meeting. On Nionday evening Hrs. Porter gave a number of clairvoyant delineations, followed by a few experiments in psychometry.</p>
        <p>THE GLEN-SPRY AND STRATHMILL DISTILLERIES Situated in the finest Whisky-producing district of Scotland re the property of W. and A. Gilbey, and the Whisky made from Rom, -2rown Earley only. Glen-Spey," 3s. 6d nd • Strathmill." 3s. 6d.. sold by W. and A. GI:BEY gents in every town.—Adet. Warrington and the Late Marchioness of Salisbury. The following is a copy of a telegram forwarded by the Meyor on Tuesday morning to tlie Prime Minister :—" The most Honourable the Marquis of Salisbury, Hatfield, I join with all my fellow townspeople in offering to yourself and your family our deep and sincere Sympathy in the irreparable loss you have sustained in the death of your noble consort.—Henry Roberts, Mayor of Warrington.</p>
        <p>BEST BOOTS FOR ALL, UNDER TICE CLOCK.--Adv Accidents.</p>
        <p>Charles Collins (45), 2, billey-street,was watching the Reservists march to the station on Monday about six o'clock, when he slipped and broke his leg. He was taken to the Infirmary for treatment. Whilst getting off a train at Bank Quay Station about five o'clock on Monday afternoon a man named John Moran, aged 30 years, of 37, White Friars'-lane, Coventry, slipped and fell,breaking one of his legs. He was removed to the Infirmary, where his injuries were attended to.</p>
        <p>ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS MINIMAL WATSRS.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Licensing: At the Borough Police Cours,on Monday morning, Mr. Browne made an application re the King and Queen Inn, Prigate-lane. He said that at the ad- journed annual licensing meeting he put before the magistrates plans for the rebuilding of the inn, but there was no accommndation for club room. Now it appeared, to the surprise ot the architect of the Brewery Company, that a large club was held at the inn for the convenience of 137 members, and no provision had been made for them. Mr. Browne having explained how it was intended to supuly the detect, his application for an alteration of the plans was agreed to.</p>
        <p>UNDER THE CLOCK FOR GOnD BOOT.—Advt. Buckley-street P.S.A. Brass Band.</p>
        <p>A concert in aid of the above band was given in the schoolroom on Nlowifty evening, there being a large audience. Councillor Tinnion presided. Songs were rendered by he MiEl3OO Armstrong and A Mor- rell, Mr C Nurse, and Mr Corbett, each artiste earn- ing well merited applause. Mr A Bates gave two violin solos, in excellent style, which were well re- ceived. A recitation by Miss Morrell called forth a well deserved encore. Misses Glover. W Richard- son, Ilampon, Gerard, and Taylor, formed an excel- lent glee piny, and were heard to advantage in their song. Ventriloquail sketches by Mr Katel were the gems of the evening. and caused roars of laugh tor. Mr A Trow made a good accompanist. At the close of the concert Mr A Scott proposed. and Mr W Cross seconded a vote of thanks to Mr Tinnion for occu- pying the chair, and also to the artistes for their services in making the concert a success.</p>
