      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, NOVEMBER 24, 1899.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, NOVEMBER 24, 1899.</p>
        <p>IN vi ew or he Appro.toning 100,G041l easoo, IL [any b,u- teresting to state that the world-famed manuf ,cturers. Go, G. BU4SEY 34 01) , of London. are preparinz a stock of Balls. which for quality, uniformity, ant durability, will be unique. These bAlls will be distinguished by the appearance thereon of their well known trade mark ,trefaie. Ask the local sports Dealer for their Catalo ;tie. If not procurable, apply to 36 ic 33, Queen Victoria-street. London Factories —Peckham, London; an I Rims well, Suffolk.—Advt.</p>
        <p>*** Secretaries of Football Clubs are requested to note that reports of matches played on Saturday must be sent to the Head Office, Bewsey-str-et. Warrington, n3t later than 10 o'clock on Thursday morning or they cannot be inserted.</p>
        <p>secured Jones and Melvin, two Welsh players, and great things are expected of them. Batley started and Davies narrowly missed kicking a goal from a penalty. Doughty kicked into the Halifax half, and Jones replied, Batley dribbling well, aud all but scored. Immediately after Spurr romped over for Batley at the corner, but Davies failed at gual. Stoyle getting p,tssession, was just brought down on the line. Halifax attacked time after time, but the defence was goad. Hulliwell was sou to advantage with a good rue, aud putting his men on side, Batley were penalised, and Wood kicked a goal. GR.th scored a try, and Fczzard kicked the goal. Half- time :—Batley, 8 points ; Halifax, 2 points. Winkell kick, d off, and a ireries of kicks followed, scrimmages being formed at centre. • Auty getting possession, ran strongly, ad passing to Davies, he scored a splendid try, the same player kicking a magnificent goal. Final —Batley, 16 points ; Halifax 2 points.</p>
        <p>and to Rudyard fCipling they owed more than to any other living poet for that spirit of revived interest in ] the things that pertained to the welfare of the British Empire. The nation bad been stirred to a great passion not of hatred nor of lust of conquest as some Little' Englanders would have them believe—(applause)—but of whale hi acted patrietiorn and leo eLy. Everywhere the call to " Pay, pay, pay, for our credit's sake " had been responded to in such way as to win the admiration of the civilized world. (Applause.) Such a movement as this could not fail to impress upon the whole I civilized world and to leave an indelible impression upon the last days of this most brilliant century It was not his intention that afternoon to enter into the ] disputed points of the origin of the war. He hated war, perhaps this was news to some people. (Laugher.) It was a great evil, the greatest evil and calamity of the times. Some people would feel more comfortable when he said that he acknowledged that the height of British, interests was not war, but peace. (Applause.) Be I hoped tee time was not far distant when woe: drums throbbed no longer and battle flags were furled in the Farliement of men—the federation of the world. What- ever views the country might hare formed as to the de- tails of the negotiations that had taken place between the Trsnsvaal Gevernment and the Government of her Majesty, it was clear he thought, that the overwhelming opinion of the country was that war had been forced upon us and to a very large extent had been caused by Kruger's contemptuous indifference to the reasonable requirements asked of hine,and which were most temper- ately expressed and also by his callousness to the horrors of war. (Applause). It was his intention to express his own opinions and convictions ; he had no brief from any newspapers or party, aad he did not intend to be gagged on that platf srm. He thought that the ultima- tum which Kruger sent to this country, for abruptness and brutality, surpassed anythMg ever attempted or issued by Napoleon even in the zenith of his power and greatness. No betel, justification for war in defence of the empire was needed after that. He thought , the cause was a right and just cause, and ha believed that liberty we, the end and aim of Britain's policy in South Africa. The policy of theat Britain had been for many years in South Africa a policy of consolation and forbearance. Be thought there was no word in the Vocabulary more distasteful to our beloved Queen, and " long may she reign "—(applause)—or to the Prime minister than the word war. He hated war, but he recognised the fact toat of all policies none was more clang:emus, and none more calculated to sap the nation's greatness than the advocacy of peace at any price. (Applaisse.) If we per- mitted the continental powers to entertain tho idea that Great Britain was indifferent te the interests of her citizens and accepted insult with equanimity from Kruger " our brother Beer "—(lauehter)—then, he thought the end of he greatness would soon come. (Hear, hear.) He believed that liberty was the end and aim of Britain's poliey in South Africa. There was no greater proof of this than the fact that our colonies had come to our help. (Applause.) He was sure they admired and appreciated the splendid loyalty evinced by everyone cif our self-governing colonies, and he sincerely hoped this would be an object lesson to France and other nations. (Loud Applause)— who indulged in the pin prick policy, and that itwould impress them, as they had never been impressed before, of the solidarity of the British Empire. They were re- minded to-day of the brotherhood of the entire British Empire, and if we were to be successful in the fight— which of course we should be—(epplausel—victory would establish British supremacy in South Africa ; the deliverance of the poor black man, who had been treated so badly by the Boers, the abolition of slavery and the advancement of salvation to the Zambesi and probably by and by to Khartoum and Cairo. (Applause.) The great feature of this century had been the steady growth of pin colonial empire. It was remarkable and marvel- lous how our empire had grown by leaps and bounds, to that the Colonies and dependencies of Great Britain now covered about one-sixth of the land surface of the glebe and nearly the same proportion of its population. How had these vast territories been acquired ? Our methods were those of right And love. It was not for our sakes merely that we colonized nor for our agrandisement, but for the benefit of the posple. It had been the rule of other countries and races, and he was afraid it was the rule of some great countries to-day, to Use their colonies for their own special pur- poses. Instead of burdening her colonies, Great Britain had spent large sums of money for their benefit. When they thought of what this country had done for India they would think that our rule had been as un- selfish and beneficial as it had been brilliant and forced. (Applause.) We took nothing front India for the ex- penses if our home Empire. No Engliehman paid less taxes because India was ours. He was preud that we had in India to-day as Viceroy representative of the Queen, Lord Curzon who was reaching the hearts of poor, op- pressed people forsaking the paths of pomp,and splendour and visiting to-slay the famine and plague-stricken dis- tricts and ministering to our fellow citzens in our great Empire in the East. (Applause.) It was too soon he thought, to speak of the future in South Africa. He ] believed that when the Union Jack floated over the Government Houses at Pretoria and Bloemfon- tein we should have a great federation of ' United States in South Africa which with federated Australia and Canada would bang us nearer to realising the federation of the whole British Empire. (Loue applause.) They were proud of the gallant fellows who were doing their country's work and upholding the traditions of the country and were fight- ing and dying for the Empire. Rudeard Kipling re- minded them that there were those who stayed at home and killed Kruger with their mouths. (Laughter.) The soldiers who were fiehting for Queen and country had a claim upon them and as the Chairman had said, it was not a question of charity. (Hear, hear.) Their wives and children had a claim which could not be discharged by cheering at publie meetings. The sufferings which the war would bring about would have to be borne by a comparatively small portion of the community and Rudvard Kipline reminded them—they were in the first place indebted to the gallant soldiers and in the second place to those who were dependent upon them. (Applause.) They could net help but , admire the loyalty and patriotism manifested by the reservists. (Loud applause.) They also appreciated the many offers which had been made by the Volunteers. (Applause.) He should like to emphasize the fact tha the Warrington reserve men had the strongest claim upon their sympathy. Th 0;e men had completed thei term of actual service and had re-entered civil life and ] now rejoined the ranks at the sacrifice of their wages, so, surely, they ought to pass round the hat. (Ap- plause.) They had read with pride of those fine brave fellows, the Dublin Fusiliers and the Gordon ' Highlanders. (Applause.) They had shown the Isame military instinct and patriotism as their forefathers who fought at Waterloo, Ineerreann, asid ,Balaclava, by fighting so gallantly and sealing the Iheights of Glencoe. (Applause.) They mourned the loss of General Symons and all these gallant men who I had fallen during the past few days. They mourned the loss of that fine young aristocrat, Captain Eeerten, whose death, he thought, would bind the army and navy more elesely together than ever. These men bad shown ] them what could be done by British soldiers in the hour ] of They were sympathising with General White, whose manly despatches had won for him the respect and admiration of the civilized world. epplause). They admired the admirable strategy and cool- ness of Baden Powell—(applaume)—and also, he was sure they were pleased to find that that very brilliant young fellow, Winston Churchill had inherited some of the dash and brilliance of his father, and was standing by the so-called eetunton soldier in defence of the Bilden flag. (Applause.) Ho rejoiced that the position of the private soldier bad been so much I improved upon, Our soldiers and sailors had to-day every device of modern medical science, unlike the brave men who lay wounded in the foul cock-pit of Nel- son's flagship "The Victory," whose wounds were cauterised with hot pitch to check the bleeding. Thank God there had been a change in that respect. Many years aeo there was a cry from the Crimea, and that cry was heard by a lady who was still livine.—Florence Niehtin:sale,—and she set out for the Crimea and ministered like an angel to the wounded soldiers. Her noble example entirely changed the whole system of nursing and to day the blunderer of fifty years ago had ben replac'd by the , trained nurses who were to day facing fearlessly the ] realities of war and fighting against wounds and suf- ferings beneath the Red Cross. (Anplause ) In con- ] elusion they were passing through ohe of those crises which only occurred now and again in the long hist sry of the country and may God save them from passion and prejudice. (Hear, !var.) They were on the thres- hold of a new era. Let them trust in God ; there was need to day for a righteous determination. He asked them to live for God in the first place and for Queen and country in the secend place. He made a special appeal before sitting down on behalf of the " Absent-minded beggar," and asked them to give an honest, ringing Amen to th't. appeal voiced in the poem and "Pass the plate for their credit'a sake and pay,pay, pay." (Loud applause.) A collection was then made ard realized £l5 16s. Whilst the collection was being usage, the Volunteer Band under tho direction of Mr. T. Armstrong played selections of music for which they were heartily ap- plauded. The Benediction haying besn pronounced by the Rev. H. G. ROBBRTS, the proceedings terminated. ] Mr. Clegg, the secretary, wao responsible for the pro- gramme in the afternoon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION. _- LANCASHIRE SENIOR COMPETITION. WARRINGTON v. OLDHAM.</head>
        <p>THE NORTHERN UNION. _- LANCASHIRE SENIOR COMPETITION. WARRINGTON v. OLDHAM.</p>
        <p>A Defeat But Not a Disgrace.</p>
        <p>There seemed little proiipen of football on Saturday when Warrington left toe Central Station en route fir Oldham, as the fog was exceediaglv dense. Marvellous to relate it clear,d a 3 the train approached Manche,ter, and continued to improve all the way to Oldham. The math therefore took puce as arranged. Warrington took a weakened team away, the absentees inclu,iing Bate, Harris, and Fish, the latter crying off at the last minute with a tad cod. Moores fillbd the vacancy. Oldham hail tneir best team out. Notwithstanding the rawness of the day the Oldham folk climbed up to Witersivd lings in crowds, and there would be fully 12,000 preient when Warrington took the field lowa 23 minut,39 late. In the meantime the V.)lunteer bacd had discoursad martial music. Mr. S. Williams was referee, and the teams tiled up in the follow.ng order. Oldham : Therass, full back; T Davies, T Fletcher, S Lees, and S Williams, three-quarters ; A Lees aad J Lawton, half backs ;Frater,Ellis, Broome, Telfer,Baasor J H Wilkinkan, G Wilkinson, and Porter forwards. . _</p>
        <p>Wa.rring,t : Boscow, full back ; Wood, Jones, Isherwood and Moores, thve-quarters ; Duckworth and Ashur, half backi ; Taylor, M)rrison, Bo trdman, Fairhurst, Eden, Scholtzs, Merryvveather, and Swift, for- wards.</p>
        <p>There was no wind, so that the winning of the toss was of no importance. Jones started for Warriagtou and play was even for a time. A Lees and Davies made headway on the o:dham side, the latter punting across where Bosaow secured. Warrington played up with some determination, and reached the Oldham quarter. Here the ball came out on the h ,nise side, and Law- ton, Fletcher and Davies passing accurately the letter scored a brilliant try after runnine three- parts of the field. S. Lees kicked a goal. This early reverse took sonee of the heart out of Warrington, but in general pay they held their own. The game pro- ceeded for the most part in midfield where Flvtcher a.gain opened the way for Davies, who scered grandly• The g,cial kick failed. Interesting play followed, but be- hind the pack Warrington were slew, and the two Lees soon nude a be tutiful opening for Williams who raced in from the centre with a third try. S. Lees hit the post: with his goal kick. Warrington bucked up and attacked again. S. Lees mulled near the line and Wood mede a fine effort to score, but was held just inside the goal lies. Oldham retaliatel with th- usual passing run and D tvies scored again, Frater failinz at goal, Merryweather wee hurt and had to be carried off, the misforcune seernieg to rouse Werringten to greater exertion. Half-time arrived with Oldhem leading by 14 points tc nil. Frater re-started and Warringtou went off with a sur- prising dash. They carried the first scrimmageossiatel by the limping Merry weather, and Duckwerth made a dash for the line, Thomas grassing him. Oldham tried hard to get away but found the Warrington men too quick for them. Something seemed to have roused the visitors to superhernan exertioes, and the way they overplayed the all-conquering Oldhanasrs w.s an eye- opener. The game was entir ly change, and Warring- ton did all the attacking. Scrimmage after scrimmige ! was won and ground gained. Wend was frequently to the fore, and eventuelly ran finely down the field. When challenged he threw to Eden who handed to Jones and the latter completed a splendid effort in dash- ing style. The score was net augmented by Taylor. The attack was meintained. Wood tackling Fletcher p'uckily. Moores received from Duckworth, eluded Davies and had hard luck at the corner where Thermal twice bowled him over. The Warrington halves beth made individual efforts to score and were never far from succeeding. The Oldham defence was strong however, though the backs made no headway at all. For a long times they were penned to their own 25, but near the C'o3o S. Lees and Williams got away on the right and ran to the Werrington end where Jones stopped them, getting hurt in the process. He was carried off the field and the gain- proceeded. Warrington kicked clear and then the whistle niunded the elose to a well conteste I game, Oldham being wieners by 1 goal, 4 tries (14 points), to Warrington 1 try (3 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>Millom v. Widnes.</head>
        <p>Millom v. Widnes.</p>
        <p>rough game was played at Millern between the above clubs and ended in a poin,!e3e draw.</p>
        <p>Swinton v. Stockport.</p>
        <p>There were only about 5,000 persons present to witness the return match between tee above clubs at Swinton. The home side were playing ?lesser for Hampson avid Pearson in place of Davies. Stockport had their full team with the exception that S Inds played instead of Hodgson. Pearson acted as captain, and having beqn beaten by the opposing captain in the toss, kicked off towards the Swinton goal. Oram failed to take the ball from a lofty kick under his own posts, and the Swinton- ians looked like scoring, but the ball was made dead. R. Valentine and Lewis both failed to take advantage of good openings, hut Pearson made amends, and scored a clever try Goodman adding an equally clever goal. Stockport livi-ned up after this, and Lewis, who had seen at fault two or three times, gave them an opening by failing to take the ball. The game was fairly even after this. The concluding stages did not provide any attractive features, and an uninteresting game resulted as follows .—Swinton,l goal 1 try; Stockport, nil. Salford v. Leigh.</p>
        <p>At Salford, bsfors 6.000 people. On the home side Doran was substitnted for Ivor Grey,whilat Leigh played &amp;minter) and Ramsdale instead of R Monts and Botrd- man. Molyneux started for the visitors, but Salford were first to attack. Tom Williams being early con- spicious with a good run. After the game had berm in duration ten minutes Griffiths got possession closo in and dropped .a fine goal for Salford. Leigh were next con- spicuous, and Molvneur on two occasions only just failed to drop a goal. Tom Williams and Hely/en played a fine game on the home side and several times the last- named almost got over. The Leigh defence was very sound and at the interval there was no further addition to the score. Half-time :—Salford, 1 goal (2 points) ;</p>
        <p>. . Leigh, nil. On resuming. Salford attacked, and a good passing bow by the whale of the three-quarter line re- sulted in Efoakins scoring at the corner. Griffiths failed from the place. After the re-start frona the 6..ntre, Leigh, by means of a free kick, made an incursion in the home terrirnry, where Smith made an awful mull letting the ball slip when he had time to clear, the eon- sequence being that Caldicott rushed down, tipped the bail to McMasters. near the posts, for that player to score a try. Molyneux easily landed A goal from the place. With both teams on an equal footing the play was naturally interesting, but the visitors now more than held their own, and, mach to the surprisa of Vv. Salford supporters, Pearson dropped a goal, which put them ahead. A. couple of minutes later Dan Smith dr •pped a goal for Salford, and thus the scores were made level again. From now to the finish each tried hard to obtain the lead. and Leigh. through Molyneux, who dropped another goal, were successful in winning mateh. Find l 3 goals 1 try (9 points) ; Salford, 2 goals 1 try (7 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
