      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>"THE ABSENT-MINDED BEGGAR." THE REV. H. G. ROBERTS ON KIPLING'S POEM.</head>
        <p>"THE ABSENT-MINDED BEGGAR." THE REV. H. G. ROBERTS ON KIPLING'S POEM.</p>
        <p>Large and enthusiastic audiences assembled in the Parr Hall on Sunday afternoon and evening, when meet- ings were held under the auspices of the P.S.A. Society in connection with the Young Men's Christian Associa- tion. No doubt the principal reason for there being such large assemblies was that the proceeds were to be devoted to the Mayor's fund for the relief of the wives and families of the reservists—a fund which commends itself to everybody for support. Special interest was centred in the afternoon gathering, as the Rev. H. G. Roberts, M.A., was announced to deliver an address on Kipline'e . latest posies " The Absent-Minded Beggar." A 2.30 the members of the Ssciety met at the Y.M.C.A. House, Friars-green, and marched in proces- sion, headed by the Volunteer Band, who played selec- tions of music in excellent style, to the Hall ; which as before stated was filled with an en'husiaetic audience. Mr. W. J. Forehaw presided, and there were also pre- sent on the platferm :—The Revs. H. G. Roberts, M.A., ang C. Finch, and Messrs. W. Hickman (the presid•nt), Henry Taylor, P.C.M., Holford, Woods, and Cleeg (secretary). The proceedings commenced with the singing of a verse of the National Anthem with much fervour, Mr. Frank Hickman presiding at the organ. After a hymn had been sung and prayer had been engaged in, The CHAIRMAN delivered a brief address, Eli con- gratulated the society on having brought teeether such a splendid gathering, and also on their patrietisns to their Queen and country, and on having secured the able ser- vices of the Rev. H G Robertt—(applause)---who he hail no doubt, would shertly give them a right and true interpretation of the" Absent-minded beggar." The object of the meeting as they were well aware was a very worthy and a very laudable object. It was to get as ) muds money as they possibly could Co assist the wives and families of those brave fellows who were nobly fighting the battle of justice in South Africa. (Applause.) They were fighting for the homes and pro- perty of our colonial fellow-subjects and they were also fighting for equal rights. He was sure that when they kthe audience) were asked to respond to the call which would be made upon them they would respond to it right well and cheertully. It must be very pleasing and comforting to them to know how well our colonies had behaved in this matter. They had come to us as one great brotherhood and transports had been sent from all parts of our colonies to South Africa in order to assist in fighting this battle of justice. 'Tlais great Em- pire was never sa loyal and noble or united as at the present tiree—(appleuss)—and they all knew that unity was strength. The British lion and his cubs were deter- mined to fight this war through and bring to an end the injustices that existed in South Africa. (Applause,) They all knew at the same time that war, even if it was a righteous war was a very terrible and sad thing. (Hear, hear.) It certainly caused many sad and sorrow- ful hearts and he wanted them to sympathise with those sorrowful hearts in a substantial way. He did not alto- gether consider it a matter of charity. Irle considered it a matter of duty. (Applause.) It was their duty to assist, and to be true to their country and to these brave soldiers: He asked them to respond to the appeal which would be made upon them, right heartily and well. (Applause.) Miss DAVIES then rendered a solo " Behold I stand at the door" in a feeling manner, after which the Rev. H. G. ROBERTS delivered his address. He said 'he must confess that he was devoutly thankful to see that the" Absent minded beggar" had so meey friends in Warrington. (Applause.) Be presumed that every member of that great gathering was familiar with the poem which had, during the past few days, wen from the pockets of the English people, £lO,OOO which be supposed had been given to that cause which had brought them there that afternoon. He was told that wherever" The Absent beggar" was recited or sung, (and he wondered who would have the honour of sing- ing it for the first time in Warrington, be only wished he had that privieege that afternoon), the people were moved to self-sacrifice as one of our great politicians said, as if by one mighty impulse, by that patriotism which Redyard Kipling had so beautifully and loyally voiced in his latest patriotic poem "The absent-minded beggar." Many opinions had ben expressed about that wonderful poem but it was not his intention to dwell upon any •f them. He felt that was not the time for criticism. The late Matthew Arnold used to say that the future of poetry was immense. He ventured to think teat that was per- fectly true; but when our present poet laureate faveured us with his strange poem on the Jam PllOll Raid, the question was asked by many who loved poetry what was to be the poetre 02 the future? Some said it would he lernethiog quite new and strange and that it would shake off all the laws of melody and know and care nothing for that beauty of form that characterized the late poet laureate, Lord Tennyson. Others said its only merit would be that of the sensuous beauty that characterized the poetry in illustrated papers. Others ventured to say that the psetry of the future would empty itself of all moral meaning and have no message for the soul if men. He knew not how much truth there was in these prophetic statements but he felt confident that the people of Ele- land would always love poetry that voiced the thoughts and feelings of the people, strongly, masterly, and sym- pethetioally. He felt confident that the people ol England would always love poetry that struck the cord of the national sentiment as Rudyard Kipling had done. (Loud applause.) He did not say that the patriotic views of Rudyard Kipling in language and style marked the high-water mark of English literature, far from that. He did not say that it really r .presented in dignity the heart-felt meanings of Christian and loyal eeeple. He cone'lere ' that in this patriotic poem Riglyard Kiplire had struck the notes to which the public heart had responded and whatever some people might say as to the vulgarity which they had detected in some of Rudyard Kipline's poems, they acknowledged him today to be the poet laureate of the Angl,-Saxon race. (Loud applause.) He might never he the poet laureate by the grace of her Majesty the Queen, but if Rudyard Kipling did not hold the title he held the posi- tion. Rudyard Ripling.to his mind had impressed the popu- lar mind and the fired popular imagination, and had interpreted the patriotic aspirations and the popular aspirations of the nation as no other writer or poet of the day had attempted to dn. He ventured to say that Reelysrd Kipling, notwithstanding some of the critic- isms he had read lately, was a great poet for he dealt with those passions which moulded human nature, and he did this in a style which was incompar- ably better than that of any other living poet. Rudyard Kipling was a great poet, and great in the breadth of his human sympathy and had expressed the patrio- tic feelings and thoughts of the mest memorable and critical days of this dying century. and when men called over the roll of poets who had rend gsd imperishable ser- vice to their 1 elem. and had given splendour to the name of Great Britain, Rudyard lifeline would hold a most honourable place among the greatest of English 'posts. (Apple:lse.) In the words of another poet " His fame in every clime on earth shall live where Saxon's Neeech shall sway." (Applause.) He felt that afternoon the true inadequacy of words to express their indebtedness to Rudyard Kipling, who ro his mind had idealized and transfigured Imperialism. They delighted to think of themselves at this critical moment, as belonging to a land on which everyone who set he foot be he Boer or Chinaman was free—(loud applause)—and they reflected with great satisfaction that they w^re members of an empire on e hich the sun never set, and to his mind it was well that the great Imperial idea should absorb the lesser national idea. It enlarged their views widened their sympathy and made them more Cathelic in their inter- ests. Be rejoiced to think that Imperialism as voiced by Rudyard Kipling had won the hold of the national mind which he hoped wneld never be relaxed. To con- solidate the Empire and to defend it against all fees must no longer be the cry of any political party but the set determination of a united people. (Loud applause.) Rudyard Kipling had - hewn them that the essence of . true Imp-rie..em . .9 not lordship but service. He had to his mind divested the Imperial function of the false tinsel and glitter with which it was too often associated and had revealed it as a duty to be performed, under the cnostraint of conscience and of God. Ho rejeiced I over the manifestation of the spirit of revived i interest in regard to this great onestien of Imperialism</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>STOP A COUGH IN ONE NIGHT.</head>
        <p>STOP A COUGH IN ONE NIGHT.</p>
        <p>Take VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE. It stops an orlinary cough in one night, and cures chronic coughs, bronchitis, asthma, catarrsh, influnnza, and whooping cough rapidly. Its vast superiority over the different emulsions and ordinary cough mixtures cannot be esti- mated. It has saved thousands of lives after they have been turned out of hospitals. It is a new scientific remedy end,rveei by medical men. Ask for 'STENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE, and see that you get it, price la. 112 d. and 2s. 9J. per bottle (copyright). Sold by fol- lowing chemists :—ASHTON, KNOWLES, Warrington ; WtLLBRIDGE, St. Flelens ; PEASE, Earlestown and N,wton ; FOGGITT, Widnes; WESTON, Runcorn ; and all chemists.</p>
        <p>ANNEXING THE TRANSVAAL—What will bs the fate of the Transvaal after the war it is difficult to fore- see, but one thing may be considered certain, and that is that every one who can will take care to annex a case of that delicious liqueur, GRANT'S MORELLA CHERRY BRANDY, which is so great a favourite at home and abroad. Enquire for it of all Wine Merchants and Hotels, and don't 13, put off with inferior makes ; also GRANT'S CHERRY WHISKY —Advt.</p>
        <p>ELASTIC AND JEAN hURGICAL BELTS.— highly moons mended by the Medical Pro:ession for completely support ing, strengthening, and improving the figure. Prices from Bs6d. to 435. each. Address the Ladies' Attendant. Artifis!al Legs, Hands, and Arms.—WHITE AND WRIGHT, Surgical Instrument and Truss Makers. 93, Renshaw-street (topot Bold-street), Liverpool.—Advt. (2).</p>
