      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>Evening Meeting.</head>
        <p>The Evening Meeting.</p>
        <p>In the evening, Parr Hall was crowded in every part, whenlpictures of a sacred character were thrown on the !creel:4ond selections given by the Borough Band. Coun- cillor Arthur Bentett presided. As in the afternoon,the proceedings commenced with the siuging of " God sayer the Queen" with much enthusiasm'. During the course of an interesting addrees, The CHAIRMAN said that he had always felt that the P.S.A. did an extremely good work,but he did not think that the Society was ever eneeged in a better work than It was that night. The object of the meeting was one that must appeal to every patrictic citizen and every Christian, for support. One of the fundamental principles of religion was to visit the wives and families in eilliction and that seemed to clearly express the objects of the fund which they were doing their best to support. Councillor Bennett went on to speak of the heroism manifested by the British troops in recent battle, which he said was be yond all praise. They were apt at times to speak some. what slightingly of " Tommy " but he thought that in times of war their opinion was ent;rely changed. In recent engagements they had seen how he had confined his attentions not only to the British wounded but to the wounded of the enemy. Es thought they ought to see that those left ,behind were pzoperly cared tor and did not suffer by the gallant services their husband were rendering to the country. Throughout the land the appeals had been nobly responded to, and be did not think their geed old town bad been any exceptien to the rule. (Applause.) A collection was tnen taken and realind £l3 154. making a total for the day of £29 lb. During the evening Mies Davies rendered two solos "Rock of Ages," and "Star ef Bethlehem " in a manner which gained the hearty plaudits of the audience. The latter song was illustrated by the lantern elides. Messrs. Gough and Hessen were heartily encored for their rendi tion of the duet " Excelsior," which was illustrated with linaelight views. Mr. Frank Hickman ably discharged the duties: of accompanist. The crowd applauded voci- ftrously when pictures of British soldiers and flags were thrower he screen, the picture of a grcup of Gordon Highlanders being receive with tremendous applause. The proceedinge were brought:te a close by the Borough Band playing the National Anthem. Great credit is due to Mr. Henry Taylor, P.C.M., who manipulated the lantern, and was responsible for the whole of tho pro g-aname in the evening.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>Earlestown Albions v. Newton Corinthians.</p>
        <p>Played on the Mesnes on Saturday in a thick fog. The Albions were the first to become dangerous and five minutes from the start, Herring scored their first goal. The Corinthians never once troubled Chew, during the whole of the first half and just before the interval Bramna added a second goal fcr the Albions. This represented the score at halftime. The second half was simply a repetition of the first, a goal being scored for the Albions by F Kerry and P Kerry and Gee scored the Coriathians only goal. Final, Albions 4 ; Corinthians 1 Rochdale v. Earlestown. Lancashire League.</p>
        <p>At Rochdale. The start was detayed nearly half an hour through five of the visitors missing their train. The visitors refused to turn out when ordered by the referee and consequently will be reported to the League. Earlestown were repeatedly prominent on the left wing, but the Rochdale backs were very safe, and the visitors' forwards did not get within shooting distance. Settle got away smartly, but Bunce, pulled him up nicely. Rochdale were baying distinctly the best of the game, but they shot wildly in close quarters. The 11)chdale players were now having much the best of the game, and from a melee in goal Chew gave a corner,which was cleared. The visit'as tried bard to get through just before the interval, but their movements lacked finish. Half-time : No score. On resuming Rochda:e had the best of the play, but their shooting was very erratic when in close quarters. The only goal of the match was scored by Rochdale soon after the resump- tion. Earlastown tried hard to equalise, and two or three times came very racar doing so, and they were un- lucky to lose the services of Parr in the last quarter of an hour. The score about reflects the state of the game. The defence on each side was better than the attack, the Rochdale: forwards showing to poor advantage with the exception cf Wilson and Boardman on the let,: wing. The backs of both lines were admirable. Earlestown were best represented by Parr at centre-half and the backs. Neither goalkeeper had muen to do ; indeed Welstenholme did not touch the ball until the second half was we!! advanced.</p>
      </div>
