      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>MR. C. H. PIGOT'S CONCERT.</head>
        <p>MR. C. H. PIGOT'S CONCERT.</p>
        <p>That the concert organised by Mr. Pigot and given on Friday evening in Parr Hall ,did not receive a larger measure of public support is to lie regretted from all points of view. But most of all is the fact to be deplored because of its direct discourage- ment of any further enterprise in this direction at a time too when it is believed that the musical education of our townspeople is being rapidly advanced. The meagre attendance especially in the better class seats detracted con- siderably from the cheerful aspect of the con- cert but it did not prevent those who were fortun- ate enough to be present from enjoying a high- class musical treat. The artistes were those com- prised in what is known as the Miss Alice Esty concert-party and included several eminent vocal- ists and instrumentalists. Miss Alice Eety herself sang some songs which, as in "The King of Thule," ballad and "The Jewel Song," (Faust) gave ample scope for the display of her highly cultured and remarkably flexible voice, and Madame Mc Kenzie likewise charmed her rapt audience with the rich resonance of her contrqlto singing in the operatic excerpt " 0 don fatale" (Verdi). The songs by Mr. Alec Marsh were in every sense adequate and musicianly interpreta- tions, t` The Sailor's Grave " being especially effec- tive when reedered in Mr. Marsh's robust voice and style. In response to a well- merited encore Mr. Marsh sang " Who carries the gun ?" with such appropriate animation as elicited a further outburst of applause. Apologies were made for the temporary indisposi- tion of Mr Flerb-qt Grover, a tenor, the fine quali- ties of whose voice and conscientiousness of style were nevertheless apparent in Mendelssohn's little song "On wings of love" which under the circumstauces, he craved permission to substitute in the first part of the programme to which alone the exigencies of time and space per- mitted us to listen. The instrumental portion of the programme was exeeptionally interesting and enjoyable. As violin and piano soloists respectively both Miss Ethel Marsh and Miss Janotha demon- strated the acquisition of a perfect tseitsique along with rare ability in interpreting the misst subtle nuance; in expression and phrasing. For the violin solo" Zigeunerweaien " (Stirasate)• Miss Mesh re- ceived a most emphatic recall reeponding with a fine example ef double stopping. Miss Janotha early established herself In the audi- ence's favour by her fine interpretation of the recently discovered Chopin noctnrn and the subse- quent Tarantella by the eaine cemposer, and being also encored Mise Janotha olaeed with a brilliant and delicete touch a weil-koven "Metier" of Mendelssohn's: There were some concerted items for the vocalists which served to give variety to the programme and a grand duet between Miss Marsh and Miss Janotha, the Hungarian Dances Nos. 2 and 5 (Brahms-Joachim): Mr. H. Van Vleet pieno.ior, e accompaniment to the provided an ideal singing and to the violin solos.</p>
      </div>
