      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <p>7Letters to the 011itar.</p>
        <p>~..N.,....W.-..,-.V.......-....</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS. Authors of letters intended for publication must in all cases send their names and addresses to the Editor. ic.atters intend,ld for publication must be written on one</p>
        <p>side only of the paper. Correspondents are strongly advised to write briefly. A short letter has much greater chance of insertion than a long one.</p>
        <p>Raffling versus Gambling at Runcorn. Sir,—l was greatly surprised that our adored Vicar did not answer in your last issue, the scurrilous letter which appeared above the nom-fie-plume of Nemesis in your paper of the previous week. I cannot understand why the Vicar should have thought it necessary to justify his action in allowing raffling at his bazaar) as we members of his flock know whatever he does is right and beyond question. I am sure that no Churchman would dare to write such a letter ; only a bigoted heretic could write so disrespect- fully of our Spiritual Father. It is like painting the lily white to attempt to add to the Vicar's arguments but I would like to give an illustration of an illegal act in one instance, being quite right when done for our Mother Church. For instance suppose that a navvy on the Ship Canal sold some bread and cheese worth a farthing for three pence, and gave a glass of beer with it; he would be taken before the magis- trates for shebeening and fined heavily or perhaps sent to gaol without the option of a fine. But at our bazaar it would have been quite right seeing that it was for the benefit of our Church, to sell a farthing cake for three- pence and give a glass of beer with it. It is very liktly that some ignorant fellow, who has no had a religious training in our schools and not been taught to respect his betters, will be objecting to this next. CHURCHMAN.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>Justice's 'Justice."</head>
        <p>Justice's 'Justice."</p>
        <p>Sir,—The case reported in your last issue, that of Mr. Ward, who applied to the magistrates for an exemption from vaccination for his two children, has destroyed another of my fond delusions. Hitherto I had held the pleasing persuasion that our magistrates were appointed to administer the law as it stood—to give to every man his ciue. The law says if a person will appear before the magistrates and say that he has a conscientious objection to vaccination, an exemption from it shall be granted to him. This Mr. War I did and—the magis- trates evaded their manifest duty by vacating the Bench. And now the applicant having been denied his legal rights by our local "shallows," mu3t be at the expense and inconvenience of re-appearing and seeking from a juster bench the power to keep his children free from, filthy operation. Mr. Ward probably thinks with xne that vaccination has never been tr.ed on its merits, and that it is often productive of other if not greater evils. Our worthy Mayor tells us what he has seen as deputy chairman of tbe Health Committee. But has he ever, seen vaccination tried as a preventative of small-pox—by itself without the assistance of its healing sisters sanitation and isolation ? I think these twin prophylactics are the true preventatives, and that the filthy disease-giving rite might be left out to the benefit of the community. Anyhow—the rieht of any one to exemption from it upon proper application must be rtaintained.—Sincerely yours.</p>
        <p>"OBJECTOR."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>War.</head>
        <p>War.</p>
        <p>Sir,—At the present time, when two professedly Christian nations are engaged in a deadly conflict, and teachers of religion and their di3ciplos ate denouncing one of the belligerents and approving and praying for the other, a few words about war and the people who wage it may not be inopportune. The first question then which presents itself to one's mind is, what is War ? Divested of all the disguises and false glory with which its advocates and defenders seek to justify its horrors it stands forth in all its hideous nakedness as the wholesale and pitiless destroyer of human and animal life. And, as might be expected, its dread operations are carried on under circumstances so atrocious and appalling as to be beyond the power of adequate description. Indeed so ruthless and remorse- less is the spirit which inspires it, and so terrible are its ravages that one is almost driven to the conclusion that war is more the work of demons than of ordinary human beings. Earthquakes, storms, and pestilence and famine have done much to deciminate and impoverish mankind, but the losses produced by these visitations of nature fall infinitely short of the sacrifice of life and pro- perty which men have brought upon themselves on the battlefields of the world. And the only redeeming feature, the one ray of mercy, that glimmers on the scene of carnage is found in the cccduct of the brave men and women whe, at the risk of their own lives, do all(in their power to comfort and relieve the wounded and dying combatants. And who, it may be asked, are the people that delight in war? As my answee to this question involves three different classes of persons I shall, for the sake of elear- rens deal with each case separately. In the first place, savages make war with kindred savages with terrible ferccity, and fill the dark places of the earth with teloed and deaelation. But while we justly deplore this great wickedness, we must remember • that there are extenuating circumstances in the case of the heathen which cannot be pleaded by tbe warriors of enliehtened and civilized lands. The heathen have never been taught any- thing about the sacredness of human life ; their views of the rights and responsibilities of property are of the crudest description,they know net God, and have never heard of the Saviour of the world. In carrying on their wars of extermination they only follow the instincts of a fallen and depraved nature, common to all men, but in their case unrelieved by the light which civilized na- tions enjoy. Then we have multitudes of men in all the enlightened countries of the world who possess the great advantages of education and are, nevertheless, de- lighters in war. Such men regard the destruction of the heathen by fire and sword as a desirable process for promoting the interests of trade and commerce. Ex- pansion of empire and the power and wealth which generally accompany it are,in the eyes of these cultured and ambitions men, more than sufficient to atone for all the killing and burning inflicted upon primitive races to promote these objects. I must, however, do them the justice to admit that but few of them make any pre- tence to be religious after the popular manner- Gold and net God is the object of their worship. And now I come to the last of the three classes of men who take up the sword. This class is perhaps the largest and most important of all and is, I deeply regret t) say, made up entirely of religious people. What- ever the motives and objects may be which prompt them to kill their fellow men, desolate their homes, and confiscate their lands they eannot be ex- cused on the ground of ignorance of a more exeellent way as the heathen can. So high indeed is the value which they attach to life that when a man slays his neighbour he is promptly strangled without mercy. They make laws upon laws for the protection of pro- perty of all kinds. As professed students of the Divine Oracles they must know that the God to whom they bow is a Being of infinite mercy, and that He, and He alone, has a just right to the disposal of his sentient creatures. And, moreover, their attendance at worship and hymn-singing to the Prince of Peace must surely indicate a knowledge,more or less, of the gospel of peace and goodwill to men. How is it then that people so en- lightened and so religious can be found willing and ready at all times to imbue their hands, directly or by proxy, in human blood for sordid motives and what the world calls glory ? The answer is that men who cherish and encourage the war spirit must be strangers to the heavenly principle or Love to God and man which is tbe essence of Christianity. A careful consideration of the life and teaching of Jesus compels the impartial mind to regard war, under any circumstances whatever, as the antithesis of Chris- tianity, and a stupendous crime against God and man. It may be waged under the auspices of the modern go pel for the pastors seem agreed that the slaughter- ing of men, and the horrors attending it, are just and righteous when employed in expanding and developing empire and opaline up new fields for missionary enter- prise. But how different is the Gospel of Christ ! That heavenly message teaches men to love one another, to love their enemies, to bless them that curse them, to do good to teem that hate them, and pray for them which despitefully use and persecute them. Where the tender and beautiful spirit of the Saviour which underlies all these counsels lives and moves in the hearts of men war is impossible. But, apart from the gospel altogether, there is a con- sideration which should induce the professors of Chris- tianity to give more thought than they seem to do to the effects of war. They believe, I suppose, in the popular teechine that after death men must survive in an fecrnal state of happiness or misery. If this doctrine be true how heartless, how cruel it must be for men, but especially for Christians, to send thousands of their fellow creeturcs in an unprepared condition to nass from the seen to the unseen world through the crime and horrors of war !—Yours truly, November 18, 1899</p>
        <p>lONS.</p>
        <p>SUPPOSED DEATH FROM POISONING AT RANDOM</p>
        <p>Mr. Brighouse held an inquest at the Ram's Head Hotel, Ilaydock, near St. Helens, on Tuesday, on the body of Emily Holmes, aged three and a half years, the daughter of William Holmes, of 10, New Boston cottages, Haydock, who died 011 &amp;tturday morning.—Mrs. Holmes said that on Tuesday, the 14th, she found that the child had been sucking the brimstone off some matches. She gave it an emetic, but it grew worse, and she sent for Dr. Latham, of Earlectown. The doctor gave evidence of a post-mortem examination that he had hold, which showed that death was due to inflammation of the brain. The matches had certainly nothing to do with the child's death.—A. verdict of "Death from natural causes" was returned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>EAR LESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EAR LESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</p>
        <p>POLICE CASES.</p>
        <p>Before Mr. Borron, at Earlestown on Tuesday morning, John Toole and Thomas Cosgrove, were summoned for being drunk in Earl estown on Mon- day night. Toole was fined 2i 61 and costs, and Cosgrove 5s and costs. P.S.A.</p>
        <p>At the usual meeting of the Earlestown and Dis- trict P.S.A. Society held on Sunday afternoon. Mr. Heaton presided. There was a large attendance and an excellent address was given by Mr. Taylor of Manchester. Recitations were given by Miss. Warburton, and vocal selections by Mr. S. Dring A special collection was made in aid of the City Mission, Manchester. EA.RLESTOWN THURSDAY FOOTBALL CLUB.</p>
        <p>Ori-Te—d-n-e-saay night the annual ball of the above Football Club was held in the Town Hall. There were about 50 couples present, this number of course being augninted as the night wore on. There was an excellent list of dances, which were gone through to the music of Mr Sudworth's string band. The M.C.'s were Messrs H Walton, 11 H Duke H Matthews,and H Hockf...nhull. The full arrange- ments for the carrying on of the ball were left in the hands of a committee consisting of Messrs El E Pritchard (secretary). Singleton, Duke, Rice, Wal- ton, W Barrow, J Barrow, Stringer, Mottishead, Matthews, Toft and Hockenhull. The principal item on the programme was tbe "Shadow Dancing' the different shades for which were exhibited by Mr Lawson.—The room was splendidly decorated by Mr Arthur Clarke, who deserves every credit for the able way in which he has carried out his part of the work. Mr George Edwardes, of the Ship Inn, catered. SUICIDE AT NRWTON. _ .. _</p>
        <p>Early on Tuesday morning a sad case of suicide was reported to the Newton Police. Joseph Brad- dock, of Park-road, Newton, 67 years of age, em- ployed as a groom by Mr. Pardey, steward to Lord Newton, was found dead with a shot wound in his neck, in the drying ground at the residence of Mr. Pardey, High-street, Newton, at 7-40 a.m. on Tues- day. The deceased left home a little before 6 a.m. on the 21st inst to go to his work. A postman named John HoNtield met him at 5-55 a.m. Brad- dock appears to have gone into the saddle room lit the gas and taken one of Mr Pardey's double barrelled breech loading gun's and gone into the drying ground, 100 yards distant; placed the gun with the stock on the ground and pushed the trigger with a stick. The contents of the right hand barrel had made a hole in the front of his neck and smashed the bones in his head. The left hand barrel appeared to have been unloaded. About 7-40 a.m, Thomas Weaver one of Mr. Pardey's gardeners had occasion to go into the grounds and found the deceased lying dead. Information was at once given to P.S. Banks and he visited the scene, and found deceased lying on his back the gun with the stick still in the guard of the trigger,lying close by. Dr. Watkins of Newton was soon on the spot and pronounced life extinct. The body was at once removed home. EARLESTOWN CO-OPERATIVE SOCIETY. The quarterly meeting of the members of this society was held in the Co-operative Hall, Earles- town, on Monday evening, when there was a large attendance. Mr. D. Forrester presided. The report and statement of accounts showed that the cash re- ceipts were £9,087 17s Bd, being an increase over last quarter of £557 2s 61, and compared with the corresponding period last year,aa increase of £2,709 Os 6il. The net profits realized amounted to £1,308 4s 6d, to whieh it was proposed to add £34 2i 6d, from dividend reserve fund, and pay a dividend of 3s in the Xto members. Profits were rather less than usual, owing to the cost of a few chief com- modities being exceptionally high, while the com- mittee did not feel disposed to raise their price pro- portionately. During the quarter 63 new members had been admitted and 16 withdrawn, leaving 1492 on the register, 1097 of whom had brought in checks. Two matters involving some expense had been executed,viz., internal alterations to facilitate access to furnishing department, and the introduc- tion of a new heating system. The minutes of the last quarterly meeting having been read, the accounts were adopted. Messrs P Parr and J G Mullins were re-elected to serve on the committee. Mr. D. Forrester was nominated for re-election as president. The question of introducing the business of selling coal in bags was brought forward, and sanction was given the committee to bring about the innovation. The usual grant of £5 to the Tech- nical Instruction Committee was authorised. On an application by Mrs. Collingwood on behalf of the Vulcan Women's Sewing meeting, it was decided to make a grant of £5 worth of materialsto enable that lady's class to make up clothing for wounded soldiers in South Africa, and it was further decided to allow her to buy any further quantity of material at cost price from the Society. Mr. G. Gooding was appointed a delegate to the half yearly meet- ing of the Co-operative Wholesale Society. This was all the business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>A LLEGED ASSAULT ON THE POLICE.</p>
        <p>Five Irish labourers named James, Thomas and Richard Brandon, Michael Morran, and Daniel Carney. at Haydock, were charged at Warring- ton Occasional Court on Monday with assaulting and doing bodily harm to a number of police-con- stables at Haydock on Saturday night. It appears that James and Richard Brandon, brothers commenced fighting near the Owl's Nest Hotel on Saturday night. When they were arrested, a gang of ten or twelve other Irishmen set upon the officers, and Constable Dagger was kicked on the head, and carried unconscious int, the hotel. Police reinforcements arrived, and the men were re-arrested, but on the way to the police station a farther attack was made on the police. and Constable Addison was also rendered un- conscious. The two constables are under the police surgeon, and in a rather serious condition, while the other members of the force were also badly assaulted. A civilian named John Eden, who went to the assistance of the police, had the muscle of his right arm burst by a most savage kick. The five men were remanded to the Newton-le-Willows Petty Sessions on the 30:h inst.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>DEATH OF MR. R.</head>
        <p>THE DEATH OF MR. R.</p>
        <p>BRI ERLEY.</p>
        <p>The Funeral.</p>
        <p>the interment of the remains of the late Mr. R. Brierley, whose death we announced last week, took place en Saturday a fternoon,in the family grave,Newton Churchyard. The funeral cortege left the deceased's late residence about 2.30, in a dense fog, and besides the re- latives, who occupied the two carriages, there were pre- sent the members of the Newton Urban District Council, the clerk and officials of the Council, and about 40 brethren of the M kkerfleld Lodge of Freemasons of which the deceased was P.P.G.S.W , and had passed through the chair. Other friends and acquaintances who were present, were Messrs B B Glover, James Glover, AR 'Nhyte, R Barton, El J Longten, J H Bod- more, J Croachlev, Ste. The carriers were Messrs. Gregory, Gorse, Hindley, Bush, Swann and Anderson. On arrival at the church the choir sang " Blest are the departed," as the body was carried in. The service in the church was performed by the Rev. James Ryder assisted by the Rev. E. Whittenbury-Kaye, reetor of Nawchurch. During the service the hymn "My God my Father" was 511112', and as the boc.y was taken out, Dr. Middleton, organist, played the Funeral March. Outside the service was completed by the Revs J Ryder EW W Kaye and Tft Matthe vs. Aslthe Freemasons filed past the grave, each dropped upon the coffin a sprig of acacia. The coffin was of plain oak, with brass mountings, and bore a plate with the inscription, " Richard Brierley, born March 1838, died November 1899." There were a number of beautiful floral wreaths sent by Mr W 13. Brierley, Cousins of Liverpoel, Mr and Mrs A Knowles, Mrs Wilberforce, Granddaughter Phyllis Mrs Brierley, Mr and Mrs E A Whyte,Mrs Smith (Brook- aid.). the Clerk and Officials of the Newt on District Council, Miss E Brierley, Dr and Miss Smith (Blackrod), his loving chadren,lir and Mrs W Hartley (York) Miss S Pennington, and Mr B B Glover. At a special meeting of the Urban District Council held on Monday, a vote of condolence with Mrs Brierley in her bereavement, was passed on the motion of Mr. C. B. F. Borron, seconded by Mr. Collingwood.</p>
        <p>Warrington Markets. At the Warrington Corn Market on Wednesday wheat fetched 104 and 4s. per bushel, oats, 2s 44. Flour, best seconds, per 2401 h., 19s Bran, is id per 201 b. At the Provision Market prices were as follows :—Fresh butter, is 3d and is 44 per lb; cheese, 61 to 8d per lb. ; fresh eggs, 6 for is. fowls, 4s 6d to 5s 61 per couple ; English beef, 4d to 94 ; mutton 61 to 104; veal,7d to 101 ; pork, 61 to 84.</p>
        <p>ENVELOPES</p>
        <p>2s. PER 1,000.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, NOVEMBER 24. 1899.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, NOVEMBER 24. 1899.</p>
        <p>received their full war outfit including the khaki suit. The men have taken quite naturally to their duties, and a very few days will suffice to fit them for active ser- vice. It has indeed been quite remarkable how promptly the men have fallen back into military ways, and it has placed beyond doubt the superiority of a reserve-fed battalion over one composed of young soldiers. During the week the time has been occupied at Preston in weeding the ranks of the regi- ment, and then the battalion, about 1,100 strong, and composed entirely of Reservists and trained men, will be ready for embarkation, which will take place at Liverpool on Monday next. The following is the list of the officers :—South Lanca- shire Regiment: Lieutenant Colonel W McCarthy O'Leary (in command), Majors R H Hall (2nd in com- mand), and T Lamb. Captains : S Upperton, H W P Waters, E T James, CGFG Birch, and B R Goren. Lieutenants: E F Oakeley, G Canyon, F M Raphael, C R S Woods, S H Skinner, GDHE wart, A Northern, and R C Trousdale. Second Lieutenants: LI R Kane, 11 K Woods, C H Marsh ; Lieutenant A H Bailey (adjutant), and Hon. Lieutenant J Huxford (quartermaster). Liverpool Regiment : Lieutenant-Colonel S H Harrison (in command) ; Majors P Schelleter (2nd in command), H J W Guise and A W H Tripp: Captains L St. C Nicholson, H Swann, T G P Glynn, H S Woolwich, F C Ommaney and E F Knight; Lieutenants C L Armitage, F W Sheppard, B. St. J W Hastings, R P Fox, A C Lewin, and H C Potter ; Second Lieutenants, R W Mac- fie, A L Hartford, 13 C Owen, G M Dean, and F E Fene- ran ; Lieutenant C H Harrington (adjutant), and Hon. Lieutenant E H Bell (Quartermaster). The Record of the South Lancashires.</p>
        <p>The Ist South Lancashire Regiment,or more familiarly known as the "Fighting Fortieth"has as the name implies a remarkable record, though it is now some time since it has seen active service. The hat time it was seriously engaged was in the Mutiny. The second battalion now stationed in India has had actual experience in S)uth Africa, having been stationed in Zululand and Natal. during the years 1884, 1885, and 1886. At that time it was quartered, among other places, at Eshowe, Help- makaar, Maritzburg, and other pla'.:es which have re- cently been prominently mentioned. A few of the older non-commissioned officers of the first battalion now go- ing out were in the ranks of the 2nd Battalion as young soldiers at the period when it was in Natal. It need hardly be said that the impending departure for the seat of the war of the South Lancashire men has creased considerable public interest, and without doubt the em- barkation will be the occasion of a greit demonstration at Liverpool. TOBACCO FOR "TOMMY." Generous Gift by Councillor Crosfield.</p>
        <p>The British troops at the front will have good reason to remember the kindly thoughtfulness of Councillor John Crosfield, of Walton Lea, who has fora arded through Mr. Harry Toft, tobacconist, of Sankey-street. one ton of the finest smoking tobacco as a Christmas gift. The tobacco has been made up in three ounce plugs and at the present moment is on its way to the seat of War. It has been addressed to "The General com- manding the British forces, Capetown" who will no doubt see that this acceptable gilt will reach the hands of "Tommy Atkins." Mr. Crosfield was very particular that the tobacco should be of the best. Several samples were submitted to him and the sample which he has selected is a specially good one.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>RESERVISTS.</head>
        <p>THE RESERVISTS.</p>
        <p>DEPARTURE FROM WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Memorable Scenes.</p>
        <p>Never, perhaps, in the history of Warrington, has such an outburst of patriotic enthusiasm been witnessed as was the case on Monday evening when reservists of the South Lancashire Regiment and the Kings Liverpool Regiment departed for Preston and Enniskillin respectively. There were some en- thusiastic scenes on Sunday and also on Tuesday but Monday's demonstration stands unapproached as a display of patriotic fervour by the inhabitants of Warrington. Many of the works were closed for the afternoon, a quite unnecessary procedure see- ing that the reservists did not depart until 6.28 p.m. and 10.14 p.m. The result was that crowds assembled early in the afternoon and about five o'clock some of the streets were almost impassible. Tired of waiting for the procession a large number resorted to public-houses, and having imbibed pretty freely they added considerably to the boisterousness of the crowd when the first batch of reservists did make its appearance. At 5.30 the South Lancashires numbering about 130 in charge of Major Ashworth left Orford Barracks for Bank Quay Station. Two'bands were in attendance,viz : the band of the depot, and the band of the local corps of Volunteers. The route taken was a'ong Orford-lane, Tanner's-lane, Crosfield-street, and Parker-street. The thoroughfares were crowded with sightseers and every coign of vantage from which a sight of the departing reservists could be obtained was used. It was necessary for a body of police to make a way for the soldiers through the surging mass of people, but the press of the crowd was sa great that immediately afterwards the ranks were closed and the reservists reached the railway station with difficulty. A large crowd commenced to march in front waving their hats and handker- chiefs, and alternating the singing of patriotic songs with vociferous cheers for the heroes at the front in South Africa. The reservists as they forced their way through the crowd appreciated the hearti- ness of the send-off and good humouredly put up with the little inconveniences it entailed. In front of the station a scene of the wildest enthusi- asm was witnessed. For some time the police under the direction of Chief Inspector Phillips and Inspector Row- bottom WPrfl able to keep a pissav,e clear in the centre and the crowd maintained a good humoured attitude, cheering, and singing patriotic songs. As the crush in- creased the people got beyond the control of the small body of police who faced the difficulties heroically, and two or three ugly rushes resulted in the closing of the passage which had been reserved for the soldiers. In vain did the police try to force the crowd back. The door at the entrance to the station was locked, as a rush was made to gain an entry. For a few minutes a scene of great confusion took place Men were shouting, women screaming, and with the crush becoming every nt)ment stronger it was feared that the door would be forced in. At that moment cams the sound of the band. The crowd immediately turned and broke out into the wildest cheering. It was still, however, impossible to obtain a passage for the entry of the soldiers, and they had to literally fight their way through the crowd. This, however, did not in the least discencert them and they reached the platform in the best of spirits:</p>
        <p>There was another enthusiastic scene inside the rail- way station. The passages leading to the platforms were lined with sightseers and the men as they passed were cheered again and again. The departure platform was packed and amongst those present were the Mayor (Alderman Roberts), members of the Corporation and officials. The men haying entrained The MAYOR addressed them as follows :—Mee of the South Lancashire Regiment, I have been asked to say a few words to you on behalf of the people of Warrington, of affectionate farewell and good cheer. (Cheers.) We I are all of us proud of the S outh Lancashire Regiment, and we are equally proud of the men whom Warrington I is sending away to-day. (Cheers.) We hope that when you get out to South Africa your services will not be required. but we know perfectly well that if required you will do your duty to your Queen and country. (Cheers.) Believe me, we will take good care while you are away of the women and children you leave behind yon and of the old fathers and mothers who have been dependent upon you for their livelihood. (Cheers.) We shall look with a great deal of anxious ineorest fnr the reports which the newspapers will give us of what you are doing and how you are getting along, but all the time we shall pray that that Power which controls alike the destinies of nations and of men will throw His pro- tecting arm around you and bring you safely back again to the country you love and the friends you now leave sorrowing behind. I can only say in conclusion that we wish you all good cheer and God bless everyone of you. (Loud cheers.) There were many touching scenes of farewell, but affecting as they were, even to the onlooker who could only faintly realise their deep sadness, the men bore up most bravely. They were accommodated in five special carriages which were attached to a train leaving the I station at half•past six. On the departure of the train the bands played "Auld fang gene," and the spectators broke out into vociferous cheers, which were heartily returned until the train was out of sight. A tooled of praise is due to Mr. Cooke, the stationmaster, for the excellent arrangements which prevailed at the railway station, not only for the deepatch of the troops but also for the accommodation of friends and relatives of the men on the platform in order that they might be the last to touch the tpierls of those whe were now, leaving them for their perilous duty in South Africa. Mt. Cooke was ably seconded in his efforts by the police, and it is a matter for congratulation that no accident of a serious kind occurred.</p>
        <p>Later in the evening there were similar demonstra- tions when a hundred Reservists of the King's Liverpool Regiment under the command of Lieutenant V.T. Bailey left Bank Quay Station for Enniskillin via Holyhead and Greenore. The Reservists left the Barracks at half-past nine and entrained at a quarter past ten to the strains of the depot band. The march to the station was witnessed by thousands of people, who expressed their good wishes for the men by hearty cheering. In front of the station there was again a big crowd ,and the scene on the arrival of the Reservists was of a deeply enthusiastic character. The men looked exceptionally fit and well. Colour-S3rgeant Chesters was the recipient of a most enthusiastic ovation.</p>
        <p>Alderman WRIGHT, :Iv Deputy-Mayor, who was accompanied by the Town Clerk and several members of the Corporation, delivered a short speech to the men as they were getting into the train. He said : You men of the Liverpool Regiment, in the name of War- rington I want to say to you, as Deputy• Mayor, in all sincerity" God speed you in the work you have on hand." My hearty desire is that every one of you may return to your homes safe and well. I am perfectly cer- tain that you will go out and do your duty as M9II and as soldiers I pray that God in His good will will bring you all back safely to your homes and familes. Patrio- tic songs were sung and as the train moved out those on the platform heartily cheered the men, whilst the band played" Auld Lang Sync."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>Departure of Reservists on Sunday.</head>
        <p>Departure of Reservists on Sunday.</p>
        <p>Up to Sunday last there had been despatched from Orford Barracks, 48 on Wednesday and 53 on Friday. A third detachment of the South Lancashire Reservists, numbering 71. was despatch° 1 on Sunday, and most enthusiastic scenes were witnessed. The townspeople turned out in hundreds to give the men a hearty send- off, and the dsmondration was unmistakeably a most worthy one. The men fell in at the Barracks at two in the afternoon. Amoxest them were Sergeants Price. Seriven and Wynne, The band of the devot played them to Bank Quay Statien. Colonel Spratt was in command, snJ was accompanied by Major Smythies (Lidjetant), and Captain Green, the latter of whom pro- ceeded wi h the men to Preston. The route was Bat- tersby-lane, Mers,y-street, Bold-etreet, and Wilson Patten-stret-t. The streets were lined with spectators who enthusiastically cheered the men. At the station they were met by the Volunteer Band and several mem- bers of the St. John Ambulance Association. A vast crowd of people had assembled, and the scene was a memorabe one. Cheer after cheer was raised, a special ovation being accorded to Sergeant Price. There was another demonstration at the railway station, where many touching farewells were witnessed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>Tuesday's Departures.</head>
        <p>Tuesday's Departures.</p>
        <p>A batch of 215 reservists of the South Lancashire Regiment left Bank Quay Statim for Preston at 5-30 on Tuesday afternoon. At about four o'clock the men formed up on the Barracks Square and after baying beeh in- spected by Colonel Spratt, left shortly after five, and proceeded by way of Orford-lane, Tanners-lane, Gros- field-street and Parker-street to the Station, headed by the band which played patriotic airs. Major Smythies accompanied the men to the S'Ation, and Nlijor Adams and Lieutenant Green proceeded with them ta Preston. A large crowd assembled at the station, and as the train steamed out the band played "Auld Lang Sync" and hearty cheers were raised. Ninety-el.ht reservists of the King's Liverpool Regi- ment left Warrington for Enniskillin via Holyhead at 10 15 on Tuesday evening. They left the Barracks at 9.30. under the cninrnand of Lietrelant Denham,headed by the band,which played "Soldiers of the Queen "and other selections. A large crowd preceded the men,sing- ing and waving a Union Jack. Colour-Sergeant Jones, captain of the Warrington Association Football Club accompanied the draft to Ireland,and he was seizad by a number of friends and carried shoulder high from one end of the platform to the other. As the train steamed out deafening cheers were raised and the band struck up" Auld Lang Sync."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>Men at Preston.</head>
        <p>The Men at Preston.</p>
        <p>Enthusiastic as has been the send off ,i from Warring- to n the men have received hearty receptions at Preston. They have been met at tho station by the band of the regiment and played into barracks. Here tlyT have</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>MR- GUY REYNOLDS AT ELAN DSLAAGTE.</head>
        <p>MR- GUY REYNOLDS AT ELAN DSLAAGTE.</p>
        <p>"A Ghastly Sight."</p>
        <p>The following are extracts from letter which has hen received by Mrs. Sylvanus Reynolds, Whatcrofc 1.1311; near Northwich, from her son, Mr. Guy Reynolds, of tne sth Dragoon Guards,now at Ladysmith :—"Lady- smith Camp, October 23rd, 1899. A few lines to tell you I'm all right up to the present. I arrived on Satur- day morning, just in time to get on to a horse and go to the battle of Elandslaagte. I went with a patrol and the Boers fired on us at about 200 yards, but only one horse was wounded. They don't shoot as well as people make out. It was a splendid fight. Our infantry did splen- didly, and the Boers were only turned out of their posi- tion as it was getting dark. We pursued the enemy for miles—ourselves and the sth Lancers, and we killed a goo I number and took a lot of prisoners—it was most exciting. After the battle I went over the field to assist the wounded, who had to stay out the whole night in the rain and cold. It was a ghastly eight, numbers of dead and wounded, mostly dordon Elighlanchrs and Manchester Regiment. We bad no sleep that night, and in the Morning our squadron went over the battle field and into the Boer laager,where we saw some horrible sights. Our shrapnel shells had made a fearful mess of the place. We got back to Lady- smith on Sunday afternoon, having been in the saddle over 38 hours. I wasn't nearly as beat as I expected to be, but I was stiff and very glad to go to sleep. They are fighting hard to-Jay all round here, but our horses have been worked very hard and are not going out. We are now fighting the Free State Boers and they are in great numbers. We shall have lots of hard fights before we get.into the Transvaal—especially near Laing's Nek and Majuba. lam very well indeed. The voyage did me worlds of good, and I like this work. I don't care about barracks work, but this is splendid. We shall get a medal and a clasp ; I should think for certain for Elandslaagte. Now no more,we may go out at any mo- ment.—Ever your loving son, GIIIr.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>HELP FOR THOSE LEFT BEHIND.</head>
        <p>HELP FOR THOSE LEFT BEHIND.</p>
        <p>The people of Warrington are nobly responding to the Mayor's appeal for help for the wives and families of the reservists and the other laudable objects in con- nection with the Transvaal Campaign Relief Fund. A sunstantial addition to the funds will be urgently needed before many weeks are over, in as much as the recall to the colours of the reservists in connection with the South Lancashire Regiment will add at least two hundred cases on the Relief Committee's books requiring weekly assistance. This will soon exhaust the funds now in hand as the amount paid weekly will probably average about £lOO. Some more generous offers have been made during the past week. The most interesting is that of Messrs Crosfield and Sons Limited who have decided to give the whole of their profits made by the sale or Perfection Soap in Warring- ton and district up to January Ist next. At the present time this means a minimum contribution of at least £8 te £lO per week, which will amount in the time named to at least £lOO. Every purchaser of Perfection Soap will thus become a direct contributor to the Mayor's Fund,and if all Warringtonians will use Perfection Soap and no other, the sum may be easily made into £2O a week, or an aggregate of at last £2OO. The workmen, at an enthusiastic and well-attended meeting held on Tuesday morning, decided to agree to the following sums being deducted from their wages in aid of the Mayor's local relief tuad :—Those receiving one pound a week one penny, those receiving 255. 21., and those receiving more than that 3.1. per week. This arrangement will continue in force as long as the wir lasts. The first suggestion was a penny a week up to 255., and 2d. over that amount ; but itiwas overruled by the majority of the men iit tavour of the first-named generous scheme, which reflects the greatest possible credit on the men. The five Reservists who were employed at Messrs. Gros- field's have each been presented with £2 95.. the result of a hurried collection. At moet of the works in t3vvia colleetions are now being made on behalf of the Fund. C elections have been made at several local churches. At Parr Hall on Sunday, the P.S.A. Society in connectisci with the Young Men's Christian Association realised £29 lls, at their two services. At Holy Trinity Church the collec- tions were &amp;motel to the same object and £25 was raised. The Rev. E. C. E. Carleton preached at the morning service a timely discourse and said that the cause for which their offerings were asked was one that pleaded for itself. It was the cause of the families of those brave men who at the call of duty had gone forth to fight the battles of their country, in the issue of which conflict they were all es deeply concerned, as was every subject of this great Ernpire.--It St. John's Pres- byterian Ceurch £2 103 was raised for a similar purpcee. £7 83 lid has been forwarded to Mr. Greenall from St. James' Chinch to be devoted to the Soldiers' and Sailors' Families Association.</p>
        <p>Workingmen's Mission, Bank Quay.—Presenta- tion to Mr. Thomas Ashley. _ . _ _</p>
        <p>On Monday evening the workers and -friends of the above mission assembled in the schoolroom for the purpose of making a presentation to Mr. Thomas Ashley, who has been connected with the mission ever since its commencement nearly 30 years ago. After tea had been partaken of the presentation (which tcok the form of a beautiful silver tea and coffee service, suitably engraved) was made by Mr. Thomas Dale on behalf of the committee and friends of the mission. Mr. IDale referred to the position that Mr. Ashley had filled for $o many years (namely, that of secretary of the chapel) and the long and valued services he had rendered ever since the mission was established. Mr. Ashley suitably responded and Mr. N. Hough expressed the wish that the recipient would long be spared to labour in their midst. Mr G Date, Mr Rl; Tanner, Mr Henry Edwards, and Mr Burndred each made a few remarks, and during the evening Mr J Hannan and Mr S Hassan rendered songs, the accompanist being Mrs Joseph Monks (jun.) Tee service was supplied by Mr. S. Fernley, Bridge-street, and will be on view in that tradesman's window until Tues- day nPxr.</p>
