      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>BAPTIST AUTUMNAL MEETINGS.</head>
        <p>BAPTIST AUTUMNAL MEETINGS.</p>
        <p>The Rev. C. F. Aked on the Renewal of Protestantism. The autumnal meetings in connection with the Wes- tern Distriet of the Lancashire and Cheshire Baptist Association were held in Golborne-street Baptist Chapel Warrington,on Tuesday. At four o'clock public worship was held and was attended by a large congregation. The Rev. C. F. Aked of Liverpool, was the preacher. The service was conducted by the Rev. T. Adamson, of Liverpool,and commenced with the singing of the hymn " 0 love Divine, how sweet thou art." The Rev. C. F. Aked preached a most interesting sermon, basing his remarks on the words "Nevertheless, I have somewhat against thee, because that bast lost thy first love " Revelations ii., 4. He observed that they never knew the highest thines of which their nature was capable until they loved. They could call ta mind those happy moments of supreme bliss that they had experie enced when they bad been borne along on the wings of affection, and carried along by the great stream of emotion, that had been without them as well as within, in those heroic hours when they had been swept on by that great swelling tide of love. The very grass had taken on a richer green,the sky a deeper blue, and the sunbeams had appeared more sweet, men nobler and women diviner. They did not grow to their full height unless they loved. Would. not Carlisle, great as he was, have been a greater man if only he had been a little tenderer and had possessed more human love. One of the greatest living preachers, John Clifford, would be a greater man if he had always taken council of his heart. They never had the glorious visions of God unless they loved. If they loved well enough the poor victim, bruised, broken, and bleeding, the poor wreck, the broken hearted widow and orphans if they loved well enough the victim of their hellish Jingoism, they must bate that Jingoism as they ought to hate it. If they loved well enough the poor victim of.the liquor traffic, if they loved well enough the man who had been degraded and debauched and the woman who hal been be-fouled and be-deviled by di ink and lust, they would hate well enough the system that made them what they were. It was because they did not love well enough that they did not hate well enough. Their heroic hatred would not last unless it was fed by love. Their object should be to arm the commonwealth against the enemies of mankind. In every true reformer's heart must be what was in the heart of God—a hatred of the wrong, but a love of tne wrougeneer. They must love as well as hate, or their hatred would not. last, anti if it did it would only accomplish one-half its object, and that not the larger half of the two. " Whosoever Beetle in love liveth in God." Public Meeting. In the evening a public meeting was held. There was a large attendance presided over by Councillor J Sbaw Green. There were also present on the platform, the Revs J T Schofield, A Vaughan,Thomas, H 0 Mackey, C F Aked and 0 R Gibbon. The CHAIRMAN who was warmly received said it was somewhat in the nature of a surprise to find himself on that platform, though perhaps he ought not to be sur- prised, for men accustomed to public life should be pre- pared for anything, and he did not therefore think any apology was necessary for his taking the chair. He had known their church for many years, even so long ago as when they worshipped in the iron church in Rylands- street, and be could not when asked by their pastor re- fuse to be present on this unusual occasion. It was well that all who were in any way seeking to direct the course of public life in a town should be rightly instructed. He did not like to stand in any uncertain position, and therefore should there be there any who did not know him he took the earliest opportunity of saying he was not .5 member of that section of God's great army, but he was serving under the same Master in that enthusias- tie section known.by the name of Methodist. (ipplause.) He came across many people who wondered why they adopted so many names and worked in an many different ways, and who were disposed to speak ungenerously of them because they had so many sections, but those of them who were so wearing Christ's colours realized that their real differences were the result of their freedom. Their Master was one, Christ Jesus, and they were not fanatics shouting for the victory of a sect or a principle but for the victory of the Cross. As members of the great Free Church they deplored the unhappy differences that divided Christianity, but he coeld not refrain from saying that their differences were as nothing compared with those that tried the tempers of the two other great spiritual organizations in this country. Nay, he ventured to say that so far as he could read their aifferencea were helping each of them under the federa- tion head, whilst the differences of their Anglican and Romish brethren seemed not unlikely to read them. "We may be sure," said Sir John Lubbock, "that whatever may be right about religion, to quarrel over it must be wrong." Whatever others might think of them, let them not forget that they were the champions, how- ever lowly or humble it may be, of the righteousness upon which the greatness of the nation depended. He did not know the line to be taken by those gentlemen who were to address thoni,but it roost be for the etrengthening of the bands of those engaged in Christian work,atad the town must benefit by the stimulus of such a meeting as ' I that. As they must have men to do the citizen's work, let them see to it that their first or prominent men were the best men. He had been long enough in public life to realise that a great deal might be done by having a ' sound, healthy, religious tone amongst the ruling autho- rity, and with all the members of the Free Church he rejoiced that a prominent member of their great body had by the unanimous voice of the Council been elected to its highest office. (Applause.) tie knew moat towns were much like their own, but he sometimes thought they were in greater need than most others of a true genuine revival of spiritual life. He was most painfully impressed with tne necessity for something being done as he saw the great crowds that thronged the streets on Mayor's Sunday both morning and evening. It was clear that hundreds upon hundreds of people were not even dreaming of visiting a place of worship—indeed, at night, as they passed some quarters he looked at the crowd and asked himself whether these people had any conception of the day itself or of the purpose and object of the procession. Their clothing, their general appearanee and conversation, betokened complete absence of all such knowledge. Surely seme- thing ought to be done. As a Council they were doing what they could to improve the sanitary,the healthy, and cleanly conditions under which their people lived and they had a right to ask the churches to go out of their way and carry tlmaGospel to them. In saying this he did not in the least wish to reflect upon the work that was being done, but he wanted to see something more. He was pleased to read that the Free Churches had decided to open the new century by a great simultaneous series of revival meetings throughout the whole king- dom, and be trusted as Warrington's needs were so great it would be amongst the foremost in that enter- prise. He sometimes feared that constant intercourse with the sad and wretched took much of the livliness and heartiness out of them but there was a responsibility upon them all. Let them look at the duty before them, and though ambitious schemes might fill their souls, let them overlook that which lay at their feet. (applause.) The Rev. A. V. THOMAS then delivered an address on " Spiritual Diagnosis." The Rev. H. V. MACKEY followed with an address upon" Our Witness for to-day." The Rev. C. F. AILED spoke on " The Renewal of Pro- testantism." He said that before they thought about renewal of Protestantism they must confess the cer- tainty with which they had witnessed a most astound- ing, incredible and unlooked for revival of Romanism. Thirty years ago one of the most philosophical historians of the nineteenth century (Mr. Froude), said of the men who were then Pusey ites, Ritualists, or High Church- men "1 wish that they would tell us plainly, do they or do they not regard the Protestant Reformation as a mistake ?" They had told them plainly since then. They said that the Reformation at its best was a blunder, and at its worst was a criTe. The Protestant Reformers they told them, nere at their very best soft-hearted and soft-headed fellows, and at their worst were Charlatans and knaves. All round they had seen a revival of Romanism. They had seen not merely medieval christianity but paganism brought back again. Against that they were bound to assert their Protestant principles and protestant faith. They had got to refurbish their arms and go back to the old polemic against Rome. Rome was a spiritual tyranny. She claimed to exercise lordship over the con- science of man. She weakened the sense of personal res- ponsibility and reduced the power of personal initiative. Not only was Rome a spiritual tyrant, but she allied herself with political deepotism and fostered ignorance and superstition and she herself shrunk from the light. She weakened and made against morality. She was reviving and fighting against a State established and State endowed Protestant Church. Sacerdotalism and Sacramentarianiem were even encroaching on their free churches. He did not believe any Baptist had been taken in by it. Certainly no educated or consistent Baptist could be taken in by it. They could only fight Rome successfully by spirituality. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>FRODSHAM.</head>
        <p>FRODSHAM.</p>
        <p>THL TRANSVAAL FIIND.—A most succersful meeting was held in the Town Hall, Frodsham, on Wednesday night, presided over by Mr. C. E. Linaker (chairman of the Frodsham Parish Council.) Be was supported on the platform by the Revs. H B Blogg (vicar), T H Bailey (Wesleyan minister), Messrs. J G Kydd, C Reynolds, W E Shore, J Moseley Williams, T Filey, T Baxter, C.C., S. Davies, and others. Loyal and patriotic spi echos were delivered, and a resolution form- ing a local Committee for the purpose of raising a fund in connection with the Duchess of Westminster's County Fund was unanimously passed. The meeting was most enthusiastic and inspiring, and argued well for the part Fredsham will takt, in ail raising of the fund.</p>
