      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>EGG FARMING.</head>
        <p>EGG FARMING.</p>
        <p>Lectures at Newton.</p>
        <p>Under the auspices of t'le Viaduct Institute Technical Instruction Committe,e,the first of a series of lectures en "En Farming" was delivered in the Town Hall,Newton on Tuesday evening by Mr. Edward Brown, F.L.S., of Reading. Mr. Arthur Smith presided, and there was only a sparse attendance. The Chairman said he should be sorry to think that the sparse attendance was an indication of the interest taken in the subject. Personally he was glad to be able to be present.</p>
        <p>The Lecturer said the lectures which would be given I this week and next had been designed because he was in the neighbourhood two years ago and gave a course of lectures. He did not intend to go over tbe same ground but rather to deal with the subject of egg-farming or egg producing with regard to profit. When he was in that hall two years ago he had to tell them that the value ot the eggs imported into this country was upwards of £4,000,000 sterling and since that time there had been an increase in value of £200,000. If they took those figures in detail to ::ry and learn what they really meant they might have some startling facts. In 1861 the import of eggs averaged 7 per head of the population. Last year, the imports were equal to 43 per bead, so that they would see in the course of 37 years the imports had advanced from 7 to 43. The quantity of eggs imported last year was something like 1750 millions, and eggs were brought into this country from abroad, and if they assumed that the productions of Great Britain and Ire- land were something like the same in quantity,it meant that the people of Great Britain consumed last year something like 3,500 millions of eggs, and year by year we have evidence on all sides that the consumption of these articles of food was growing very rapidly. One ef the things he had been giving attention to of late years ha d been the quality of eggs coming to us from abroad for he thought the more we could teach our people as to the value of the eggs they would see it would be b etter to get a fresh egg than those foreign eggs low in price. He found the imports of the bee class of foreign eggs were, falling off, whereas the im- ports of the cheaper eggs were employed for manufac- turing purposes. He believed the main reason for the falling off in French eggs was due to the efforts of our own people in trying to multiply the producti on to meet the increased demand. We had only Ito travel up and down the country to find that the Ipeople were awakening to the importance of this subject land see the signs on all sides that poultry keeping was being recognised to a greater extent than it has been before. It was surprising the number of people who seemed to imagine that any body could make a success of poultry rearing. The first direction in which it was possible to develops what might be termed egg-farming ' was by keeping peultry on farme,and he must deal with that because it was essential they should get the abso- lute truths in their minds. In different parts of the country they found there were farmers who had largely developed this as a business. He believed there was great capability for increase. There had already been considerable advance in this direction in recent years._ In 1885, the Board of Agriculture included in its returns, statistics of the number of fowls kept on farms. Nature in all her operations was always striving to maintain a balance. There must be this balance, otherwise they had various troubles. It was probably the fact that all the diseases which came to human beings were due to the upsetting of the balance of nature. A farmer would tell them that if he grew corn or any crop upon his land, year by year. and did not take the necessary steps to put back into the land the material which the crop drew out, he exhausted the soil, and so the soil became poorer. He had to keep in view also that the enrichment of land by manure would have the tendency to encourage parasite growths, such as weeds. The more intense our cultivation on terms, the mere necessary was it to increase our poultry as they would maintain the balance of cultivation. In connection with farms we should the more recognise the great value of poultry, and in adding poultry to farming we are obtaining another crop without disturbing anything else. In every district where agriculture was carried on it was a fact which could not be too strongly referred to. They would have to give attention in the future to the question of making the poultry fit in with the needs of the crops, as well as make the crops fit in with the needs of the poultry: If they continued to put manure on the land and neglected to crop it; every farmer would tell them that that land would become a manure heap in course of time, and he mentioned an instance of poultry straying on to a field eown with corn, and of no differ- ence being made in the crop. He then proceeded to show by means of limelight views different kinds of poultry houses, and runs. There should be greater economy in feeding. He believed the amount wasted in feeling our poultry in this ',country was something enormous, and many people would find that their fowls would lay very much better if they paid attention to that. (Applause). Mr. J. Clark proposed and Mr. Webster seconded a vote of thanks to the lecturer.</p>
        <p>Mr. Brown, in acknowledgment,said if anybody oared to piovide him with a fowl, he ehluld be prepared to give a demonstration as to the best method of dressing and trussing it for roasting or boiling. He concluded by moving a vote of thanks to the chnirman, which being accorded closed the proceedings.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-24_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>"Breeds for Egg Production."</head>
        <p>"Breeds for Egg Production."</p>
        <p>The second lecture was given in the Co-lperative Hall, Earlestown, on Wednesday evening. Dr. Patterson pre- sided.</p>
        <p>The Lecturer said the subject for that night was the breeds of poultry for egg production. He wanted to say something as to the different races of poultry that were the most likely to give the best results for egg farming. All breeds of fowls or hens lay eggs, tub there was a wonderful difference in the quantity of eggs pro.</p>
