        <p>LATEST NE Fighting in the Soudan. Khalifa's Forces Routed. 400 Killed. Colonel Wingate and the Egyptian force attacked Ahmed Fedil and 2,500 followers at Abu Adil. The enemy were routed, 400 being killed. Illness of Lord Salisbury. The " Central News " regrets to learn that Lord Salisbury is confined to his room by an attack of influ- enza, and is unable to transact any business. 40 THE WAR. LATEST TELEGRAMS. A Sortie from Kimberley. A message from De Aar, dated Wednesday, says : A despatch-rider has just come through from Kimberley with the news that all is well there. On the 16th inst., Colonel Kekewich ordered a sortie upon the advanced works of the besieging Boers, and it was carried out in gallant fashion. The Boers were driven out of their positions, and left ninet3en dead on the field. Our losses were trifling. Boer Advance to Pieter., maritzburg. Fighting at Mooi River. A " Daily Telegraph" message from Pietermaritzburg, dated Wednesday, 5.15 p.m. ,says :—Our advance picket was fired on by Boers two miles west of Mooi river last night, three or four men being wounded. One of the number, Private Davey, 2nd Devon, was shot through the right lung, and expired shortly after being brought into hospital this morning. The Boers from the kopje on the north sent several shells in the direction of the Mooi river camp, and a dozen fell close to our men, without, however, doing any damage. Five hundred men of the Imperial Light Infantry arrived at Pietermaritzburg from Durban this afternoen and encamped at Fort Napier. Alarming News from Mafeking. Garrison In Want of Good Water. Mafeking, Monday (handed in at the Magalapye telegraph station by spacial runner on November 20).— The Boars coetinued to shell Mafeking, but their fire has considerably slackened during the last few days. Their riflemen are inactive. We are suffering from want of pure water, and fever is rife in the town. The wounded are doing well. General Gatacre's Column. Intended Immediate Advance. QI7EENSTOWN, WEDNESDAY. For strategical reasons, and also for the purpose of re- assuring the British population, General Gatacre has de- cided on a partial for ward movement. Yesterday he reconnoitred the whole line as far as Molteno,and visited the outposts of the regulars, irregulars, and police. More troops are arriving to-night.</p>
        <p>duced. Those breeds in which the table qualities had been specially developed, did not lay large numbers of eggs. It was ',essentially important in making the choice, that we should study the different breeds, see what were their characteristics and as far as possible mike choicA in accordance therewith. By means of lime light views; he described the different breeds of poultry, giving a definition of egg qaalities, which classes of fowls are the best layers, and nonsitting varieties, general purpose varieties, crossing and crosses, soil in relation to breed, and climatic in- fluences. A vote of thanks was accorded the lecturer, on the motion of Mr. W. Davies, seconded by Mr. J. Younger, and a similar compliment was paid the chairman.</p>
